[{"category":"section","location":"api/Propagator/#Propagator","page":"Propagator","text":"","title":"Propagator"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Propagator/#Integrator-schemes","page":"Propagator","text":"The integrating-factor scheme supertype and its concretes — the default third-order low-storage RK3, then the cheaper, imaginary-axis-unstable second-order Heun.","title":"Integrator schemes"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Propagator/#Step-scratch","page":"Propagator","text":"The preallocated per-step working set the integrator writes into — built per concrete scheme, sized so a step allocates no field-sized memory.","title":"Step scratch"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Propagator/#Time-stepping","page":"Propagator","text":"One in-place step of the coupled velocity and passive scalar together through the shared RK stages.","title":"Time stepping"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Propagator/#Timestep-ceiling","page":"Propagator","text":"A scheme's advective-CFL ceiling.","title":"Timestep ceiling"},{"category":"module","location":"api/Propagator/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Propagator","page":"Propagator","text":"Propagator\n\nThe interchangeable time-marching schemes, concrete AbstractIntegrator subtypes; a run chooses one as the integrator of its Simulation. All are of the integrating-factor family: the stiff diffusive part is advanced exactly and the nonlinear term explicitly by a Runge–Kutta stage combination.\n\nIFLowStorageRK3 (the default), IFRK2 — the schemes (RK3 low-storage; IFRK2/Heun a cheaper two-stage alternative, imaginary-axis unstable).\nIntegratorBuffers, allocate_buffers — the preallocated per-step scratch.\nstep! — advance the coupled velocity and passive scalar one timestep.\nrecommended_cfl — a scheme's advective-CFL ceiling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Propagator"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Propagator/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Propagator.AbstractIntegrator","page":"Propagator","text":"AbstractIntegrator\n\nSupertype of the time-marching schemes. A scheme implements step!(integrator, state, generator, domain, buffers, derived, dt), advancing the state's velocity hatboldsymbolu and scalar hatvarphi (both in spectral representation) by one timestep in place, and may specialize recommended_cfl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Propagator.AbstractIntegrator"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Propagator/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Propagator.IFLowStorageRK3","page":"Propagator","text":"IFLowStorageRK3()\n\nIntegrating-factor third-order low-storage Runge–Kutta (Williamson 1980, 2N-storage). With the diffusive integrating factor mathcalE_D(tau) built by the generator's velocity_factor! / scalar_factor! over a substep interval tau (D = nu for the velocity, varkappa = numathrmSc for the scalar) and widehatboldsymbolN each field's right-hand side, the step carries a state hat q and a single accumulator boldsymbolh through three stages i = 123,\n\nbeginaligned\nboldsymbolh leftarrow A_i boldsymbolh + Delta t widehatboldsymbolN(hat q) \nhat q leftarrow hat q + B_i boldsymbolh \nhat q leftarrow mathcalE_D(d_iDelta t) hat q \nboldsymbolh leftarrow mathcalE_D(d_iDelta t) boldsymbolh\nendaligned\n\nwith A = (0 -59 -153128), B = (13 1516 815), and node increments d = (13 512 14) (abscissae c = (0 13 34)). The velocity and scalar are advanced in lockstep (the same ABd), each with its own diffusivity, and any post-step forcing is applied after the third stage. Three nonlinear evaluations per step, two field registers per field — the default workhorse: third-order and stable on the imaginary axis (limit sqrt3), unlike IFRK2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Propagator.IFLowStorageRK3"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Propagator/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Propagator.IFRK2","page":"Propagator","text":"IFRK2()\n\nIntegrating-factor second-order Runge–Kutta (Heun). With the velocity and scalar integrating factors mathcalE_nu and mathcalE_varkappa (the generator's velocity_factor! / scalar_factor! at D = nu and D = varkappa = numathrmSc over the step Delta t) and widehatboldsymbolN each field's right-hand side, one step of a field hat q with integrating factor mathcalE is\n\nbeginaligned\nhat q^* = mathcalE (hat q_n + Delta t widehatboldsymbolN_1) \nhat q_n+1 = mathcalE hat q_n + tfracDelta t2(mathcalE widehatboldsymbolN_1 + widehatboldsymbolN_2)\nendaligned\n\nwith widehatboldsymbolN_1 at hat q_n and widehatboldsymbolN_2 at the predictor hat q^*; velocity and scalar use mathcalE_nu and mathcalE_varkappa respectively, then the forcings are applied. Two nonlinear evaluations per step.\n\nnote: Note\nEvery second-order two-stage explicit Runge–Kutta has stability function R(z) = 1 + z + z^22, for which lvert R(mathrmiy)rvert^2 = 1 + y^44  1: the scheme is unstable on the imaginary axis, so it is stable only where the viscous damping of the integrating factor dominates. It is adequate for well-resolved, modest-Reynolds-number runs but is not the default — prefer IFLowStorageRK3 for high Reynolds number or marginal resolution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Propagator.IFRK2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Propagator/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Propagator.IntegratorBuffers","page":"Propagator","text":"IntegratorBuffers\n\nThe preallocated per-step scratch the integrator writes into, sized so a step allocates no field-sized memory. Built per scheme by allocate_buffers(integrator, domain; scalar), which allocates exactly that scheme's working set: a register another scheme would use is left nothing, never allocated.\n\nFields\n\nShared — every scheme allocates these:\n\nvorticity_physical, velocity_star — the substage velocity/vorticity registers in physical representation (the start-of-step fields come from the run's DerivedFields charts).\nstage1 — the first Runge–Kutta stage register (the accumulator for IFLowStorageRK3, the first stage for IFRK2).\nintegrating_factor — the register the exact linear subflow is written into each substep.\n\nScheme-specific — allocated only for the scheme that uses it, else nothing:\n\nnonlinear — the assembled right-hand side (IFLowStorageRK3 only).\nstage2, velocity_star_spectral — the second stage register and the substage velocity predictor hatboldsymbolu^* in spectral representation (IFRK2 only).\n\nNested:\n\nscalar — a ScalarBuffers for the passive scalar varphi when scalar = true, else nothing; when nothing, the run evolves no scalar and none of its field-sized buffers are allocated.\n\nThe right-hand-side assembly scratch (the quadratic products, the phase-shift buffers, the forcing-rate arrays) lives with the generator, on the ResolvedGenerator — the buffers here are only the scheme's own stage registers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Propagator.IntegratorBuffers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Propagator/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Propagator.allocate_buffers","page":"Propagator","text":"allocate_buffers(integrator, domain; scalar = false) -> IntegratorBuffers\n\nAllocate the IntegratorBuffers integrator needs, dispatching on the concrete scheme so it allocates exactly its working set — never a register another scheme would use. IFLowStorageRK3 allocates stage1 and nonlinear; IFRK2 allocates stage1, stage2, and velocity_star_spectral. scalar = true adds the nested ScalarBuffers, likewise sized to the scheme. A scheme with no method here is a hard error — there is no over-allocating fallback. The run layer calls this when assembling a Simulation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Propagator.allocate_buffers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Propagator/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Propagator.step!","page":"Propagator","text":"step!(integrator, state, generator, domain, buffers, derived, dt)\n\nAdvance the state (a ResolvedFields holding the velocity hatboldsymbolu and passive scalar hatvarphi in spectral representation) by one timestep dt in place, using the integrator, the reified generator (a ResolvedGenerator — the equations of motion, their conditions, and their assembly scratch), the domain, the scheme's buffers, and the run's observation cache derived (a DerivedFields). Dispatches on the concrete integrator type (see IFRK2, IFLowStorageRK3).\n\nA scheme does only stage arithmetic: it opens the step with the generator's prepare!, evaluates the right-hand sides and exact linear factors through the generator protocol at each stage, and closes with the generator's finalize! — it never assembles the physics itself. The start-of-step velocity and vorticity in physical representation are read through derived's accessors — recomputed automatically when stale, so a hand-run loop needs no manual refill — and the cache's fields epoch is advanced when the step completes, marking every derived chart for recomputation on its next read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Propagator.step!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Propagator/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Propagator.recommended_cfl","page":"Propagator","text":"recommended_cfl(integrator, domain)\n\nA conservative advective-CFL ceiling C for the scheme, for a timestep Delta t = C Delta x  max_ilvert u_irvert.\n\nThe advective eigenvalues of the dealiased system are imaginary, of magnitude up to kappa_maxsuplvertboldsymbolurvert; stability needs this times C to stay within the scheme's imaginary-axis stability limit y_mathrms. The timestep divides by the cheaply-measured maximum component max_ilvert u_irvert (within a factor sqrt3 of suplvertboldsymbolurvert); that gap is absorbed by the conservative factor below, since the eigenvalue bound itself over-estimates the true spectral radius (not every mode carries the peak speed). The base ceiling is calibrated for the 23 rule (kappa_maxDelta x = 2pi3 approx 209): for IFLowStorageRK3 y_mathrms = sqrt3 gives C lesssim 083, taken conservatively as 05; IFRK2 is imaginary-axis unstable (y_mathrms = 0, relying on viscous damping), so a small 025.\n\nIt scales that base by the domain's dealiasing: a strategy that keeps modes to a higher kappa_max (e.g. HouLi, out to the Nyquist wavenumber) takes a proportionally smaller step, C propto 1kappa_max. This is the value Simulation uses; override cfl for a specific run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Propagator.recommended_cfl"},{"category":"section","location":"api/SimulationRun/#SimulationRun","page":"SimulationRun","text":"","title":"SimulationRun"},{"category":"section","location":"api/SimulationRun/#The-simulation","page":"SimulationRun","text":"The assembled, ready-to-run case — the front-door Simulation(; grid_points_per_axis, viscosity, …) and the domain-taking Simulation(domain; …) constructors. Read its measured state by applying the Observables to sim.state — for example kinetic_energy(sim.state.velocity) in a run! callback.","title":"The simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/SimulationRun/#Advancing-the-run","page":"SimulationRun","text":"Advance one step, run to a stopping condition (with an optional per-interval callback), then spin up to statistical stationarity.","title":"Advancing the run"},{"category":"section","location":"api/SimulationRun/#Clock-accessors","page":"SimulationRun","text":"The elapsed simulation time and the step count.","title":"Clock accessors"},{"category":"section","location":"api/SimulationRun/#Flow-parameters-and-spectrum-validation","page":"SimulationRun","text":"Bridge the run's measured state to a Phenomenology FlowParameters, and compare the measured energy spectrum against a model spectrum.","title":"Flow parameters and spectrum validation"},{"category":"module","location":"api/SimulationRun/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun","page":"SimulationRun","text":"SimulationRun\n\nThe run layer:\n\nSimulation — the assembled state of a case.\nadvance!, run!, warmup! — advance one step, advance to a stopping condition (with an optional per-interval callback), and spin up to statistical stationarity.\ncurrent_time, current_step — the elapsed time and step count.\nflow_parameters — the run's measured flow state as a Phenomenology FlowParameters.\nvalidate_spectrum — compare the run's measured energy spectrum against a Phenomenology model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun"},{"category":"type","location":"api/SimulationRun/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun.Simulation","page":"SimulationRun","text":"Simulation(; grid_points_per_axis, k_max_η = 2, kinetic_energy = 1.5,\n             side_length = 2π, element_type = Float64, array_type = Array,\n             dealiasing = TwoThirds(), kwargs...)\nSimulation(domain; k_max_η = 2, kinetic_energy = 1.5, kwargs...)\n\nA ready-to-run case — the FixedDomain, the IntegratorBuffers, the ResolvedGenerator (the equations of motion, carrying the FlowConditions and the assembly scratch), the chosen integrator, the ResolvedFields state (the velocity hatboldsymbolu and passive scalar hatvarphi in spectral representation), the DerivedFields observation cache, any Tracers, and a clock. The first form is the front door: from grid_points_per_axis and side_length it builds every piece with sensible defaults, seeds a divergence-free velocity (and, with scalar = true, a mean-zero scalar varphi), and returns a Simulation at t = 0; the second takes a FixedDomain you built yourself. Advance it with advance! / run! / warmup!; read its measured state by applying the observables to its state fields (e.g. kinetic_energy(sim.state.velocity)) or through the cached physical views (physical_velocity(sim), physical_vorticity(sim)), summarize it with flow_parameters, and track its progress with current_time / current_step.\n\nSetting the Reynolds number\n\nBy default the run is forced and stationary — a BandRescaling holds the total (whole-field) energy at kinetic_energy (i.e. over = :total) — and the viscosity is chosen from the grid rather than supplied:\n\nGrid mode (no viscosity): the constructor picks the highest Reynolds number consistent with the chosen resolution criterion k_max_η. It reads the dissipation the box sustains from the box-normalized dissipation law Li2008 (in Phenomenology.Scalings), varepsilon = (kG)^32L with kinetic_energy the kinetic energy k, side_length the side L, and G the box-normalization group constant (approx 09), then sets nu so that kappa_maxeta equals k_max_η — the resolution criterion, with kappa_max the max_wavenumber (the dealiasing cutoff, not the grid Nyquist). Since kappa_maxeta = fpi etaDelta x with f the retained-Nyquist fraction, k_max_η = 2 gives Delta x approx eta for the default 23 rule (f = 23); a wider-band strategy (larger f) resolves a finer dissipation scale eta on the same grid at the same k_max_η. The kinetic_energy and side_length fix the units; the grid and k_max_η fix mathrmRe_lambda sim N^23. The chosen nu, varepsilon, and mathrmRe_lambda are logged.\nExplicit mode: pass viscosity and it is used directly (k_max_η is ignored) — for a decaying run (velocity_forcing = NoForcing()) or manual control.\n\nKeywords\n\nThe keywords marked (front door only) configure the FixedDomain and are consumed by the first form; the rest are forwarded to the Simulation(domain; …) form.\n\ngrid_points_per_axis — the number of grid points N along each side (front door only); required.\ndomain — a pre-built FixedDomain, the positional argument of the second form (the front door builds one from grid_points_per_axis, side_length, dealiasing, element_type, array_type).\nk_max_η = 2 — the resolution criterion kappa_maxeta used to set nu in grid mode (the keyword spells kappa_max in ASCII); ge 15 is well-resolved, higher trades Reynolds number for margin.\nviscosity — the kinematic viscosity nu (sets mathrmRe = 1nu); omit for grid mode.\nkinetic_energy = 1.5 — the initial, and when forced the held, kinetic energy k; 1.5 gives u = 1.\nside_length = 2π — the box side L (front door only).\nelement_type = Float64, array_type = Array — the real element type and storage backend (front door).\ndealiasing = TwoThirds() — the DealiasingStrategy (23 sharp, HouLi smooth, or PhaseShift); it sets kappa_max and hence the CFL step (front door only).\nschmidt = 1.0 — the Schmidt number mathrmSc (scalar diffusivity numathrmSc).\nvelocity_forcing, scalar_forcing — an AbstractForcing (or a DeferredForcing from an operator-free constructor); velocity_forcing defaults to a BandRescaling holding the total (whole-field) energy at kinetic_energy (over = :total), scalar_forcing to NoForcing(). The velocity and scalar are forced independently.\nscalar = false, scalar_variance = 1.0, peak_wavenumber = 2.0 — whether to evolve a passive scalar (a mean-zero varphi), its initial variance, and the large-scale envelope wavenumber kappa_p of the initial condition (the shell-summed spectrum peaks at kappa_psqrt2).\ninitial_spectrum = nothing — a Phenomenology EnergySpectrum (e.g. VonKarman1948()) to draw the initial velocity from, in place of the default large-scale Gaussian; its shape is evaluated at the run's state (its integral scale set to 1/peak_wavenumber) and normalized to kinetic_energy.\nintegrator = IFLowStorageRK3(), cfl = recommended_cfl(integrator, domain) — the AbstractIntegrator scheme and its advective-CFL ceiling (adapts to dealiasing).\nnumber_of_tracers = 0, tracer_scheme = CatmullRom — the number of Lagrangian Tracers and their interpolation scheme, forwarded to the Tracers constructor.\nrng — the random stream for the initial condition and the tracer seeding.\n\nExamples\n\n# Grid mode — a 128³ forced-stationary run, ν set from the resolution criterion:\nsim = Simulation(grid_points_per_axis = 128)\n\n# Explicit mode — set ν directly, carry a passive scalar, force at constant power:\nsim = Simulation(grid_points_per_axis = 128, viscosity = 5e-3;\n                 scalar = true, velocity_forcing = ConstantPower(target = 0.1))\n\n# Domain-first — build the frame yourself, then hand it to the second form:\ndomain = FixedDomain(128, 2π; dealiasing = HouLi(), element_type = Float64, array_type = Array)\nsim = Simulation(domain; viscosity = 5e-3)\n\nFields\n\ndomain, buffers, generator, integrator — the FixedDomain, IntegratorBuffers, the ResolvedGenerator (whose conditions field is the run's FlowConditions), and the chosen scheme.\nstate — the ResolvedFields (the evolving û, φ̂).\nderived — the DerivedFields observation cache (the stamped physical velocity/vorticity charts).\ntracers — the Tracers being advected along with the fields, or nothing.\ncfl, clock — the advective-CFL coefficient, and the time/step counter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun.Simulation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/SimulationRun/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun.advance!","page":"SimulationRun","text":"advance!(sim; dt = nothing)\n\nAdvance sim by one timestep in place and return it. With dt = nothing the step size is the advective-CFL value for the current velocity; pass dt to force a fixed step. Advects the tracers, if any, by Heun-in-time on the start- and end-of-step velocities, and updates the clock.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun.advance!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/SimulationRun/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun.run!","page":"SimulationRun","text":"run!(sim; turnovers | time | steps, every = nothing)\nrun!(sim; ...) do sim … end\n\nAdvance sim until exactly one stopping condition is met — a number of eddy-turnover times (turnovers, in units of kvarepsilon), a span of simulation time, or a number of steps — and return it. A callback (the do block, or a first positional function) fires with the current Simulation after every step, or once per every units of simulation time if every is given — the place to stream statistics along the run.\n\nExamples\n\nrun!(sim; turnovers = 10)                        # advance ten eddy-turnover times\n\nhistory = Float64[]                              # sample the kinetic energy every 0.1 time units\nrun!(sim; time = 5.0, every = 0.1) do s\n    push!(history, kinetic_energy(s.state.velocity))\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun.run!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/SimulationRun/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun.warmup!","page":"SimulationRun","text":"warmup!(sim; gate_scalar = true, gate_skewness = true, min_turnovers = 5,\n        window_turnovers = 4, tol = 0.05, skew_floor = 0.1, max_turnovers = 60)\nwarmup!(sim; turnovers | time | steps)\n\nSpin sim up to a statistically stationary state before measurement, and return it. By default it detects stationarity: it advances until the running dissipation varepsilon(t) stops drifting — a two-half-window mean test over the trailing window_turnovers eddy-turnover times, relative drift below tol, not tested before min_turnovers (past the start-up overshoot) — and gives up at max_turnovers with a warning. varepsilon (the small scales, which settle last) is read rather than the kinetic energy, which a constant-energy forcing clamps flat and a fixed-injection forcing makes ring.\n\nTwo stricter gates are on by default. With gate_skewness, the velocity-derivative skewness (derivative_skewness) must also have plateaued and reached lvert Srvert ge textttskew_floor — the decisive \"real turbulence yet\" signal, since a random-phase field starts at S approx 0 and only a developed cascade reaches S approx -05. With gate_scalar, when the scalar is forced, the scalar variance langlevarphi^2rangle must be flat too (the scalar reaches stationarity after the velocity); an unforced scalar has no stationary state and is not gated.\n\nPassing an explicit turnovers, time, or steps instead runs a fixed budget (no detection) — the simple spin-up when the transient length is known.\n\nExamples\n\nwarmup!(sim)                          # detect stationarity (ε- and skewness-plateau gates)\nwarmup!(sim; gate_skewness = false)   # relax the skewness gate (a shorter, looser spin-up)\nwarmup!(sim; turnovers = 20)          # or a fixed eddy-turnover budget, no detection\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun.warmup!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/SimulationRun/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun.current_time","page":"SimulationRun","text":"current_time(sim)\n\nThe elapsed simulation time of sim.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun.current_time"},{"category":"function","location":"api/SimulationRun/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun.current_step","page":"SimulationRun","text":"current_step(sim)\n\nThe number of steps sim has taken.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun.current_step"},{"category":"function","location":"api/SimulationRun/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun.flow_parameters","page":"SimulationRun","text":"flow_parameters(sim)\n\nThe measured flow state of sim as a FlowParameters — the bridge that lets any Phenomenology reference law be evaluated at the run's measured state. The viscosity nu and the Schmidt number are read from the generator's conditions; the dissipation varepsilon, the r.m.s. velocity u = sqrt2k3 (from the kinetic energy k), the longitudinal integral scale L_11, and — when the run carries a scalar — the scalar dissipation varepsilon_varphi are measured from the current state (the scalar dissipation is left NaN only for a scalar-free run, where the scalar reference laws do not apply).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun.flow_parameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/SimulationRun/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun.validate_spectrum","page":"SimulationRun","text":"validate_spectrum(sim, model)\n\nCompare the measured energy spectrum of sim against a Phenomenology model spectrum model (an EnergySpectrum, e.g. Pope2000()), evaluated at the run's flow_parameters. The measured energy_spectrum is a per-shell energy (k = sum_kappa E(kappa)) while the model is a spectral density (k = int_0^infty E(kappa)mathrm dkappa), so the density is multiplied by the shell width Deltakappa = 2piL before comparison — making the two agree at any box side L (not only the default L = 2pi, where Deltakappa = 1). Returns a named tuple (; wavenumbers, measured, predicted, relative_error): the resolved shell wavenumbers, the measured and predicted per-shell E(kappa), and the relative L^2 discrepancy over the resolved (non-empty) shells.\n\nnote: Note\nA developed, well-resolved run agrees with a good model across the inertial range; at low Reynolds number or coarse resolution the inertial range is narrow, so a large discrepancy is expected and reflects the run, not the model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun.validate_spectrum"},{"category":"section","location":"phenomenology/#Phenomenology-—-reference-turbulence-laws","page":"Overview","text":"Phenomenology collects the established turbulence phenomenology — model spectra, structure-function laws, and dimensionless scaling constants — as evaluatable reference predictions. It is the predicted counterpart to the measured observables: the DNS measures, the laws predict, and the validation layer sets them side by side. No law is assumed by the solver; each is a testable prediction, and our own fits sit beside the literature on equal footing.\n\nOne pattern. Each law family is an abstract type; each parametrization is a concrete subtype carrying its fitted coefficients and its provenance, evaluated against a FlowParameters bundle. Choosing a law is choosing the subtype — the published fit is the default, and any coefficient can be overridden to re-fit:\n\nusing GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology         # FlowParameters, spectrum, …\nusing GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra  # the model presets (Pope2000, …)\n\nfp = FlowParameters(viscosity = 1e-3, dissipation = 0.1, rms_velocity = 1.0, integral_scale = 1.0)\nE  = spectrum(Pope2000(), 10.0, fp)          # Pope (2000) model spectrum at κ = 10\n\nNaming — a three-level handle. A model's name disambiguates on three axes: the quantity is the submodule (Spectra versus StructureFunctions), the paper is the author-year type (Pope2000), and a same-author-year-quantity collision takes a trailing letter (Pope2014a). So the existing StructureFunctions.Kolmogorov1941 (the 2/3 and 4/5 laws) and a spectrum counterpart — a Spectra.Kolmogorov1941, were one added — would be distinct, separated by the submodule alone.\n\nOpt-in namespace. using GBGIsotropicTurbulence brings the DNS API only; the laws are reached through the submodule namespace, e.g. using GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra (this module is designed to be extractable into a standalone package).","title":"Phenomenology — reference turbulence laws"},{"category":"section","location":"phenomenology/#Pages","page":"Overview","text":"Reference laws — the law interface and the FlowParameters bundle with its derived scales.\nSpectra — model energy spectra E(kappa), composed of an inertial core and swappable roll-off factors.\nStructureFunctions — the inertial-range structure-function laws D_Ldots(r).\nScalings — the dimensionless constants and their Reynolds-number trends.","title":"Pages"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#Derived-Fields","page":"Derived fields","text":"","title":"Derived Fields"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#What-this-page-covers","page":"Derived fields","text":"A run integrates two fields in time and computes many more from them along the way. This page states which of those computed quantities the package keeps in memory from one time step to the next, which it recomputes when asked, and why the division falls where it does.\n\nIt is written for someone who wants to read fields out of a running simulation — for a diagnostic, a statistic, or a figure — and needs to know what is available, at which instant it is valid, and what requesting it costs. The rules below are stated first in general terms, then applied to the equations this package solves.\n\nThroughout, time level means one of the instants t_0 t_1 t_2 dots at which time steps begin and end, in the usual finite-difference sense. A run of n steps passes through n+1 time levels. A derived field is any field computed from the state rather than integrated in time.\n\n","title":"What this page covers"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#1.-Principles","page":"Derived fields","text":"","title":"1. Principles"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#1.1-The-state,-and-everything-else","page":"Derived fields","text":"Exactly two fields are integrated in time: the velocity boldsymbolu and the passive scalar varphi, both held in spectral representation as hatboldsymbolu and hatvarphi. These are the state. They are the only thing a restart needs, and every other field in the run is a function of them.\n\nDerived fields are not lesser objects — the physical velocity, the vorticity, and the advective tendency are what most diagnostics actually want — but they are reproducible. Keeping one is therefore a choice about time and memory, never about information.","title":"1.1 The state, and everything else"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#1.2-A-time-step-is-what-makes-a-derived-field-obsolete","page":"Derived fields","text":"Nothing invalidates a derived field except an advance of the state. Between two time levels, the velocity in physical representation is a fixed object; once a step completes, every field derived from the old state is obsolete simultaneously.\n\nThis is why time levels, rather than steps, are the natural instants for observation: a derived field is meaningful at t_n, and a run of n steps offers n+1 such instants, including the initial condition.","title":"1.2 A time step is what makes a derived field obsolete"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#1.3-What-may-be-kept:-three-conditions","page":"Derived fields","text":"A derived quantity is kept from one time level to the next only when all three of the following hold. Each excludes something real, and dropping any one of them admits a value that is either wrong or expensive.\n\nIt is a function of the state at a time level, and of nothing else. Every argument must be evaluated at that instant — not at an intermediate stage of the time-stepping scheme, and for a quantity carried along a particle trajectory, not at an intermediate particle position either. The quantity may not depend on the step size Delta t, on a forcing's internal variables, or on the coefficients of the integration scheme.\nThe step that begins at that time level computes it anyway. The package does not perform extra work so that a quantity can be stored. If the solver would not compute it, storing it would mean paying for it, and the user is better served by computing it themselves at the moment they need it.\nThe solver writes it to an array. Some intermediate results are consumed inside a single pass over the grid and never exist as a complete field in memory. Such a quantity cannot be handed to a reader without first restructuring the computation to write it out, which costs both memory and a pass over the grid.\n\nCondition 1 is about meaning, condition 2 about cost, condition 3 about the shape of the arithmetic.","title":"1.3 What may be kept: three conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#1.4-*When*-a-quantity-is-computed-is-a-choice,-not-a-property","page":"Derived fields","text":"A quantity satisfying conditions 1 and 2 can be evaluated as soon as the state exists — that is, at the end of the step that produced it — instead of at the start of the step that consumes it. The two give identical values, because nothing between them alters the state, and they cost the same, because the work happens once either way.\n\nThe package uses this: every kept derived field is evaluated at the end of a step, from the just-advanced state. A diagnostic reading between steps therefore finds current values without triggering any computation, and the following step reads what is already there instead of building it again. The total number of Fourier transforms per step is unchanged by this arrangement.\n\nThe consequence worth stating plainly is that \"this quantity is not available at a time level\" is almost never a fact about the quantity. It is a statement about where the code happens to place the computation, and that placement can be moved.","title":"1.4 When a quantity is computed is a choice, not a property"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#1.5-Values-that-cross-a-time-level-are-shared-through-the-record;-values-inside-a-step-are-not","page":"Derived fields","text":"A quantity that must survive from one time level to the next is held in the derived-field record and read from it. A quantity used only within a single step is passed directly from the routine that computes it to the routine that consumes it.\n\nThis division is not stylistic. Within a step, a Runge–Kutta scheme evaluates the right-hand side at several intermediate states, and a routine that fetched the velocity by name could not tell which of those states it was being handed. Passing intermediate values explicitly makes such a mismatch impossible to express. The record deliberately has no vocabulary for intermediate states.","title":"1.5 Values that cross a time level are shared through the record; values inside a step are not"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#1.6-Storage-is-requested,-and-the-list-of-what-may-be-requested-is-fixed-by-the-solver","page":"Derived fields","text":"Storing a field costs memory proportional to the grid, so no derived field is kept unless the run is configured to keep it. Conditions 1–3 make the list of requestable quantities finite and derivable: it is exactly the set of quantities the solver already evaluates at a time level and writes to an array. Anything outside that set remains available by computing it directly from the state.","title":"1.6 Storage is requested, and the list of what may be requested is fixed by the solver"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#1.7-Each-stored-value-records-the-time-level-it-belongs-to","page":"Derived fields","text":"A stored field carries the index of the time level at which it was computed, and a read compares that index with the run's current one. This is what makes a stale value impossible to obtain: a field whose recorded index is behind the run's is recomputed before it is returned, rather than handed back as though it were current.\n\nRecording the index, rather than a validity flag, is also what allows a scheme to keep several time levels of the same quantity — a multi-step particle integrator holds the velocity along the trajectory at t_n, t_n-1, and so on, each labelled with the instant it belongs to.","title":"1.7 Each stored value records the time level it belongs to"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#1.8-Derived-fields-are-defined-at-time-levels,-and-only-there","page":"Derived fields","text":"Between time levels — that is, while a step is in progress — the arrays holding derived fields serve as the solver's working storage for intermediate states. This is what allows a stored field and the solver's own intermediate register to be one array rather than two.\n\nThe contract is therefore: a derived field is defined at every time level and undefined during a step. Reading one from inside a step is an error, and is reported as one.\n\n","title":"1.8 Derived fields are defined at time levels, and only there"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#2.-How-the-solver-works","page":"Derived fields","text":"The rules above refer to the arithmetic of a step, so this section sets out what a step does. Only the structure matters here; the schemes themselves are documented with the Propagator module.","title":"2. How the solver works"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#2.1-The-equations","page":"Derived fields","text":"The package advances the incompressible Navier–Stokes equations in rotational form on a triple-periodic box, together with a passive scalar and a set of tracer particles. Writing the convective term through the identity (boldsymbolucdotnabla)boldsymbolu = nabla(tfrac12lvertboldsymbolurvert^2) - boldsymbolutimesboldsymbolomega and letting the Leray–Helmholtz projection mathbb P onto divergence-free fields absorb both the pressure and the kinetic-energy gradient,\n\nbeginaligned\npartial_t boldsymbolu = mathbb P(boldsymbolutimesboldsymbolomega)\n                             + nunabla^2boldsymbolu + boldsymbolf\nqquad nablacdotboldsymbolu = 0 2pt\npartial_t varphi = -nablacdot(boldsymboluvarphi)\n                      + varkappanabla^2varphi + f_varphi 2pt\nfracmathrm dboldsymbolXmathrm d t = boldsymbolu(boldsymbolX(t) t)\nendaligned\n\nwith boldsymbolomega = nablatimesboldsymbolu the vorticity, nu the kinematic viscosity, varkappa = numathrmSc the scalar diffusivity, boldsymbolf and f_varphi the forcings, and boldsymbolX(t) the position of a tracer particle. The scalar advection is written in conservative form, which equals -boldsymbolucdotnablavarphi because the velocity is divergence-free, and which conserves the scalar mean exactly under the dealiasing rule.","title":"2.1 The equations"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#2.2-Which-terms-are-evaluated,-and-which-are-not","page":"Derived fields","text":"The three terms on each right-hand side are treated differently, and only one of them is ever evaluated as a field:\n\nThe quadratic terms mathbb P(boldsymbolutimesboldsymbolomega) and -nablacdot(boldsymboluvarphi) are evaluated explicitly, and doing so is essentially the entire cost of a step.\nThe diffusion terms nunabla^2boldsymbolu and varkappanabla^2varphi are never formed. They are diagonal in the spectral representation, so they are integrated exactly by multiplying the field by the integrating factor mathcal E(boldsymbolkappa) = exp(-Dlvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2Delta t) with D = nu or varkappa. No dissipation field exists at any point in the calculation.\nThe forcings enter through one of two paths. A forcing linear in the field folds its rate into the same exponential mathcal E; any remaining part is added to the right-hand side at each stage.","title":"2.2 Which terms are evaluated, and which are not"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#2.3-Where-the-Fourier-transforms-happen","page":"Derived fields","text":"The state is held in spectral representation, where derivatives are multiplications and the projection is a per-wavenumber operation. A product of two fields, however, is a convolution in that representation, and evaluating it directly would cost far more than transforming. So each evaluation of a quadratic term follows the pseudospectral route: transform to physical representation, multiply pointwise, transform back, and remove the aliasing error the pointwise product introduced.\n\nFor the velocity, one evaluation is:\n\nrecover boldsymbolu in physical representation from hatboldsymbolu — 3 inverse transforms;\nform hatboldsymbolomega = mathrm iboldsymbolkappatimeshatboldsymbolu and recover boldsymbolomega in physical representation — 3 inverse transforms;\nmultiply pointwise to obtain the Lamb vector boldsymbolutimesboldsymbolomega on the grid;\ntransform the product back — 3 forward transforms;\napply the projection mathbb P and the dealiasing rule, both per-wavenumber multiplications.\n\nSix inverse and three forward transforms per evaluation. The scalar adds one inverse transform to recover varphi and three forward transforms for the flux boldsymboluvarphi.\n\nThese transforms carry essentially all of the arithmetic: one scalar transform costs mathcal O(N^3log N^3) operations, some 10^2–10^3 per grid point, whereas every pointwise operation in the list costs of order ten. Consequently the meaningful measure of a step's cost is its transform count, and the meaningful question about storage is whether keeping a field changes that count. Arranging for kept fields to be evaluated at the end of a step, as in §1.4, does not.\n\nA three-stage Runge–Kutta step evaluates each right-hand side three times, giving 18 inverse and 9 forward transforms per step for velocity alone, and 21 inverse and 18 forward with the scalar.","title":"2.3 Where the Fourier transforms happen"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#2.4-Where-the-particles-fit","page":"Derived fields","text":"Tracer positions are advanced by a two-stage scheme requiring the velocity at the start of the step, sampled at the particle positions, and the velocity at the end of the step, sampled at an intermediate predicted position. Both samples are Mtimes3 arrays for M particles, obtained by interpolating the physical velocity field — so the particles consume a derived field and produce point values, never a field of their own.\n\n","title":"2.4 Where the particles fit"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#3.-Applying-the-principles","page":"Derived fields","text":"","title":"3. Applying the principles"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#3.1-Term-by-term","page":"Derived fields","text":"Every quantity appearing in §2.1, with its disposition and the condition of §1.3 that decides it.\n\nQuantity Representation Kept at a time level Reason\nhatboldsymbolu, hatvarphi spectral always — this is the state integrated in time, not derived\nboldsymbolu physical, grid yes evaluated at t_n by the step, and written to an array\nboldsymbolomega physical, grid on request same, but costs one vector field to keep\nvarphi physical, grid on request same, when the run carries a scalar\nmathbb P(boldsymbolutimesboldsymbolomega), dealiased spectral on request a function of the state alone\n-nablacdot(boldsymboluvarphi), dealiased spectral on request a function of the state alone\nboldsymbolu(boldsymbolX(t_n) t_n) Mtimes3 point values yes the velocity along the trajectory; the quantity Lagrangian statistics accumulate\nboldsymbolutimesboldsymbolomega physical, grid no the step carries the projected form forward, so keeping the bare product would cost an extra field (condition 2)\nnunabla^2boldsymbolu, varkappanabla^2varphi — never formed applied as a spectral multiplier; no such field exists (condition 3)\nboldsymbolf, f_varphi spectral no depends on Delta t and on a forcing's internal variables, so not a function of the state alone (condition 1)\nintermediate boldsymbolu, boldsymbolomega physical, grid no evaluated at an intermediate stage, not at a time level (condition 1)\nvelocity at the predicted particle position Mtimes3 point values no evaluated at a position no particle occupies (condition 1, on the position argument)","title":"3.1 Term by term"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#3.2-Reading-the-table","page":"Derived fields","text":"Three observations are worth drawing out.\n\nThe pressure never appears. It is absorbed by the projection mathbb P and is not computed at any stage, so it is not a derived field that could be requested. Obtaining it requires solving its Poisson equation separately.\n\nThe dissipation term is not a field. nunabla^2boldsymbolu is integrated as a multiplier, so no array holding it ever exists. The dissipation rate varepsilon = nulanglelvertboldsymbolomegarvert^2rangle is a different object — a single number, obtained by a reduction over the state — and is available at any time through dissipation.\n\nThe two velocity samples along a trajectory are not the same kind of object. The start-of-step sample boldsymbolu(boldsymbolX(t_n) t_n) is the velocity at a position the particle occupies at an instant it occupies it, and is the quantity a Lagrangian velocity statistic accumulates. The second sample is taken at a predicted position that the particle never reaches — it differs from the endpoint position by mathcal O(Delta t^2) — and exists only to give the position update its second-order accuracy. It is an intermediate quantity of the integration scheme that happens to have the units of a velocity, and it is not available for reading.","title":"3.2 Reading the table"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#3.3-What-keeping-a-field-costs","page":"Derived fields","text":"Because a stored field and the solver's own intermediate register can be the same array (§1.8), requesting a derived field does not necessarily allocate anything new. What it costs is the freedom to overwrite that array.\n\nThe vorticity is the clearest case. When boldsymbolomega is not requested, the array holding it can receive the product boldsymbolutimesboldsymbolomega as it is formed, since the vorticity is not read again afterwards. When it is requested, it must survive, and the product needs an array of its own. Requesting the vorticity therefore costs one vector field on the grid — 384 MiB at 256^3 in double precision — paid as an overwrite that can no longer be performed, rather than as a new allocation.\n\nThe number of Fourier transforms is unchanged either way.\n\n","title":"3.3 What keeping a field costs"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#4.-Reading-derived-fields-from-a-run","page":"Derived fields","text":"Storage is requested when the observation layer is built, and reading a field that was not requested is an error rather than a silent recomputation, so that the memory a run occupies is a stated property of its configuration:\n\nderived = DerivedFields(domain; charts = (:velocity, :vorticity))\n\nThe accessors physical_velocity and physical_vorticity return the stored field when it is current for the present state and recompute it first otherwise, so a stale value cannot be obtained. Tracer point values are read with tracer_velocities.\n\nIf a run changes the state outside the time-stepping routine — reseeding an initial condition, say, or applying a filter — it must announce that through resolved_fields_modified!, which marks every stored field as belonging to a superseded time level. The routines that mutate the state as part of their normal operation do this themselves.\n\nQuantities outside the list of §3.1 are computed directly from the state at the moment they are wanted. This is the intended route for anything an individual study needs: it costs the transforms that quantity requires and no resident memory, which is the correct trade for something read occasionally rather than every step.\n\n","title":"4. Reading derived fields from a run"},{"category":"section","location":"derived_fields/#5.-Current-state-of-the-implementation","page":"Derived fields","text":"This page states the design. The parts already in place are the observation layer itself, the requested-storage rule, the recorded time level with recomputation on a stale read, and the tracer velocity along the trajectory. The following differ today and are being brought into line:\n\nStored fields are evaluated when first read rather than at the end of the preceding step, so a diagnostic running between steps may trigger a recomputation that the arrangement of §1.4 avoids.\nA stored field and the solver's intermediate register are separate arrays, so the saving described in §1.8 and §3.3 is not yet taken.\nThe velocity along the trajectory is held with the particle set rather than in the derived-field record, and its currency is a documented precondition rather than a recorded time level.\nThe two advective terms of §3.1 are not yet requestable.\n\nThe rationale for each, and the record of what changed when, is in the architecture notes.","title":"5. Current state of the implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/phenomenology/StructureFunctions/#StructureFunctions","page":"StructureFunctions","text":"","title":"StructureFunctions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/phenomenology/StructureFunctions/#Laws","page":"StructureFunctions","text":"The inertial-range structure-function laws, evaluated by structure_function (amplitudes) or scaling_exponent (the intermittency exponents, for SheLeveque1994 and Kolmogorov1962).","title":"Laws"},{"category":"section","location":"api/phenomenology/StructureFunctions/#References","page":"StructureFunctions","text":"The published provenance of each law on this page, read from its provenance(law) — the extraction-bound leaf carries its own citations, so these are not duplicated in the ARCHITECTURE.md reference list.\n\nimport GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology as Phen\nimport GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.StructureFunctions as SF\nimport Markdown\nlaws = [SF.Kolmogorov1941(), SF.Kolmogorov1962(), SF.SheLeveque1994(), SF.Yaglom1949(), SF.Lundgren2002()]\ncites = unique([Phen.provenance(l).citation for l in laws])\nMarkdown.parse(join([\"- \" * c for c in cites], \"\\n\"))","title":"References"},{"category":"module","location":"api/phenomenology/StructureFunctions/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.StructureFunctions","page":"StructureFunctions","text":"Phenomenology.StructureFunctions\n\nInertial-range structure-function laws — parametrizations of the longitudinal velocity structure function D_Ldots(r) and the mixed velocity–scalar structure function langledelta u_L (deltavarphi)^2rangle (Yaglom), evaluated by structure_function.\n\nKolmogorov1941 — the K41 inertial-range laws: the exact 4/5 law, the 2/3 law, and the self-similar exponents (via scaling_exponent).\nKolmogorov1962 / SheLeveque1994 — the K62 log-normal and She–Lévêque intermittency models: the anomalous scaling exponents zeta_p (via scaling_exponent).\nYaglom1949 — the exact 4/3 law for the mixed velocity–scalar structure function.\nLundgren2002 — the finite-Reynolds-number correction to the 4/5 law.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.StructureFunctions"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/StructureFunctions/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.StructureFunctions.StructureFunction","page":"StructureFunctions","text":"StructureFunction <: TurbulenceLaw\n\nSupertype of the inertial-range structure-function laws — parametrizations of the longitudinal velocity moments D_Ldots(r) = langle(delta u_L)^prangle (the p-th moment of the longitudinal velocity increment across a separation r) and the mixed velocity–scalar moment langledelta u_L (deltavarphi)^2rangle (Yaglom1949), evaluated by structure_function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.StructureFunctions.StructureFunction"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/StructureFunctions/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.StructureFunctions.Kolmogorov1941","page":"StructureFunctions","text":"Kolmogorov1941(; C₂ = 2.0)\n\nThe Kolmogorov (1941) inertial-range structure-function laws for the longitudinal velocity increment delta u_L. structure_function(law, r, fp; order) returns:\n\norder = 2 — the 2/3 law D_LL(r) = C_2 (varepsilon r)^23, the second-order increment; the constant C_2 approx 20 is empirical (default 2.0).\norder = 3 — the 4/5 law D_LLL(r) = -tfrac45 varepsilon r, the third-order increment; this one is exact (a consequence of the Kármán–Howarth–Monin relation), carrying no free constant.\n\nBoth hold in the inertial range eta ll r ll L. The state fp (a FlowParameters) supplies the dissipation rate varepsilon; any other order throws (K41 fixes only these two longitudinal moments universally). Its scaling_exponent(law; order) gives the self-similar K41 exponent zeta_p = p3.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.StructureFunctions.Kolmogorov1941"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/StructureFunctions/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.StructureFunctions.Kolmogorov1962","page":"StructureFunctions","text":"Kolmogorov1962(; μ = 0.25)\n\nThe Kolmogorov (1962) log-normal (refined-similarity) intermittency model. Its scaling_exponent(law; order) gives\n\nzeta_p = fracp3 - fracmu18 p (p - 3)\n\nwith μ the intermittency exponent (approx 025). Like SheLeveque1994 it is exact at zeta_3 = 1 and bends below the self-similar p3 for p  3, but as a quadratic in p (the log-normal ansatz) rather than She–Lévêque's log-Poisson form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.StructureFunctions.Kolmogorov1962"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/StructureFunctions/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.StructureFunctions.SheLeveque1994","page":"StructureFunctions","text":"SheLeveque1994()\n\nThe She–Lévêque (1994) intermittency model for the inertial-range scaling exponents. Its scaling_exponent(law; order) gives the anomalous exponent\n\nzeta_p = fracp9 + 2left1 - left(tfrac23right)^p3right\n\nof the p-th-order longitudinal structure function D_Ldots(r) sim r^zeta_p. It is parameter-free, exact at zeta_3 = 1 (the 4/5 law), and bends below the self-similar p3 for p  3 — the signature of small-scale intermittency. It predicts exponents, not amplitudes, so it implements scaling_exponent but not structure_function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.StructureFunctions.SheLeveque1994"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/StructureFunctions/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.StructureFunctions.Yaglom1949","page":"StructureFunctions","text":"Yaglom1949()\n\nThe Yaglom (1949) \"4/3 law\" — the exact inertial-range scaling of the mixed third-order velocity–scalar structure function, the passive-scalar analogue of the 4/5 law (a consequence of the Yaglom relation); it carries no free constant. Its classical -tfrac43 coefficient is stated for the dissipation rate of the half-variance tfrac12langlevarphi^2rangle, namely varkappalanglelvertnablavarphirvert^2rangle — just as the 4/5 law pairs with varepsilon = nulanglelvertboldsymbolomegarvert^2rangle, the dissipation of the half-energy k. This package's varepsilon_varphi = 2varkappalanglelvertnablavarphirvert^2rangle (scalar_dissipation) is the full-variance rate, twice that, so in these variables the law is\n\nlangledelta u_L (deltavarphi)^2rangle(r) = -tfrac23 varepsilon_varphi r\n\nstructure_function(law, r, fp; order = 3) returns it, reading the scalar dissipation varepsilon_varphi (fp.scalar_dissipation, which must be set); any other order throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.StructureFunctions.Yaglom1949"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/StructureFunctions/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.StructureFunctions.Lundgren2002","page":"StructureFunctions","text":"Lundgren2002()\n\nThe finite-Reynolds-number correction to the 4/5 law for the third-order longitudinal velocity structure function,\n\nD_LLL(r) = -left(tfrac45 - frac845mathrmRe_lambda^23right)varepsilon r\n\nwhich relaxes to the exact -tfrac45varepsilon r of Kolmogorov1941 as mathrmRe_lambda to infty — turbulence at a finite Reynolds number never quite reaches the asymptotic 4/5 slope (Lundgren 2002; Sagaut & Cambon 2018, Table 4.5). structure_function(law, r, fp; order = 3) returns it, reading mathrmRe_lambda from fp via taylor_reynolds; any other order throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.StructureFunctions.Lundgren2002"},{"category":"section","location":"api/phenomenology/Phenomenology/#Reference-laws-—-the-interface","page":"Reference laws","text":"","title":"Reference laws — the interface"},{"category":"section","location":"api/phenomenology/Phenomenology/#The-flow-state-bundle","page":"Reference laws","text":"The dimensionless flow state a law is evaluated against.\n\nThe derived scales, computed from the bundle.","title":"The flow-state bundle"},{"category":"section","location":"api/phenomenology/Phenomenology/#The-law-interface","page":"Reference laws","text":"The law supertype and its provenance.\n\nThe per-family evaluators — spectrum, structure_function / scaling_exponent, and scaling — a law's provenance, and the fit seam.","title":"The law interface"},{"category":"module","location":"api/phenomenology/Phenomenology/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology","page":"Reference laws","text":"Phenomenology\n\nReference turbulence phenomenology — the fitted scalings, model spectra, and structure-function laws that theory and experiment have established, each an author-year parametrization of an abstract law family. This is the predicted counterpart to the measured Observables: a provider the validation layer reads to compare a run against, never a comparer itself and never assumed by the dynamics.\n\nA law family is an abstract type; each parametrization is a named preset — usually a concrete subtype carrying its fitted coefficients and its provenance, or (for the composed velocity spectra like Spectra.Pope2000) a factory function that assembles one. Choosing a law is choosing the preset (the published fit is the default); a name is disambiguated by its submodule (the quantity), its author-year (the paper), and a trailing letter (a same-year collision).\n\nThe flow state a law reads:\n\nFlowParameters — the flow-state bundle a law evaluates against, with the derived scales kolmogorov_length, kolmogorov_time, taylor_microscale, taylor_reynolds, and scalar_diffusivity as helpers.\nprovenance — a law's citation and validity range (a Reference); fit is the (future) seam for fitting a parametrization to measured data.\n\nThe law families, one submodule per representation:\n\nSpectra — model velocity and scalar spectra, functions of wavenumber, evaluated by spectrum.\nStructureFunctions — the inertial-range laws and their intermittency exponents, evaluated by structure_function and scaling_exponent.\nScalings — the dimensionless constants and their Reynolds-number trends, evaluated by scaling.\n\nnote: Extraction-bound\nBuilt inside GBGIsotropicTurbulence but designed to be cut into a standalone GBGTurbulencePhenomenology with no rewrite: it takes no field/statistics/dynamics dependency, and the umbrella does not re-export it — reach the laws opt-in through Phenomenology (using GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Phenomenology/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.FlowParameters","page":"Reference laws","text":"FlowParameters(; viscosity, dissipation, rms_velocity, integral_scale,\n                 schmidt = 1.0, scalar_dissipation = NaN)\n\nThe flow-state bundle a law is evaluated against — the analogue of FlowConditions and FixedDomain on the phenomenology side. It carries the independent dimensional quantities of a homogeneous isotropic state (nu, varepsilon, u, L_11); the small scales and the dimensionless Taylor-scale Reynolds number are derived from them, not stored.\n\nFields (all keyword; a homogeneous-isotropic state fixes the rest):\n\nviscosity — the kinematic viscosity nu.\ndissipation — the mean dissipation rate varepsilon = nulanglelvertboldsymbolomegarvert^2rangle.\nrms_velocity — the single-component r.m.s. velocity u (so the turbulent kinetic energy is k = tfrac32 u^2).\nintegral_scale — the longitudinal integral length L_11 (a large-scale quantity, independent of nu and varepsilon; it fixes the energy-containing range).\nschmidt — the Schmidt number mathrmSc (the scalar diffusivity is varkappa = numathrmSc); default 1.0.\nscalar_dissipation — the scalar dissipation rate varepsilon_varphi (only scalar laws read it); default NaN (unset).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.FlowParameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/phenomenology/Phenomenology/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.kolmogorov_length","page":"Reference laws","text":"kolmogorov_length(fp) -> Float64\n\nThe Kolmogorov (dissipation) length eta = (nu^3varepsilon)^14 of the state fp (a FlowParameters) — the scale at which viscosity terminates the cascade.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.kolmogorov_length"},{"category":"function","location":"api/phenomenology/Phenomenology/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.kolmogorov_time","page":"Reference laws","text":"kolmogorov_time(fp) -> Float64\n\nThe Kolmogorov time tau_eta = (nuvarepsilon)^12 of the state fp (a FlowParameters) — the turnover time of the smallest eddies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.kolmogorov_time"},{"category":"function","location":"api/phenomenology/Phenomenology/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.taylor_microscale","page":"Reference laws","text":"taylor_microscale(fp) -> Float64\n\nThe Taylor microscale lambda = sqrt15 nu u^2varepsilon of the state fp (a FlowParameters) — the intermediate scale set by the isotropic relation varepsilon = 15 nu u^2lambda^2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.taylor_microscale"},{"category":"function","location":"api/phenomenology/Phenomenology/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.taylor_reynolds","page":"Reference laws","text":"taylor_reynolds(fp) -> Float64\n\nThe Taylor-scale Reynolds number mathrmRe_lambda = ulambdanu of the state fp (a FlowParameters) — the standard measure of how developed the turbulence is, and the argument of every Scalings trend. Equivalently mathrmRe_lambda = u^2sqrt15(nuvarepsilon).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.taylor_reynolds"},{"category":"function","location":"api/phenomenology/Phenomenology/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.scalar_diffusivity","page":"Reference laws","text":"scalar_diffusivity(fp) -> Float64\n\nThe scalar diffusivity varkappa = numathrmSc of the state fp (a FlowParameters) — the diffusivity of the passive scalar varphi, set by the Schmidt number.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.scalar_diffusivity"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Phenomenology/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.TurbulenceLaw","page":"Reference laws","text":"TurbulenceLaw\n\nSupertype of every reference law — a model spectrum, a structure-function law, or a scaling constant. A concrete law is a parametrization: it holds its fitted coefficients, reports its provenance, and implements the evaluator for its family (spectrum, structure_function, or scaling), all against a FlowParameters bundle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.TurbulenceLaw"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Phenomenology/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Reference","page":"Reference laws","text":"Reference(citation; valid_Reλ = (0.0, Inf))\n\nThe provenance of a law: a citation string (a full author–year reference) and the Taylor-scale-Reynolds-number range valid_Reλ = (lo, hi) over which the fit is meant to hold. A comparison reads valid_Reλ so a law is not judged outside its stated domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Reference"},{"category":"function","location":"api/phenomenology/Phenomenology/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.provenance","page":"Reference laws","text":"provenance(law) -> Reference\n\nThe Reference of a law (a TurbulenceLaw) — its citation and stated Reynolds-number validity range. Every parametrization implements it; it is the single source of the law's attribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.provenance"},{"category":"function","location":"api/phenomenology/Phenomenology/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.spectrum","page":"Reference laws","text":"spectrum(law, κ, fp) -> Float64\n\nThe reference spectrum predicted by a model law at wavenumber κ for the state fp (a FlowParameters) — the velocity energy spectrum E(kappa) for an EnergySpectrum (the density in wavenumber of the kinetic energy, k = int_0^infty E(kappa)mathrm dkappa), or the scalar spectrum E_varphi(kappa) for a ScalarSpectrum. Its home and the composed form are in Spectra.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.spectrum"},{"category":"function","location":"api/phenomenology/Phenomenology/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.structure_function","page":"Reference laws","text":"structure_function(law, r, fp; order) -> Float64\n\nThe reference structure function of the given order predicted by a law (a StructureFunction) at separation r for the state fp (a FlowParameters). Most laws give the longitudinal velocity moment D_Ldots(r) = langle(delta u_L)^prangle (the order-th moment p) of the velocity increment delta u_L across a distance r; a mixed velocity–scalar law (Yaglom1949) instead gives langledelta u_L (deltavarphi)^2rangle, the transport of scalar variance. Its home is StructureFunctions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.structure_function"},{"category":"function","location":"api/phenomenology/Phenomenology/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.scaling_exponent","page":"Reference laws","text":"scaling_exponent(law; order) -> Float64\n\nThe inertial-range scaling exponent zeta_p of the order-p longitudinal velocity structure function, D_Ldots(r) sim r^zeta_p, predicted by a law (a StructureFunction) — the self-similar zeta_p = p3 of K41, or the anomalous exponent of an intermittency model. Its home is StructureFunctions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.scaling_exponent"},{"category":"function","location":"api/phenomenology/Phenomenology/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.scaling","page":"Reference laws","text":"scaling(law, fp) -> Float64\n\nThe reference dimensionless constant predicted by a scaling law (a ScalingLaw) for the state fp (a FlowParameters) — a single number or its Reynolds-number trend (e.g. the normalized dissipation C_varepsilon or the Kolmogorov constant C_K). Its home is Scalings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.scaling"},{"category":"function","location":"api/phenomenology/Phenomenology/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.fit","page":"Reference laws","text":"fit(::Type{L}, ...) -> L\n\nFit a parametrization of law family L to measured data — the seam by which a run's own measurements become a parametrization sitting beside the literature. No methods are defined yet; it is the recorded extension point for fitting a run's own measurements to a parametrization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.fit"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Observables/#Observables","page":"Observables","text":"","title":"Observables"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Observables/#Energy-and-variance","page":"Observables","text":"The velocity kinetic energy and its scalar analogue, the variance — both Parseval quadratic invariants.","title":"Energy and variance"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Observables/#Dissipation-rates","page":"Observables","text":"The kinetic-energy dissipation ε and the scalar-variance dissipation ε_φ.","title":"Dissipation rates"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Observables/#Derivative-skewness","page":"Observables","text":"The velocity-derivative skewness — the developed-turbulence marker that gates warm-up.","title":"Derivative skewness"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Observables/#Timestep-control","page":"Observables","text":"The maximum velocity component, which sets the advective-CFL step — a max-norm reduction, not a physics invariant.","title":"Timestep control"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Observables/#Spectrum-and-length-scale","page":"Observables","text":"The measured energy spectrum and the integral scale — the measured counterparts of a Phenomenology model spectrum, for validation.","title":"Spectrum and length scale"},{"category":"module","location":"api/Observables/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables","page":"Observables","text":"Observables\n\nThe measured functions of the flow state:\n\nkinetic_energy, scalar_variance — the turbulent kinetic energy and the scalar variance.\ndissipation, scalar_dissipation — their dissipation rates.\nderivative_skewness — the velocity-derivative skewness, the K41 (Kolmogorov 1941) marker of developed turbulence.\nmax_speed — the maximum velocity component, which sets the advective-CFL step.\nenergy_spectrum, integral_scale — the measured energy spectrum and the integral length scale derived from it.\n\nTheir plateaus gate warm-up (warmup!). Each reduces a field to a scalar (the energy spectrum, to a distribution over wavenumber); none dealiases first. The quadratic reductions (kinetic_energy, dissipation, scalar_variance, scalar_dissipation) are Parseval-exact: each is a normalized inner product, summed exactly from the spectral coefficients regardless of representation; the equivalent physical-space mean picks up no aliasing either, because the quadratic's zero mode folds in nothing when 2kappa_max  N (the 23 rule). The cubic derivative_skewness is not: its grid mean folds a small residual from the truncation edge back into the mean, so it carries a slight aliasing on a 23 field (negligible when well-resolved; exact cubic dealiasing would need the 12 rule).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Observables/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables.kinetic_energy","page":"Observables","text":"kinetic_energy(u)\n\nThe turbulent kinetic energy tfrac12langlelvertboldsymbolurvert^2rangle of the velocity field u. Computed as tfrac12 langleboldsymboluboldsymbolurangle with the normalized inner product, so by Parseval's theorem it accepts u in either representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables.kinetic_energy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Observables/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables.scalar_variance","page":"Observables","text":"scalar_variance(φ)\n\nThe scalar variance langlevarphi^2rangle of the scalar field φ (for a mean-zero varphi). Computed as langlevarphivarphirangle with the normalized inner product, so by Parseval's theorem it accepts φ in either representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables.scalar_variance"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Observables/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables.dissipation","page":"Observables","text":"dissipation(ω, viscosity)\ndissipation(û, domain, viscosity)\n\nThe mean kinetic-energy dissipation rate of a homogeneous flow — a single number, not a field — at the kinematic viscosity nu:\n\nvarepsilon = nu langlelvertboldsymbolomegarvert^2rangle\n            = 2nulangle S_ijS_ijrangle\n\nthe two forms equal for a homogeneous flow, with the strain-rate tensor S_ij = tfrac12(partial_x_ju_i + partial_x_iu_j).\n\nTwo routes to the same rate; take the one whose input is already in hand.\n\ndissipation(ω, viscosity) — from a vorticity field ω boldsymbolomega in either representation, as the normalized inner product (Parseval). Use it when boldsymbolomega exists for its own sake: the observation cache's chart, or a vorticity the caller supplies.\ndissipation(û, domain, viscosity) — straight from the velocity in spectral representation û hatboldsymbolu, with the FixedDomain domain supplying the multiplier, and no vorticity formed at all. Use it to measure off the state, where a boldsymbolomega would be built only to be reduced away.\n\nThe second route trades the curl for a lvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2-weighted spectral inner product against the domain.squared_wavenumber_kernel multiplier: from\n\nlvertboldsymbolkappatimeshatboldsymbolurvert^2\n  = lvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2lverthatboldsymbolurvert^2\n  - lvertboldsymbolkappacdothatboldsymbolurvert^2\n\nthe enstrophy of a divergence-free velocity is its Dirichlet (H^1) energy, langlelvertboldsymbolomegarvert^2rangle = langlehatboldsymbolulvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2hatboldsymbolurangle.\n\nwarning: Warning\nThe û route drops the lvertboldsymbolkappacdothatboldsymbolurvert^2 term, so it holds only for a divergence-free velocity — exact for a state advanced by the Leray-projected dynamics, wrong for an arbitrary vector field. The vorticity route carries no such condition. (The scalar twin, scalar_dissipation, is unconditional: a scalar has no solenoidal constraint to invoke.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables.dissipation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Observables/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables.scalar_dissipation","page":"Observables","text":"scalar_dissipation(φ̂, domain, diffusivity)\n\nThe mean scalar-variance dissipation rate varepsilon_varphi = 2varkappa langlelvertnablavarphirvert^2rangle, from the scalar field in spectral representation φ̂ hatvarphi, the FixedDomain bundle domain, and the scalar diffusivity varkappa = numathrmSc. The gradient energy langlelvertnablavarphirvert^2rangle is the Dirichlet (H^1) energy langlevarphilvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2varphirangle — a lvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2-weighted spectral inner product against the domain.squared_wavenumber_kernel multiplier, formed straight from the spectral coefficients with no physical gradient nablavarphi materialized. It is the scalar twin of dissipation's spectral route, and unconditional where that one needs a divergence-free velocity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables.scalar_dissipation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Observables/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables.derivative_skewness","page":"Observables","text":"derivative_skewness(û, domain)\n\nThe velocity-derivative skewness S, written coordinate-free as the normalized third invariant of the strain-rate tensor boldsymbolS = operatornamesymnablaboldsymbolu (the italic S is the scalar skewness, the bold boldsymbolS the strain-rate tensor):\n\nbeginaligned\nS = C fraclangleoperatornametrboldsymbolS^3ranglelangleboldsymbolSboldsymbolSrangle^32\n   = 3C fraclangledetboldsymbolSranglelangleboldsymbolSboldsymbolSrangle^32 \nC = tfrac47sqrt152 approx 15649\nendaligned\n\nBecause boldsymbolS is symmetric and, for the incompressible field, traceless, operatornametrboldsymbolS^3 = 3detboldsymbolS. By isotropy this equals the classical longitudinal skewness langle(partial_x_1 u_1)^3rangle  langle(partial_x_1 u_1)^2rangle^32; the constant follows from langleoperatornametrboldsymbolS^3rangle = tfrac1058langle(partial_x_1 u_1)^3rangle and langleboldsymbolSboldsymbolSrangle = tfrac152langle(partial_x_1 u_1)^2rangle (Betchov 1956). So the reported value is the familiar S approx -05 of developed turbulence, but from a genuine tensor invariant rather than an axis-dependent gradient.\n\nFrom the velocity field û in spectral representation, the strain is formed directly with symmetric_gradient and the wavenumber multipliers of the FixedDomain bundle domain (skipping the full nine-component velocity gradient), carried to physical space by its rank-2 tensor transform plans, and reduced to langleboldsymbolSboldsymbolSrangle (inner) and langledetboldsymbolSrangle (det); domain also sizes the per-call tensor and scalar scratch. Because langledetboldsymbolSrangle is a cubic invariant, its grid mean carries a small residual aliasing on a 23-truncated field — negligible for a well-resolved run, but, unlike the quadratic reductions, not exactly alias-free. S is the classic marker of developed turbulence: a random-phase (Gaussian) field has S approx 0 (all odd invariants vanish), while fully developed isotropic turbulence has S approx -05 (an empirical, weakly Reynolds-number-dependent value) — the enstrophy-production / vortex-stretching signature. Its plateau is the strict \"real turbulence yet\" gate of the warm-up detector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables.derivative_skewness"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Observables/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables.max_speed","page":"Observables","text":"max_speed(u)\n\nThe largest velocity component in absolute value, max_ilvert u_irvert, of the velocity field u in physical representation — an allocation-free reduction used to set the advective-CFL timestep. It differs from the true suplvertboldsymbolurvert by at most sqrt3, a gap absorbed by the conservative recommended_cfl ceiling (whose eigenvalue bound over-estimates the true spectral radius).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables.max_speed"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Observables/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables.energy_spectrum","page":"Observables","text":"energy_spectrum(û, domain)\n\nThe measured kinetic-energy spectrum E(kappa) of the velocity field û (in spectral representation) — the turbulent kinetic energy resolved over wavenumber-magnitude shells, k = sum_kappa E(kappa). Returns (κ, E): the shell-center wavenumber magnitudes and the energy in each shell (brought to the host, an occasional diagnostic). It is the GBGPeriodicFields radial spectrum of û halved — the field reduction sums lverthatboldsymbolurvert^2 while the kinetic energy carries the tfrac12. The domain (a FixedDomain) supplies the wavenumber multipliers. This is the measured counterpart of a Phenomenology model spectrum, for validation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables.energy_spectrum"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Observables/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables.integral_scale","page":"Observables","text":"integral_scale(û, domain)\n\nThe longitudinal integral length scale of the velocity field û, measured from its energy_spectrum E(kappa),\n\nL_11 = frac3pi4 fracint_0^infty E(kappa)kappamathrm dkappaint_0^infty E(kappa)mathrm dkappa\n\nL_11 is the large-eddy scale — the one flow parameter a Phenomenology model spectrum needs that the small scales do not fix. The integrals above are the idealized continuous definition; they are evaluated as sums over the measured energy_spectrum shells (whose per-shell energy makes the shell width cancel in the ratio). The domain (a FixedDomain) supplies the wavenumber multipliers.\n\nFor reference, two related scales follow from the same spectrum. The transverse integral scale is L_22 = L_112 (the incompressible-isotropic relation). The scale defined from the full trace velocity correlation R_ii(r) = langleboldsymbolu(boldsymbolx)cdotboldsymbolu(boldsymbolx+rboldsymbole)rangle (boldsymbole a unit vector) — with no directional projection — is\n\nbeginaligned\nL_E = fracpi2 fracint_0^infty E(kappa)kappamathrm dkappaint_0^infty E(kappa)mathrm dkappa \n    = tfrac23L_11 = tfrac13(L_11 + 2L_22)\nendaligned\n\nthe isotropic average of the one longitudinal and two transverse contributions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables.integral_scale"},{"category":"section","location":"api/State/#State","page":"State","text":"","title":"State"},{"category":"section","location":"api/State/#The-state","page":"State","text":"The evolving fields a run advances, in spectral representation — the single source of truth.","title":"The state"},{"category":"section","location":"api/State/#Physical-field-recovery","page":"State","text":"Recover the velocity, vorticity, and scalar in physical representation from the state in spectral representation (the sources are preserved) — the chart map the observation cache and the nonlinear assemblies consume.","title":"Physical-field recovery"},{"category":"section","location":"api/State/#The-observation-cache","page":"State","text":"Stamped, memoized derived fields with recompute-on-staleness accessors — the sanctioned way for a reader (a diagnostic, a particle push, a statistics callback) to the physical representations the time loop would otherwise compute and discard.","title":"The observation cache"},{"category":"section","location":"api/State/#Random-initial-conditions","page":"State","text":"Seed a large-scale-peaked, dealiased, rescaled random solenoidal velocity, or a mean-zero scalar.","title":"Random initial conditions"},{"category":"module","location":"api/State/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State","page":"State","text":"State\n\nThe evolving state a run advances, its derived views, and how it is seeded:\n\nResolvedFields — the inert record of the evolving velocity and scalar in spectral representation (the single source of truth; the physical fields are derived, never stored).\nvelocity_to_physical!, vorticity_to_physical!, scalar_to_physical! — recover the velocity, vorticity, and scalar in physical representation from the spectral state.\nDerivedFields — the observation cache: stamped, memoized derived fields with recompute-on-staleness accessors.\nphysical_velocity, physical_vorticity — the sanctioned reads of the cached velocity and vorticity in physical representation.\nresolved_fields_modified! — declare an out-of-band state mutation so every cached chart is recomputed on its next read; spectral_scratch — the cache's spectral working space.\nrandom_solenoidal_velocity! and random_scalar! — the random initial conditions (a divergence-free velocity, a mean-zero scalar) that seed it.\n\nAll sized to a FixedDomain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State"},{"category":"type","location":"api/State/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.ResolvedFields","page":"State","text":"ResolvedFields(domain; scalar = false)\n\nThe inert record of the evolving fields a run advances, in spectral representation — the single source of truth, from which the velocity, vorticity, and scalar in physical representation are derived on demand and never stored, so the state cannot go stale. Build it on a FixedDomain; pass scalar = true to carry a passive scalar.\n\nFields\n\nvelocity — the velocity in spectral representation, hatboldsymbolu.\nscalar — the scalar in spectral representation, hatvarphi, or nothing when the run carries no passive scalar.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.ResolvedFields"},{"category":"function","location":"api/State/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.velocity_to_physical!","page":"State","text":"velocity_to_physical!(u, û, domain, spectral_scratch)\n\nRecover the velocity in physical representation into u, from the spectral û (left unchanged), on the FixedDomain domain whose transform plans carry it. The scratch spectral_scratch (a vector field in spectral representation) is the working space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.velocity_to_physical!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/State/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.vorticity_to_physical!","page":"State","text":"vorticity_to_physical!(ω, û, domain, spectral_scratch)\n\nCompute the vorticity boldsymbolomega = nablatimesboldsymbolu in physical representation into ω, from the velocity û in spectral representation, leaving û untouched. The spectral vorticity mathrmiboldsymbolkappatimeshatboldsymbolu is formed in the scratch spectral_scratch (a vector field in spectral representation) and transformed to physical representation by the FixedDomain domain's plans.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.vorticity_to_physical!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/State/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.scalar_to_physical!","page":"State","text":"scalar_to_physical!(φ, φ̂, domain, scalar_scratch)\n\nRecover the scalar in physical representation into φ, from the spectral φ̂ (left unchanged), on the FixedDomain domain whose transform plans carry it. The scratch scalar_scratch (a scalar field in spectral representation) is the working space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.scalar_to_physical!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/State/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.DerivedFields","page":"State","text":"DerivedFields(domain; charts = (:velocity, :vorticity))\n\nThe observation cache of a run — preallocated, stamped, memoized derived fields, giving every reader (a diagnostic, a particle push, a statistics callback) a sanctioned way to the physical representations the time loop would otherwise compute and discard. Each chart slot holds a derived field's values — on the grid lattice, or (for future trajectory-bound charts) at the tracer positions; particle content never enters. An accessor (physical_velocity, physical_vorticity) returns the stored field when it is current for the present state and recomputes it first otherwise, so a stale read is impossible.\n\nBuild the cache on a FixedDomain; charts names the slots to allocate — resident memory is opt-in, and reading a chart that was not declared is an error. The available charts are :velocity (the velocity boldsymbolu in physical representation) and :vorticity (the vorticity boldsymbolomega = nablatimesboldsymbolu in physical representation).\n\nFreshness\n\nThe cache carries one epoch counter per dynamical factor of the run — fields_epoch for the resolved fields (advanced by every completed step!, and by resolved_fields_modified! after an out-of-band mutation) and particles_epoch for the tracers (reserved for trajectory-bound charts). A slot's stamp records the epoch it was computed at; a stamp behind the epoch marks the slot stale, and the accessor recomputes and restamps it on the next read.\n\nFields\n\ncharts — a NamedTuple of the allocated slots; each slot holds the derived field's preallocated storage and its stamp.\nspectral_scratch — the cache's spectral working space (see spectral_scratch).\nfields_epoch, particles_epoch — the per-factor epoch counters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.DerivedFields"},{"category":"function","location":"api/State/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.physical_velocity","page":"State","text":"physical_velocity(derived, state, domain)\nphysical_velocity(sim)\n\nThe velocity boldsymbolu in physical representation, current for the state — the sanctioned read of the derived velocity field. When the DerivedFields derived's :velocity chart is already current (its stamp matches the fields epoch) the stored field is returned as is; otherwise it is first recomputed from the spectral hatboldsymbolu of state (a ResolvedFields) by velocity_to_physical! on the FixedDomain domain, and restamped. Treat the returned field as read-only: it is the cache's storage, shared by every reader until the fields next advance. The second form reads a Simulation's cache, state, and domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.physical_velocity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/State/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.physical_vorticity","page":"State","text":"physical_vorticity(derived, state, domain)\nphysical_vorticity(sim)\n\nThe vorticity boldsymbolomega = nablatimesboldsymbolu in physical representation, current for the state — the sanctioned read of the derived vorticity field, memoized exactly as physical_velocity (recomputed by vorticity_to_physical! when stale, returned as is when current; treat as read-only). The second form reads a Simulation's cache, state, and domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.physical_vorticity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/State/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.resolved_fields_modified!","page":"State","text":"resolved_fields_modified!(derived)\nresolved_fields_modified!(sim)\n\nDeclare that the resolved fields — the spectral hatboldsymbolu, hatvarphi — were changed outside the sanctioned propagators (a re-seeded velocity, any direct write to the state's storage), so every derived chart is stale and will be recomputed on its next read. Advances the cache's fields epoch and returns derived.\n\nThe sanctioned mutators call this for you: step! advances the epoch itself when it completes. A hand-run loop that mutates the spectral state directly must call it — a forgotten call leaves the charts serving the previous fields, the one staleness the cache cannot detect on its own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.resolved_fields_modified!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/State/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.spectral_scratch","page":"State","text":"spectral_scratch(derived)\n\nThe observation cache's working space in spectral representation (a vector field) — the scratch the chart recomputations, and the run layer's spectral reductions, write through. Its contents are undefined between operations: any single operation may use it freely; nothing may store into it across operations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.spectral_scratch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/State/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.random_solenoidal_velocity!","page":"State","text":"random_solenoidal_velocity!(û, domain; kinetic_energy, peak_wavenumber,\n                            spectrum = nothing, rng = default_rng())\n\nFill û (a velocity field in spectral representation) with a random, divergence-free velocity boldsymbolu of a chosen energy spectrum, dealiased and rescaled so the kinetic energy tfrac12langlelvertboldsymbolurvert^2rangle equals kinetic_energy. The Leray–Helmholtz projection mathbbP enforces nablacdotboldsymbolu = 0.\n\nBy default (spectrum = nothing) the velocity is concentrated at large scales by a Gaussian envelope expbig(-lvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2kappa_p^2big) on the mode amplitudes, whose characteristic wavenumber is kappa_p (peak_wavenumber). Because the envelope multiplies the amplitudes, the shell-summed energy spectrum propto kappa^2expbig(-2kappa^2kappa_p^2big) peaks at kappa_psqrt2, not at kappa_p itself (the kappa^2 is the shell's mode count). Pass spectrum, a function kappa mapsto E(kappa) giving a target radial energy spectrum, to seed that shape instead: each mode is scaled by sqrtE(kappa)kappa, so the shell-summed spectrum is propto kappa^2(sqrtEkappa)^2 = E(kappa) (the kappa^2 is the shell's mode count) and the overall amplitude is fixed by the kinetic_energy rescaling — e.g. a von Kármán or Pope form from the Phenomenology submodule's model spectra, which the run layer can wire in for you.\n\nThe domain (a FixedDomain) is what the field is generated on; the rng is the random stream (defaults to Random.default_rng()).\n\nnote: Note\nSeeding into a running case mutates the resolved fields out of band — follow it with resolved_fields_modified! so the derived charts are recomputed on their next read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.random_solenoidal_velocity!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/State/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.random_scalar!","page":"State","text":"random_scalar!(φ̂, domain; scalar_variance, peak_wavenumber,\n               spectrum = nothing, rng = default_rng())\n\nFill φ̂ (the scalar varphi in spectral representation) with a random, mean-zero scalar of a chosen spectrum, dealiased and rescaled so the variance langlevarphi^2rangle equals scalar_variance. The mean vanishes by construction: the envelope is zero at boldsymbolkappa=boldsymbol 0, and that mode's coefficient is the spatial mean langlevarphirangle.\n\nBy default (spectrum = nothing) the scalar is concentrated at large scales by a Gaussian envelope expbig(-lvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2kappa_p^2big) on the mode amplitudes, whose characteristic wavenumber is kappa_p (peak_wavenumber). Because the envelope multiplies the amplitudes, the shell-summed scalar-variance spectrum propto kappa^2expbig(-2kappa^2kappa_p^2big) peaks at kappa_psqrt2, not at kappa_p itself (the kappa^2 is the shell's mode count). Pass spectrum, a function kappa mapsto E_varphi(kappa) giving a target radial scalar-variance spectrum, to seed that shape instead: each mode is scaled by sqrtE_varphi(kappa)kappa (the same construction the velocity uses), so the shell-summed spectrum is propto E_varphi(kappa) and the amplitude is fixed by the scalar_variance rescaling — e.g. an Obukhov–Corrsin form from Phenomenology.\n\nThe domain (a FixedDomain) is what the field is generated on; the rng is the random stream (defaults to Random.default_rng()).\n\nnote: Note\nSeeding into a running case mutates the resolved fields out of band — follow it with resolved_fields_modified! so the derived charts are recomputed on their next read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State.random_scalar!"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Architecture","page":"Architecture","text":"This is the design document for GBGIsotropicTurbulence — what the package is, how it is organized, and why it is shaped the way it is. It is the spec: read it before any structural change, and do not re-litigate a recorded decision (§11) without the user. CLAUDE.md is the terse working copy and defers here; the rendered symbol glossary is docs/src/notation.md.\n\nThe document is self-contained — every symbol, mathematical or code, is introduced in plain language at first use — and reads top to bottom. Mathematics follows the glossary's notation: the wavenumber vector is boldsymbolkappa with magnitude kappa; the turbulent kinetic energy is k; the scalar diffusivity is varkappa; the integrating factor is mathcal E. Pope (2000) is a standard reference for turbulence notation (§References).\n\n","title":"Architecture"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#1.-Identity","page":"Architecture","text":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence is a small, focused pseudospectral direct numerical simulation (DNS) of homogeneous isotropic turbulence: one incompressible velocity field boldsymbolu and one passive scalar varphi on a triple-periodic box, advanced by interchangeable integrating-factor time-stepping, with Lagrangian particle probes and Eulerian + Lagrangian statistics. It is a library of composable pieces with a thin ergonomic run layer on top, built as a consumer of GBGPeriodicFields (the field and spectral machinery) and GBGStreamingStatistics (the statistics).It is not a general PDE or CFD framework, and not multi-physics: the physics is a thin, fixed core — Navier–Stokes plus one passive scalar, no chemistry, phases, or processes. It is not dimensional — control is by Reynolds and Schmidt number, never units. It is not a field library (that is GBGPeriodicFields) or a statistics library (that is GBGStreamingStatistics), and it builds no speculative extension points — no generality ahead of a concrete need (§9).\n\nThe package inverts the richness of its sibling consumer GBGCloudPhysics: where Cloud carries a rich constitutive physics over a thin numerical core, GBGIsotropicTurbulence carries a thin fixed physics over rich, interchangeable numerics and statistics. That inversion is the single fact that explains most of what follows.\n\n","title":"1. Identity"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#2.-Running-a-simulation","page":"Architecture","text":"The primary measure of this library's quality is how easily one sets up and runs a case — including warm-up to a statistically stationary state before any measurement. The headline interface is therefore a thin run layer that assembles the machinery with sensible defaults and owns the time loop:\n\nusing GBGIsotropicTurbulence\n\n# Assemble a forced case. Everything the run needs — the domain, buffers, physics,\n# integrator, state, and initial condition — is built with defaults from a few numbers.\nsim = Simulation(grid_points_per_axis = 128, viscosity = 5e-3;\n                 velocity_forcing = ConstantPower(target = 0.1),\n                 scalar = true, schmidt = 1.0)\n\n# Spin past the transient: advance until the running dissipation ε(t) stops drifting\n# (k(t) is deliberately not used — a fixed-injection forcing like this makes it ring).\nwarmup!(sim)\n\n# Advance and measure. The callback fires on a fixed interval with the current state.\nrun!(sim; turnovers = 20, every = 0.1) do s\n    accumulate_statistics!(my_stats, s)\nend\n\nThe run layer holds the pieces of §4 in one Simulation value, chooses a stable timestep each step from the advective-CFL condition (§4.5), reads the physical fields through the DerivedFields observation cache (§4.1 — recomputed exactly when stale, so nothing must be manually refreshed), and treats warm-up as a first-class verb. By default warmup! advances until the flow is statistically stationary, watching the running dissipation varepsilon(t) and the velocity-derivative skewness S(t) — the small-scale signals that settle last — rather than the kinetic energy a constant-energy forcing clamps flat; a fixed turnover, time, or step budget is the explicit alternative, and §4.7 gives the exact stopping test. A forced flow must reach statistical stationarity before a structure function or a PDF taken over it means anything.\n\nThe composable pieces stay fully exposed: a study that needs to run the loop by hand, swap a stage, or interleave a bespoke measurement drops straight to §4. The run layer is a convenience over that machinery, never a wall in front of it.\n\nStatus. The run layer is implemented (module SimulationRun) and covered by tests; the example above runs as written — a forcing can be named operator-free (ConstantPower(target = 0.1), a DeferredForcing) and warmup! detects stationarity. The API may still evolve, since setup ergonomics is the package's primary quality target, but it is no longer a sketch.\n\n","title":"2. Running a simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#3.-The-organizing-frame","page":"Architecture","text":"A run is fixed by two independent things: what continuous problem you pose, and how you realize it numerically. Beneath both lies a single organizing idea.","title":"3. The organizing frame"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#The-foundation-—-one-dynamics-under-a-ladder-of-projections","page":"Architecture","text":"The architecture is founded on the Mori–Zwanzig decomposition. One dynamics, seen under a ladder of projections, resolves into the four roles every rung shares — State, Generator, Observables, Propagator — and the modules are those roles. The decomposition can bear this weight because it is exact: projecting the full dynamics onto a coarser description is not an approximation but an identity, one that leaves behind an exact memory and an exact noise. What is investigated, never assumed is the closure — the model of that memory. So founding the architecture on Mori–Zwanzig and refusing to assume the physics are one commitment, not two: the framework is exact, and the approximation it demands is precisely the research question.\n\nThe four roles are level-agnostic — a DNS, a large-eddy model, and a moment closure all carry them, differing only in which state each holds and how much memory its generator must model:\n\nthe State — the field the dynamics acts on (§4.1);\nthe Generator — the right-hand side that evolves it, itself split into a reversible and an irreversible part (§4.2);\nthe Observables — the measured functions of the state (§4.3);\nthe Propagator — the map that advances the state through time, realized by a choice of integrating-factor scheme (§4.5).\n\nToday only the finest rung, DNS, is built. The roles are named and carved so the coarser rungs slot in without re-carving (§7), but no generality is built ahead of them.","title":"The foundation — one dynamics under a ladder of projections"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#Specification-and-realization","page":"Architecture","text":"On this foundation, a run is fixed by a specification — the continuous problem — and a realization — the numerics. The specification has two parts:\n\nThe System: the flow scenario — forced homogeneous isotropic turbulence, freely decaying turbulence, uniform-mean-gradient scalar mixing, and (a deferred frame, §7) homogeneous shear. A System is a choice of forcing, initial condition, and the dimensionless parameters mathrmRe and mathrmSc.\nThe Model: the level of description — a rung on the ladder of projections. DNS resolves every active scale; a large-eddy Model resolves the large scales and models the rest; a filtered-density-function or moment Model carries statistics rather than fields. Descending the ladder applies a coarser projection, and the memory it leaves is what the Model's closure must supply.\n\nThe realization is the Method — the numerics — in two layers the package keeps distinct:\n\nA Model-fibered family — the choices that only make sense for a given Model: the time-stepping scheme and its dealiasing for a field Model (§4.5), or the particle pushers and mixing model a statistical Model would need. The Model sets these.\nModel-agnostic axes — precision, memory footprint, and compute backend (§6) — which cut across every Model uniformly.\n\nNaming these apart is why the frame is specification × realization rather than three co-equal axes: the Method is fibered over the Model, not independent of it.","title":"Specification and realization"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#The-comparison-machine","page":"Architecture","text":"Two research disciplines live on these two kinds of variation:\n\nMethod-independence. Vary the realization, hold the specification fixed, and the answer must not change beyond discretization error. Two schemes, two precisions, or two grid sizes for the same DNS must agree; agreement is the correctness signal, and the cheapest realization that still agrees is a finding, not merely a default.\nStatistical commutation. Descend the Model ladder, hold the System fixed, and the coarse Model must reproduce the fine Model's statistics. Filtered DNS against LES of the same flow is the test — a match in distribution, never pathwise (§8), because the coarse state cannot determine its own tendency, and that missing tendency is exactly the Mori–Zwanzig memory. This is the closure question made operational.\n\nBoth disciplines are the same comparison machine — run a configuration against a reference and check chosen physical functionals (spectra, fluxes, conserved quantities, a target statistic) within tolerance — pointed at different axes. Those functionals are exactly the Observables of §4.3.\n\n","title":"The comparison machine"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#4.-Modules","page":"Architecture","text":"Each concern is its own module, included in dependency order by the umbrella src/GBGIsotropicTurbulence.jl. The four Mori–Zwanzig roles carry their role names — State, Generator, Observables, Propagator; the System's forcing, the discretized frame, the Lagrangian instruments, the run layer, and the reference laws are named by their job. The rule is that a role recurring at every rung of the ladder wears its role name, while a concrete mechanism wears its job — so the load-bearing structure is legible at a glance, and no piece of plain machinery is dressed in theory it does not carry.","title":"4. Modules"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#4.1-State-and-SimulationDomain-—-the-field-and-the-frame-it-lives-on","page":"Architecture","text":"Two modules separate what evolves from the frame it evolves on.\n\nSimulationDomain provides the discretized periodic box the run lives on — the reusable, read-only frame. Its concrete type is the FixedDomain, the fixed, unsheared cube, realized by the grid, the real–Fourier layout, the real-to-complex transform plans (one for the velocity, one for the scalar, one for the rank-2 strain tensor the skewness diagnostic needs), the spectral multipliers, the squared-wavenumber array lvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2 (in spectral space, -nabla^2 acts as multiplication by lvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2), and the dealias kernel from the run's chosen dealiasing strategy (the sharp 23 rule by default). The name marks what can vary: a FixedDomain has time-independent, separable wavenumbers, unlike the deferred sheared frame (ShearedDomain, §7). Precision and backend enter here and flow everywhere from here: element_type fixes the real element type (Float32 or Float64) and array_type the storage (Array, CuArray, ROCArray), and every buffer is allocated similar to this frame, so a run is CPU/GPU-agnostic and single- or double-precision by construction.\nState owns the DNS realization of the Mori–Zwanzig State role. ResolvedFields is the inert record of the evolving spectral fields — the velocity hatboldsymbolu and, when the run carries one, the scalar hatvarphi — and the single source of truth: the physical velocity, vorticity, and scalar are functions of it, never independent state. No operation hangs on it; the generator advances it and the observables read it, both as external functions of (hatboldsymbolu hatvarphi) — the reification the Mori–Zwanzig lens demands, kept a bare carrier so the arrows stay primary (§11 decision 26). The chart map — the recoveries velocity_to_physical!, vorticity_to_physical!, scalar_to_physical! that evaluate those functions — is homed here with the state it reads, and DerivedFields memoizes its values: an observation cache of stamped chart slots (opt-in, an undeclared chart is an error) with one epoch counter per dynamical factor of the run (fields, particles). An accessor (physical_velocity, physical_vorticity) returns the stored field when its stamp matches the fields epoch and recomputes it first otherwise, so a stale read is impossible; step! advances the epoch as it completes, and an out-of-band mutation of the spectral state is declared through resolved_fields_modified! — the one freshness event the cache cannot detect itself (§11 decision 30). Its initial conditions seed the state: random_solenoidal_velocity! fills a divergence-free velocity with a large-scale spectral envelope, rescaled to a target k, and random_scalar! fills a mean-zero scalar to a target variance. The per-step scratch is not state: the scheme's registers live with the integrator (IntegratorBuffers in Propagator, §4.5) and the assembly scratch with the generator (ResolvedGenerator, §4.2), each preallocated so a step allocates no field-sized memory (§9, invariant 4) and sized to the run (no scalar buffers when the run carries no scalar; GPU memory is tight). A Simulation composes the ResolvedFields and its DerivedFields, and the time loop advances the state in place.","title":"4.1 State and SimulationDomain — the field and the frame it lives on"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#4.2-Generator-—-the-evolution,-split-reversible-and-irreversible","page":"Architecture","text":"Generator realizes the Mori–Zwanzig generator: the right-hand side the propagator advances. Its physical inputs are bundled in FlowConditions — the viscosity nu (mathrmRe = 1nu), the Schmidt number mathrmSc (scalar diffusivity varkappa = numathrmSc), and the two forcings; nu and mathrmSc set the diffusion, the forcings the source, and folding this bundle into Generator is what adds the Generator → Forcing dependency. It carries a metriplectic seam — a reversible part that conserves energy and an irreversible part that dissipates it (§8) — and the numerical split is built to honor it. The incompressible momentum equation is advanced in rotational (Lamb-vector) form: writing the convective term through the identity (boldsymbolucdotnabla)boldsymbolu = nabla(tfrac12lvertboldsymbolurvert^2) - boldsymbolutimesboldsymbolomega (with vorticity boldsymbolomega = nablatimesboldsymbolu) and letting the Leray–Helmholtz projection mathbb P onto divergence-free fields absorb the pressure and the kinetic-energy gradient, the equation becomes\n\npartial_tboldsymbolu = mathbb P (boldsymbolutimesboldsymbolomega) + nunabla^2boldsymbolu + boldsymbolf \n\nIts three terms are the seam made explicit, and the time-stepping treats each according to its nature:\n\nThe reversible term. The recoveries (velocity_to_physical!, vorticity_to_physical!, homed with the state, §4.1) produce the physical velocity and vorticity from hatboldsymbolu; velocity_rhs! then assembles mathbb P (boldsymbolutimesboldsymbolomega), dealiased by the 23 rule to remove the aliasing of the quadratic product (the pseudospectral method; Orszag & Patterson 1972, the 23 rule Orszag 1971). Rotational form is the cheap, energy-conserving way to compute the advection spectrally, and incompressible Euler is genuinely Hamiltonian — so this term is the flow's reversible, energy-conserving generator, stepped explicitly.\nThe irreversible term. The diffusion nunabla^2boldsymbolu is the flow's irreversible, net-dissipative generator. Being diagonal in Fourier, it is integrated exactly: the factor pair velocity_factor! / scalar_factor! builds the flow map mathcal E(boldsymbolkappa) = expbig((A(boldsymbolkappa) - Dlvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2) Delta tbig) — the exact propagator of the linear part over a step, with diffusivity D = nu for the velocity and D = varkappa = numathrmSc for the scalar, and A(boldsymbolkappa) an optional linear forcing rate (the source, below) — and multiplying a field by mathcal E advances its diffusion with no stability restriction. mathcal E is a propagator, a flow map over a step, not a generator; keeping the two apart is exactly the Generator/Propagator distinction of §4.5.\nThe source. boldsymbolf is the forcing, an external drive that is neither seam half (§4.4). When a forcing is linear in the field (boldsymbolf = A boldsymbolu), its rate A(boldsymbolkappa) is diagonal and linear like the diffusion, so it folds into the same exponential mathcal E for exactness — and there it injects energy, mathcal E  1 wherever A  Dlvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2. So mathcal E cross-cuts the seam: it carries the irreversible diffusion and, when the drive is linear, the source. The seam is a decomposition of the right-hand side, not of mathcal E.\n\nThe passive scalar rides the same generator: scalar_to_physical! transforms hatvarphi to the physical varphi at each stage, and scalar_rhs! assembles the advection in conservative form -nablacdot(boldsymboluvarphi) (equal to -boldsymbolucdotnablavarphi since nablacdotboldsymbolu = 0); the conservative form conserves the scalar mean exactly under the 23 rule.\n\nThe metriplectic reading is a lens, not a literal thermodynamics: incompressible isothermal flow carries no genuine entropy — the dissipated kinetic energy simply leaves the model as untracked heat — so the load-bearing content is the reversible/irreversible split of the right-hand side and the constraint it puts on any closure: net dissipation lives in the diffusion term alone, so a subgrid model may only remove energy through that half (§8).\n\nThe generator is reified: ResolvedGenerator bundles the FlowConditions with the assembly scratch its evaluations write through (the quadratic-product fields, the PhaseShift displaced-evaluation buffers, the per-mode forcing-rate arrays), behind a six-verb protocol — prepare! opens the step (advances each forcing's internal state, arms the linear rates), velocity_rhs! / scalar_rhs! evaluate each field's explicit right-hand side at a stage state (dealiased nonlinear term plus additive forcing source), velocity_factor! / scalar_factor! build each field's exact linear subflow, and finalize! closes the step (the post-step forcing projections). velocity_rhs! and velocity_factor! are the required core; prepare! and finalize! default to no-ops. A scheme consumes the physics only through this protocol, so it can never assemble the right-hand side from a mismatched intermediate — the class of the stage-ordering bug that motivated the reification (§11 decisions 29, 31).","title":"4.2 Generator — the evolution, split reversible and irreversible"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#4.3-Observables-—-the-measured-functions-of-the-state","page":"Architecture","text":"Observables realizes the Mori–Zwanzig observables: the functions of the state that are measured. Each states the physical quantity first, then the reduction that computes it:\n\nkinetic_energy k = tfrac12langlelvertboldsymbolurvert^2rangle and dissipation varepsilon = nulanglelvertboldsymbolomegarvert^2rangle;\nscalar_variance langlevarphi^2rangle and scalar_dissipation varepsilon_varphi = 2varkappalanglelvertnablavarphirvert^2rangle;\nmax_speed, which sets the advective-CFL;\nderivative_skewness, the K41 marker S approx -05 of developed turbulence, computed coordinate-free as the normalized third invariant of the strain rate boldsymbolS, 3ClangledetboldsymbolSranglelangleboldsymbolSboldsymbolSrangle^32 with the exact isotropy constant C = tfrac47sqrt152 (Betchov 1956; reproduces the classical longitudinal skewness);\nenergy_spectrum E(kappa), the halved GBGPeriodicFields radial spectrum, and integral_scale derived from it.\n\nThese are not incidental: they are the fitness functionals the comparison machine of §3 evaluates, and the stationarity signals warmup! watches. They are also the measured half of the validation harness whose predicted half is Phenomenology (§4.8).","title":"4.3 Observables — the measured functions of the state"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#4.4-Forcing-—-the-source","page":"Architecture","text":"Forcing is the System's energy-injection strategy — the source term boldsymbolf of §4.2, and the one deliberately designed-in extension point. AbstractForcing is the supertype; a forcing enters the step through a small interface whose four methods each default to doing nothing:\n\nset_forcing_rate! — for a forcing linear in the field (boldsymbolf = A boldsymbolu): fill the per-mode rate A(boldsymbolkappa), folded into the integrating factor (exact, order-preserving);\nadd_forcing_source! — add an additive source at each Runge–Kutta stage;\nprepare_forcing! — advance internal state once per step (a stochastic process);\napply_forcing! — apply a post-step projection or rescale.\n\nThe four hooks form one per-step lifecycle with a fixed order — prepare_forcing! and set_forcing_rate! before the stages, add_forcing_source! at every stage, apply_forcing! after the completed step — and the rate and the source are two channels of one forcing term (the exactly linear part folded into mathcal E, the remainder added at stages). A strategy owns whatever scratch its hooks need; none receives the integrator's buffers (§11 decision 32).\n\nNoForcing (free decay), BandRescaling (hold a band's or the total energy fixed — a deterministic clamp, fit for spin-up and amplitude-holding but not a faithful stationary sampler; its docstring carries the Berendsen-class caveat, Bussi, Donadio & Parrinello 2007), Linear (fixed-rate negative damping, Lundgren 2003), and ConstantPower (its rate reset each step to hold the injection rate) — the two linear forcings folded into mathcal E — are provided; a stochastic Eswaran–Pope Ornstein–Uhlenbeck forcing is archived (§7). One implementation serves both the velocity (a vector field) and the scalar (a scalar field), because these four methods are written through tensor-order-generic field operators — the only field-specific step is the solenoidal (Leray) projection, applied to the velocity alone. Each forcing has an operator-taking constructor and an operator-free keyword form (ConstantPower(target = 0.1), a DeferredForcing) materialized when Simulation builds the frame, so a case can name its forcing before the grid exists.","title":"4.4 Forcing — the source"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#4.5-Propagator-—-the-map-that-advances-the-state-in-time","page":"Architecture","text":"Propagator realizes the Mori–Zwanzig propagator: the map that carries the state forward in time, built on the generator's reversible/irreversible seam. Its numerical schemes are the Model-fibered part of the Method (§3).\n\nAbstractIntegrator is the family of integrating-factor schemes dispatched by step!, which advances the spectral velocity hatboldsymbolu and scalar hatvarphi together through the same Runge–Kutta stages. Coupled stepping is deliberate: the scalar is passive, so time-accuracy needs both nonlinear terms evaluated at each stage's state; advancing the velocity fully and the scalar afterward would drop the substage velocities and degrade the coupling to first order.\nIFLowStorageRK3 (the Williamson 2N / RKW3 workhorse) is third-order and imaginary-axis stable; IFRK2 (Heun) is a cheaper two-stage option that is imaginary-axis unstable and leans on viscous damping. The recommended_cfl helper gives each scheme its conservative advective-CFL ceiling C (for Delta t = C Delta xmax_ilvert u_irvert), which the run layer uses with max_speed to choose Delta t.\nIntegratorBuffers is the scheme's own per-step scratch — the Runge–Kutta stage registers, the factor register, and the substage recovery pair — built by allocate_buffers dispatching on the concrete scheme, so each allocates exactly its working set. The right-hand-side assembly scratch lives with the generator on the ResolvedGenerator (§4.2), and the start-of-step physical fields are the observation cache's charts (§4.1): a step! is stage arithmetic over the generator protocol and the scheme's registers, nothing more. None of this scratch is state (§11 decisions 26, 30, 31).","title":"4.5 Propagator — the map that advances the state in time"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#4.6-Particles-—-Lagrangian-instruments","page":"Architecture","text":"Particles are instruments — probes of the turbulence — not a second physics. A particle is a state-carrying trajectory object: today a tracer (position boldsymbolx); the deferred ladder is inertial (boldsymbolx boldsymbolv) and oriented (boldsymbolx boldsymbolv boldsymbolL) probes (§7). Tracers holds positions and a chosen interpolation scheme; the push is Heun-in-time on the velocity at the start and end of the step (the fields the solver already produces), split across record_trajectory_velocity! (before the step) and advect! (after it); sample! reads any field along the trajectories for Lagrangian statistics.\n\nThe split is what keeps an M-point instrument at M-point cost. A run holds one physical velocity field — the observation cache's chart, recomputed in place once the state advances — so a single-call push would need the start-of-step field retained, an N^3times3 resident buffer and an N^3times3 copy per step to serve an Mtimes3 interpolation. Interpolating onto the tracers before the step instead records the same information in the Mtimes3 buffer the tracer set already owns. Tracked and untracked runs therefore have identical field-scale residency and per-step traffic — the zero both audits now assert.\n\nTheir numerics are first-class, on the same footing as the field schemes. Two axes matter: the trajectory integrator — today a single second-order Heun push, with higher-order pushers the studied future axis — and the sampling-consistency axis, the interpolation scheme's order relative to the field's spectral accuracy (CatmullRom by default, higher-order LalescuSpline / LagrangeSpline available). When the inertial and oriented probes arrive, the integrating-factor split carries over for the translational stiff drag (field viscosity and a particle's linear or matrix drag are the same linear-exact move), while orientation dynamics on the rotation group is a distinct geometric integrator — the reversible generator of §8 again — so the split does not naively unify all of it.","title":"4.6 Particles — Lagrangian instruments"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#4.7-SimulationRun-—-the-front-door","page":"Architecture","text":"A Simulation value bundles what a case advances: the FixedDomain, the ResolvedFields state (the spectral fields hatboldsymbolu and hatvarphi the generator acts on), the DerivedFields observation cache, the ResolvedGenerator (carrying the FlowConditions), the IntegratorBuffers scheme registers, the chosen AbstractIntegrator, any Tracers, a stored CFL ceiling, and a clock. Over it:\n\nSimulation(...) — the assemble-with-defaults constructor: from a grid size and a forcing it builds every piece of §4.1–§4.6, seeds a divergence-free initial condition, and picks the default scheme. The viscosity is set from the grid by default — the highest Reynolds number consistent with the chosen resolution criterion k_max_η (decision 20) — with an explicit viscosity the opt-in override. Setup is a few numbers.\nadvance!(sim; dt) and run!(sim; ...) own the time loop: choose Delta t from the scheme's CFL and the current max_speed (read through the velocity chart), record the start-of-step velocity along any trajectories, step!, push the tracers on the two endpoint velocities the accessors serve, and fire a user callback on a fixed time interval.\nwarmup!(sim; ...) advances to statistical stationarity — by default until the running dissipation varepsilon(t) and the velocity-derivative skewness S(t) have both plateaued (a trailing-window flatness test), reading varepsilon rather than the clampable k(t), and, when the scalar is forced, until langlevarphi^2rangle settles too; or a fixed turnover, time, or step budget.\ncurrent_time(sim) and current_step(sim) report the clock, and the observables applied to sim.state (e.g. kinetic_energy(sim.state.velocity), dissipation on the recovered vorticity) the current-state diagnostics, for a callback to read.\n\nThe layer wires §4.1–§4.6 together and holds no physics or numerics of its own.","title":"4.7 SimulationRun — the front door"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#4.8-Phenomenology-—-the-reference-laws-(extraction-bound)","page":"Architecture","text":"Phenomenology is the library of reference turbulence phenomenology: the fitted scalings, model spectra, structure-function laws, and dimensionless constants that theory and experiment have established. It is the symmetric counterpart to Observables — those are the measured functions of the DNS state; these are the predicted values of the same functions. The DNS measures, the laws predict, and the comparison machine of §3 sets them side by side. Two properties follow, both instances of the charter investigate the physics, do not assume it:\n\nA provider, not a comparer. A law evaluates itself from the flow parameters; it knows nothing of the DNS. The comparison lives consumer-side (the validation layer), which reads both.\nNo parametrization is privileged. A published fit and one of our own are the same kind of object — subtypes of one law family, discovered and validated alike, never baked in as truth.\n\nThe abstraction is the strategy pattern already used for AbstractForcing / AbstractIntegrator / DealiasingStrategy: a law family is an abstract type, each parametrization a named preset — usually a concrete subtype carrying its fitted coefficients and provenance, or (for the composed velocity spectra) a factory function that assembles a ModelSpectrum — implementing one small interface: evaluate the law at its argument (kappa, r, mathrmRe_lambda) given a FlowParameters bundle; report its provenance (the citation and validity range); and eventually fit itself to data. Choosing a law is choosing the preset (Pope2000 versus VonKarman1948) and optionally overriding its coefficients (Pope2000(; C_K = 1.6), the published fit being the default). Provenance is first-class — the comparison needs a law's validity range to be fair, and it is the single source of the citation.\n\nNaming is a three-level handle, because one author publishes many models across years and quantities: the quantity is the submodule (Spectra.Pope… versus StructureFunctions.Pope…), the paper is the author-year handle (Pope2000, first author's surname, the full list in provenance), and a same author-year-quantity collision takes a trailing letter (Pope2014a, Pope2014b). The validating case would be Kolmogorov 1941 across two submodules — StructureFunctions.Kolmogorov1941 (the 2/3 and 4/5 laws) and a spectrum counterpart Spectra.Kolmogorov1941, were one added — separated by the submodule alone. The full handle Phenomenology.Spectra.Pope2000 reads like a citation and is unique by construction.\n\nSubmodules are cut by representation, so they stay few while the models multiply as subtypes:\n\nSpectra — functions of kappa (velocity E(kappa), scalar E_varphi(kappa)), composed: an inertial core C_K varepsilon^23kappa^-53 times a swappable large-scale factor f_L(kappa L_11) and dissipation-range factor f_eta(kappaeta), a named preset fixing a published triple.\nStructureFunctions — functions of r (the D_Ldots(r) laws and, sharing the representation, any two-point correlation law when one is added).\nScalings — the dimensionless numbers and their mathrmRe_lambda trends: the Kolmogorov constant C_K (Sreenivasan1995), the normalized dissipation constant C_varepsilon (Sreenivasan1998), and the box-normalized dissipation group k(Lvarepsilon)^23 (Li2008, which the run layer inverts to set the viscosity). The derivative skewness, the flatness, the Lagrangian C_0, and the mechanical-to-scalar timescale ratio are forward-looking additions to the family, not yet built.\n\nThree exist now — enough for the validation checklist — and the count caps near six: PDFs, Decay (k sim t^-n), and Lagrangian are named future seams (§7), not owed. A single FlowParameters bundle carries the quantities a law reads (varepsilon, nu, L_11, u, mathrmSc, varepsilon_varphi), with the derived scales (eta, tau_eta, lambda, mathrmRe_lambda, varkappa) as helpers — the analogue of the FlowConditions and FixedDomain bundles.\n\nThe laws are consumed to build runs, not only compared against: an initial-condition or forcing spectrum drawn from Spectra, and the reference curves the validation harness reads, all from one library. And the loop closes: we measure (with this package and SS), fit a parametrization to that measurement, and the new fit lands beside the literature, to be validated against the next run.\n\nFormula verification. A transcribed law is checked not against a reference implementation — none is authoritative, and cross-checking against another code merely moves the trust — but against the physical relations it must satisfy: the asymptotic slopes (the kappa^-53 inertial range, the low-wavenumber kappa^sigma, the dissipation cutoff), the zeta_3 = 1 constraint on every intermittency model, and the roll-off limits f_L f_eta to 1. This self-consistency battery lives in the test suite and catches a wrong exponent, coefficient, or sign without trusting a second source.\n\nExtraction requirement (recorded). Phenomenology is built inside this package for now but is designed to be cut into a standalone GBGTurbulencePhenomenology with no rewrite — the demand-driven path that extracted GBGStreamingStatistics. The requirement constrains the design. It is a dependency-light leaf, depending on neither GBGPeriodicFields (a law is a scalar function, no field math) nor GBGStreamingStatistics (it provides reference values, computes no statistics) nor this package's dynamics — first in the include order, importing nothing turbulence-dynamical. And it is a self-contained namespace: the umbrella does not re-export it (using GBGIsotropicTurbulence brings the DNS API only; the laws are reached opt-in through Phenomenology), and its notation and API pages form their own group, separable from the DNS surface. Extract when a second consumer appears; until then the seam is real but uncrossed (decision 18).","title":"4.8 Phenomenology — the reference laws (extraction-bound)"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#4.9-Layout-summary","page":"Architecture","text":"SimulationDomain → State → Forcing → Generator → Observables → Propagator → Particles → SimulationRun. Cross-module access is through explicit using ..Module: name lists; Phenomenology (§4.8) leads this order as the dependency-light leaf the initial-condition, forcing, and validation consumers read. Statistics live consumer-side of this package too: the sampling in Particles and the observables in Observables produce the samples, and a statistics bridge that streams them to GBGStreamingStatistics is deferred (§7).\n\n","title":"4.9 Layout summary"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#5.-The-consumer-boundary-—-what-belongs-here,-and-what-does-not","page":"Architecture","text":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence owns the turbulence-specific glue and delegates everything domain-neutral. The dividing line is domain-neutrality, not \"thinness\":\n\nTo GBGPeriodicFields — every field operation: the transforms, curl! / cross! / divergence!, the Leray–Helmholtz projection helmholtz_projection! (mathbb P), convolve! and the dealias/band kernels, the reductions (inner, the radial spectrum and correlation), and grid↔point interpolation. The rule everything diagonal in Fourier is a GBGPeriodicFields operator is followed in the code.\nTo GBGStreamingStatistics — every estimator: histograms, densities, conditional means and variances, conditional and transition densities.\nOwned here — the Navier–Stokes and scalar nonlinear assembly, the integrating factor, the forcing strategies, the coupled time-stepping, the particle tracking, the run layer, and the turbulence-specific spectral glue a backend must not carry (the deferred sheared frame's non-separable multipliers, §7).\n\nTwo standing rules follow:\n\nIf a capability's true home is GBGPeriodicFields or GBGStreamingStatistics, raise it and extend it there — do not reimplement it here. Only genuinely domain-neutral capability migrates out; a backend has an anti-accretion charter, so turbulence-specific glue stays here.\nA deferred-by-design future is not a missing feature (§7).\n\n","title":"5. The consumer boundary — what belongs here, and what does not"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#6.-Precision-and-footprint-as-studied-axes","page":"Architecture","text":"Precision and the memory-footprint/accuracy trade-off are objects of study, not fixed defaults — the numerical counterpart of the charter that the tool investigates the physics rather than assuming it (§8).\n\nBoth precisions are supported equally, with no privileged default. Float32 is the development and desktop precision; Float64 is the production precision. The element type T flows from the frame (§4.1): no kernel silently fixes the arithmetic. (The FlowConditions scalar parameters are stored Float64 and converted to T at use — a scalar convenience, not a field commitment.) The one computational exception is statistics: reductions and online moments aggregate in Float64 even for a Float32 field, since summing many single-precision values loses accuracy — precision flows from the frame where the field is stored and stepped, never where it is accumulated.\nThe footprint/accuracy trade-off is held at both ends — low-storage integrators, the dealias fraction, truncation — and the working point is chosen by direct fitness test, never decreed. Which precision, order, or footprint suffices for a regime is a measured finding.\nThe fitness arbiter is the comparison machine of §3 — the same one that adjudicates method-independence and DNS-versus-LES statistical commutation. One machine ranges over model fidelity, integrator, precision, footprint, and closure; the sufficiency frontier (the cheapest realization that still passes) is a deliverable of the tool.\n\nThis is why the GBGPeriodicFields element-type genericity and GPU-first, allocation-free discipline are load-bearing here: they are what make precision and backend free to vary, and they are what keep the state a compile-time product of concretely-typed buffers (§9, invariant 4). \"Low footprint\" is the default working point on a studied axis, not a universal law.\n\n","title":"6. Precision and footprint as studied axes"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#7.-Deliberately-deferred","page":"Architecture","text":"These are named so they are not precluded, and deliberately left un-abstracted: no extension point or generalization is built for them until a second concrete case forces the abstraction. None is a gap.\n\nStochastic (Eswaran–Pope OU) forcing — StochasticOU, a band-limited Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process added to the RHS. Built and validated, then archived from the public surface (un-exported, undocumented) to be re-added; it owns its noise scratch (decision 32), and its host-side random increment is the one allocation-free exception (invariant 4), so the re-add is gated on a GPU-tuned generator (Eswaran & Pope 1988).\nInertial and oriented particles — (boldsymbolxboldsymbolv) with matrix drag, then (boldsymbolxboldsymbolvboldsymbolL) with torque. The translational drag reuses the integrating-factor split; orientation needs a geometric integrator (§4.6, §8).\nThe sheared frame (Rogallo), a ShearedDomain. A uniform mean velocity gradient makes the wavenumbers time-dependent (mathrmdboldsymbolkappamathrmdt = -boldsymbolA^topboldsymbolkappa) and non-separable — the SimulationDomain counterpart to FixedDomain (§4.1). This is the one future with a concrete GBGPeriodicFields dependency: its separable per-axis multipliers cannot hold a sheared lattice, so it is a bounded extension there (rule 1 of §5), not a job here.\nThe Model ladder beyond DNS — a large-eddy Model (filter the DNS, model the subgrid stress) and a filtered-density-function or moment Model (§8). Each is a coarser projection on the ladder of §3, and the four roles are already carved to receive it — but, per invariant 9, no generality is built until a second rung forces it. Each is discriminated from DNS by statistical commutation; the canonical acceptance test is the relaxation of a two-mode scalar distribution (a micromixing benchmark). The natural first spectral rung is a Chollet–Lesieur eddy viscosity nu_e(kappa  kappa_c) at the truncation wavenumber kappa_c (Chollet & Lesieur 1981) — architecturally a per-mode linear rate, a negative linear-forcing citizen folded into the integrating factor exactly as the armed forcing rate is, with the plateau–cusp profile resident and only its sqrtE(kappa_c)kappa_c scale updated per step from one shell reduction. The Hou–Li filter at nu = 0 is its crude implicit sibling (numerical-dissipation LES), with the known cutoff-bottleneck caveat.\nThe statistics bridge — the sampling-to-GBGStreamingStatistics path (Eulerian field samples and Lagrangian trajectory samples in, streamed summaries out).\nAlternative dealiasing and nonlinear forms (partly built). DealiasingStrategy now owns the dealiased nonlinear assembly — hatboldsymbolu (and hatvarphi) in, the dealiased term out — so a strategy may restructure how the product is computed, not merely filter its output. The sharp 23 rule and the smooth Hou–Li filter kernel-multiply the single realization; PhaseShift (decision 27) averages a half-cell-displaced evaluation to cancel aliasing and truncates spherically to tfracsqrt23N, at 2times the transforms. Still deferred siblings of the same assembly: 32 zero-padding, and the skew-symmetric momentum form (which reorganizes the quadratic product to conserve energy in the discrete equations). The assembly touches only the Generator and its scratch, never the State (§4.1); the rotational form + spherical 23 stays the lean default.\nA temporal-accuracy step control. The step is set today by the advective CFL — a stability-calibrated, conservative ceiling with a manual cfl override — and nothing adapts it to a target temporal accuracy. An embedded-error or PI step controller, distinct in purpose from the stability CFL, would make temporal accuracy a studied fitness axis beside precision and footprint (§5). Deferred: HIT runs are typically stability-limited near the CFL ceiling, so a fixed step at the CFL is rarely the accuracy bottleneck.\nUser-selectable FFT planner effort. GBGPeriodicFields lets each transform plan carry a backend planner flag and time limit — the fft_flags and fft_timelimit keywords of FieldTransformPlans — yet FixedDomain (§4.1) builds its velocity, scalar, and tensor plans without them, so every run takes the planner default (FFTW.ESTIMATE on the CPU path) and neither the domain nor the Simulation front door exposes an override. Closing it is a two-keyword passthrough, deferred on cost/benefit: the transform is the dominant per-step cost, so a one-time FFTW.MEASURE / FFTW.PATIENT planning search is a genuine performance lever beside the precision and footprint axes (§5) — but that search is an FFTW planning concept that pays off on the CPU path, IT's development-and-small-run platform, not the GPU-primary production target (invariant 4), where the plan is effectively fixed. It is wired when a long CPU campaign makes the search worth the added front-door surface — named for the planning-effort choice a user makes, not the raw backend flag.\nThe nu = 0 truncated-Euler experiment — an integrator and dealiasing oracle. At zero viscosity (accepted today; the integrating factor degenerates to the identity) the sharp-masked assembly is the exact Galerkin truncation of the Euler equations, which conserves kinetic energy in continuous time — so measured energy drift isolates the time integrator alone: a per-scheme conservation and order-of-accuracy test (the drift must scale with the scheme's order), a second witness that the sharp strategies are exactly Galerkin (an alias residue breaks the detailed conservation of the retained triads), and a direct measurement of the Hou–Li filter's effective dissipation (the one deliberately non-conservative strategy). The long run is a validation target of its own: the truncated system thermalizes toward absolute equilibrium — an equipartition E(kappa) propto kappa^2 tail growing from the cutoff, the thermalized tail acting on the large scales like an eddy viscosity while it grows (Cichowlas et al. 2005). It is not a turbulence run — the dissipation-based diagnostics degenerate (varepsilon to 0, mathrmRe_lambda undefined) — and it is deferred as a test, not a capability.\n\n","title":"7. Deliberately deferred"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#8.-Lenses-and-prior-art","page":"Architecture","text":"The package makes real theory legible. One framework — Mori–Zwanzig — is its foundation (§3); the rest are lenses read through it. Nothing in the numerics is load-bearing on the subordinate lenses — the arithmetic would be identical had they never been named — but the architecture is founded on the Mori–Zwanzig decomposition, which is exact and so can carry that weight. The lenses organize understanding and set what the tool is built to investigate, not assume.\n\nMori–Zwanzig — the foundation, elaborated. §3 states the decomposition; here is its machinery. A projection mathcal P splits the full state into a resolved part and the rest, and applying it to the exact dynamics gives the resolved variables a generalized Langevin equation: a streaming term (the projected drift), a memory integral over a kernel K(t-s) carrying the history the projection discarded, and a colored-noise term living in the unresolved subspace. A Model is a choice of mathcal P and a closure that approximates that kernel and that noise; DNS is the identity projection, for which both vanish, so it needs no closure. The memory kernel K is the precise content of the tendency a coarse state cannot supply on its own (§3), and modeling it is the closure problem the tool exists to test.\nOpen systems / non-equilibrium — a reading of the projection. The Mori–Zwanzig cut is the open-system system/environment split: the resolved variables are the system, the projected-out variables the environment, which survives only as the memory + noise above. A driven-dissipative flow is an open system in a non-equilibrium steady state — energy injected by the forcing (an energy reservoir, the source of §4.2), dissipated to the unmodeled viscous bath (the sink), balanced at stationarity. The lens carries its own caveat: whatever a model retains of the environment — a stochastic forcing's internal state, a closure's subgrid degrees of freedom — is not the environment but the finite Markovian surrogate kept on the system's own side, a modeled degree of freedom; so it homes, when built, as a modeled slot on the one State, never a separate \"environment state\".\nThe commuting square — the modeling ideal. With Pi a coarse-graining and S a flow map, one would like Picirc S_textfine = S_textcoarsecircPi. It cannot hold pathwise: the coarse state does not determine its own tendency (that is the memory and noise made concrete), and turbulence is chaotic. The achievable ideal — the design target of any Model — is statistical commutation, agreement of the distributions and fluxes on the attractor, exactly what the DNS-versus-LES comparison of §3 measures.\nGeometric mechanics (metriplectic / GENERIC) — the reversible/irreversible split. The integrating-factor method is a split into a reversible generator (the advection, in rotational form, §4.2) and an irreversible one (the diffusion, integrated exactly). Read as a metriplectic system — a Hamiltonian (symplectic) bracket generating the reversible part, a dissipative (metric) bracket the irreversible — the lens says why the split is structure-preserving and constrains every closure to be net-dissipative. Here it is a lens rather than a literal thermodynamics: isothermal incompressible flow has no genuine entropy (§4.2). The term is Morrison's (metric + symplectic); Öttinger's GENERIC is the same structure.\nRenormalization / scale invariance — a hypothesis, never a foundation. Small-scale universality is what would make closure constants transferable, but it is approximate (intermittency), it fails for scalar small scales (persistent gradient anisotropy), and the unclosed terms are conditional quantities that re-import the large scales. So the tool is built to test how universal a closure is, per regime, not to assume it. This is the general charter: the architecture investigates the physics; it must not assume it — the same discipline as the model-free statistics it consumes.\nThe skew-product tower — what a Simulation is. The composite system factors as a tower t to forcing noise to fields to particles: the generator is block lower-triangular (each row reads only itself and the rows above — the fields never read the tracer positions, the clock reads nothing), so the flow is a skew product and advance! is its Gauss–Seidel sweep in topological order (step the fields, refresh the observation, push the tracers, tick the clock). Triangularity is what licenses the composed propagator — splitting error cannot propagate up the triangle — and it gives every state slot a litmus: the restart record holds exactly what the flow map needs (hatboldsymbolu, hatvarphi; the tracer positions; a stochastic forcing's internal state — the extended-phase-space discipline of molecular dynamics' thermostats); the observation cache holds spent compute, reconstructable from the record (delete it and the trajectory is unchanged); and the clock and output cursors are the experimenter's logbook. The DerivedFields charts are the tower's algebraic variables — solved, never integrated — and their per-factor epochs inherit the triangle: a chart is stale against exactly the factors it reads.\n\n","title":"8. Lenses and prior art"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#9.-Invariants-—-the-deliberate-absences","page":"Architecture","text":"The public surface and these absences are the spec. Do not add a capability \"for completeness\"; a missing one usually encodes a decision.\n\nNo units. Control is by mathrmRe (through nu) and mathrmSc; every diagnostic is dimensionless.\nNo field or statistics machinery reimplemented. Field operations come from GBGPeriodicFields, estimators from GBGStreamingStatistics (§5).\nNo privileged precision or backend. T and the array type flow from the frame; both precisions and CPU/GPU are first-class (§6).\nAllocation-free stepping over a compile-time state — with an audited footprint. A time step allocates no field-sized memory over the preallocated IntegratorBuffers — load-bearing for GPU hot loops. The evolving state is a compile-time product of concretely-typed buffers (a parametrized record the compiler specializes), never a runtime-heterogeneous container — the discipline that keeps the step type-stable and device-generic, and that lets the state grow (a scalar today, a coarser Model's extra fields tomorrow) to more slots at no runtime cost. Each scheme allocates exactly its working set: allocate_buffers dispatches on the concrete integrator and builds only the registers that scheme writes — there is no over-allocating ::AbstractIntegrator fallback, so a scheme with no method is a hard error, never a silent superset (an IFLowStorageRK3 run does not carry IFRK2's two predictor registers).\nGPU-first, single-node, backend-agnostic. Shared-memory only; no distributed or multi-GPU scope (inherited from GBGPeriodicFields).\nNo baked-in physical model, closure, or universality assumption. The tool investigates these; it does not assume them (§8). The Mori–Zwanzig foundation is exempt because it is exact, not a model — a projection is an identity, and what remains modeled is the closure the tool is built to test (§3).\nA library, not a framework. The run layer is a convenience over exposed pieces (§2, §4.7), never a mandatory shell.\nThe scalar is denoted varphi, never theta — in code, mathematics, and prose.\nDeferred futures are accounted for, not owed (§7); no speculative extension points.\n\n","title":"9. Invariants — the deliberate absences"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#10.-Conventions","page":"Architecture","text":"Docstrings are operator-first, then scheme. State the continuous operator or PDE term and its exact/ideal form, then the discrete realization — as velocity_factor! leads with partial_t f = Dnabla^2 f before mathcal E(boldsymbolkappa), and velocity_rhs! with the Lamb-vector identity before the pseudospectral product. This is the turbulence analogue of the siblings' physical-form-first / estimand-first rules.\nCoordinate-free where a coordinate-free form exists. Homogeneous isotropic turbulence is statistically isotropic — no preferred direction — so a diagnostic or computation that picks a coordinate axis imports a spurious direction-dependence; state the rotation-invariant form, not an axis projection. The reference is derivative_skewness: the normalized third invariant of the strain rate boldsymbolS, not the axis-picking longitudinal velocity-derivative skewness (decision 17).\nTwo-tier docs. CLAUDE.md is the terse working map and defers here; this document carries the rationale and the decision log. Mathematics is rendered LaTeX in the glossary's notation (docs/src/notation.md); a code identifier is always in backticks and never inside a math span; each code object is bound to its mathematical symbol on first mention — rendered as one LaTeX span, textttdissipation equiv varepsilon.\nNo dead references on the user-facing surface. A rendered docstring — and a thrown error — must read complete at the REPL, where cross-links are dead: it cites no section number (§4.5), decision-log entry, or repo path. Design rationale and section citations stay in # comments and this document.\nCross-referencing is discriminating-minimalist. A link earns its place: promote a backticked identifier to @ref / @extref only for a genuine jump with one clear destination; link a target at most once per docstring; never link the docstring's own binding, its arguments, or bare Base names — except the storage-backend / element types and GBGPeriodicFields objects, which @extref to their own package docs (the DocumenterInterLinks setup).\nVerification is by numerical oracle, not a formalization ledger. Because the package introduces no novel mathematical objects (they are GBGPeriodicFields' and GBGStreamingStatistics'), its nontrivial claims are dynamical — the exactness of mathcal E, the order of the integrator, imaginary-axis stability, mean conservation — and are pinned by numerical oracle tests (a Taylor–Green order study, unforced energy decay, exact diffusion). This is why the package follows the GBGCloudPhysics consumer pattern with no FORMALIZATION.md; a typed-statement ledger is a backend's need. The design's structure — the module carving and role wiring — is separately checked against a relational-logic model (docs/architecture.als, decisions 21–22); that model carries no dynamics, so it complements the oracle rather than becoming the ledger. Revisit only if a built Model rung introduces genuinely package-specific formalism.\nCategory/type errors are their own correctness class. A claim can be perfectly formatted and read true yet conflate two kinds of object — a field with its representation (physical vs spectral); a diagnostic scalar with the field it reduces (the rate varepsilon, a number, vs a dissipation field; k vs the spectrum E(kappa)); an operator with its parameter (mathcal E vs nu; mathbb P vs mathcal P); a rate with its time-integral; a spectral density E(kappa) with an energy. Type each object from the code and expand the statement — the physics mirror of the sibling backends' type-error guard (GBGStreamingStatistics' density-vs-measure, estimator-vs-estimand).\nThe /check-docs battery. Documentation quality is enforced by a full matrix — (Correct / Self-contained / Notation / Formatted / Current / Cited / variant-pairs) × (every surface: ARCHITECTURE.md, every src/*.jl docstring, every docs/src page) — run to completion in one pass (.claude/commands/check-docs.md). Correct extends to physical-formula and normalization checking (the 1N, the 2varkappa, the tfrac12 in the energy, Parseval factors) and to the category/type-error class above. Two build lints (lint_inline_math, lint_notation_variants) and checkdocs = :exports are the mechanical backstop.\nCitations follow author–year; the full references are §References. Never cite an inaccessible source; the material under inspiration/ is private and never referenced. A reference block of two or more citations is a bullet list — one citation per line — never a semicolon-joined paragraph.\n\n","title":"10. Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#11.-Decision-log","page":"Architecture","text":"Append-only; each entry stands on its own. Dates in YYYY-MM.\n\n(2026-06) Integrating-factor split with exact diffusion. The stiff viscous/diffusive part is integrated exactly by mathcal E(boldsymbolkappa); only the advection is explicit. Removes the diffusive stability limit and preserves scheme order under linear forcing.\n(2026-06) Rotational (Lamb-vector) form for the advection. Cheap, energy-conserving spectral advection; the reversible generator of the geometric lens.\n(2026-06) IFLowStorageRK3 (Williamson 2N / RKW3) is the default; per-scheme CFL. Third-order, imaginary-axis stable, two registers per field; IFRK2 kept as a cheaper, viscously-stabilized option.\n(2026-06) One passive scalar varphi, coupled in-stage. Velocity and scalar advance through the same RK stages to keep the passive coupling time-accurate.\n(2026-06) Lagrangian tracers by Heun-in-time; interpolation scheme is the accuracy axis. The pusher uses the start- and end-of-step velocities the solver already produces.\n(2026-07) Statistics extracted to GBGStreamingStatistics. The conditional/transition-density machinery is domain-neutral and shared across turbulence, cloud microphysics, and molecular dynamics; it is consumed, not owned (§5).\n(2026-07) Organising frame is specification × realization, not three co-equal axes. Method is fibered over Model; the two research disciplines (method-independence, statistical commutation) map onto the two kinds of variation (§3). (Revised from an earlier \"System × Model × Method\" framing after an adversarial review.)\n(2026-07) Mori–Zwanzig is the central lens, not a foundation. The code does not depend on it; it organizes understanding and is demonstrated, not assumed. The other lenses (GENERIC, the commuting square, RG) are read through it, and RG/universality is a hypothesis to test (§8).\n(2026-07) Precision and footprint are studied axes. Both precisions supported equally, the working point chosen by fitness test; the sufficiency frontier is a deliverable (§6).\n(2026-07) Particles are instruments with first-class numerics. The role is a probe; the  trajectory integrator and sampling-consistency are fitness-tested Method work (§4.6).\n(2026-07) The run + warm-up layer is a first-class, present concern. Ease of setup and running  is the primary quality metric, so the ergonomic front door is built now, not deferred (§2, §4.7).\n(2026-07) Single ARCHITECTURE.md (consumer archetype) + docs/src/notation.md; no  FORMALIZATION.md. IT is an operator consumer; its claims are dynamical and pinned by tests  (§10).\n(2026-07) warmup! detects stationarity on the dissipation varepsilon and the  velocity-derivative skewness, not the kinetic energy. varepsilon(t) (the small scales, which  settle last) must stop drifting over a trailing window; k(t) is avoided because a constant-energy  forcing clamps it and a fixed-injection forcing makes it ring. The skewness S(t)  (derivative_skewness) must also have plateaued with lvert Srvert above a floor — the strict  \"real turbulence yet\" gate (S runs from 0 toward approx -05). When the scalar is  forced, langlevarphi^2rangle must flatten too — the scalar reaches stationarity after the  velocity. A fixed turnover/time/step budget is the explicit alternative. (Follows the warm-up  literature.)\n(2026-07) Operator-free forcing construction. ConstantPower(target = 0.1) (and the other banded  forcings) return a DeferredForcing capturing the parameters, materialized into the concrete  AbstractForcing once Simulation builds the domain — so a case names its forcing before the grid  exists. The operator-taking constructor is unchanged. (Originally the keyword was power and the  return type ForcingSpec; both since renamed — power → target, ForcingSpec → DeferredForcing  — decision 25.)\n(2026-07) BandRescaling can hold the band or the total energy (over = :band | :total).  :band (default) fixes the forced band's energy and lets the total float; :total rescales the band  to pin the whole-field energy — hence the r.m.s. velocity u and mathrmRe_lambda — the  constant-total-energy forcing convention. A modeling choice with two defensible answers, so it is a selectable  option rather than a fixed default.\n(2026-07) Modules named from the Mori–Zwanzig lens; Equations split into Dynamics +  Observables; Driver → SimulationRun. Since MZ is the central lens (§8), the physics concerns  take their names from it — the dynamics (the generator/RHS) and its observables (the measured  functions of state) — rather than from production-DNS convention (rhs/stats) or generic software  terms. Equations conflated the two and under-described; splitting it makes each concern its own  module and its own name. Realization and infrastructure concerns keep job-names (TimeIntegration,  Forcing, SimulationState, and the run layer SimulationRun, paired with SimulationState).\n(2026-07) Consume GBGPeriodicFields's fused domain; the velocity-derivative skewness becomes a  coordinate-free strain invariant. Four fused operations were requested of, and shipped by, PF —  combine! (axpby), weighted_inner, symmetric_gradient, det — and wired in. The combine! op replaces  the hand-rolled RK/OU field_data broadcasts (charter invariant 2). The weighted_inner op fuses the  per-step band energy in the forcings and expresses scalar_dissipation as the Dirichlet energy, with  no nablavarphi materialized. And derivative_skewness becomes the normalized third invariant of  the strain rate boldsymbolS, 3ClangledetboldsymbolSranglelangleboldsymbolSboldsymbolSrangle^32,  with the exact isotropy constant C = tfrac47sqrt152approx 15649 reproducing the classical  Sapprox-05 — the axis-picking longitudinal form was coordinate-dependent, the invariant is the  honest object, and symmetric_gradient forms the strain without the full velocity-gradient tensor.  The physical strain tensor is unavoidable (cubic ⇒ non-Parseval), so this diagnostic allocates its  rank-2 buffers per call (sampling cadence) rather than keeping them resident — footprint over compute.\n(2026-07) Phenomenology — a reference-law library, author-year naming, extraction-bound. A new  submodule (§4.8) collecting the established turbulence phenomenology — model spectra, structure-function  laws, dimensionless-constant fits — as one strategy family per quantity: each law family an abstract  type, each parametrization an author-year preset (Spectra.Pope2000), disambiguated on three axes  (quantity → submodule, paper → author-year, same-year collision → a trailing letter). It is the  predicted counterpart to Observables — a provider the validation layer reads, not a comparer — and  holds our own fits beside the literature on equal footing. Built inside this package but designed to  be cut into a standalone GBGTurbulencePhenomenology with no rewrite (the GBGStreamingStatistics  precedent): a dependency-light leaf — no GBGPeriodicFields, GBGStreamingStatistics, or dynamics  dependency — that the umbrella does not re-export, with its own doc and API group. Extract when a  second consumer appears.\n(2026-07) The measured-vs-predicted validation harness — the first Phenomenology consumer. The  velocity energy spectrum is measured from a run (energy_spectrum, the halved GBGPeriodicFields  radial spectrum) and the integral scale integral_scale from it; flow_parameters bridges a  Simulation to a Phenomenology FlowParameters (nu, varepsilon, u, L_11 from the  measured state), and validate_spectrum(sim, model) compares the measured spectrum against a model  spectrum evaluated at that state, returning a named tuple of the measured and predicted spectra and  their relative L^2 discrepancy. This realizes the  comparison-machine path of §3 for spectra — the measured half is a GBGPeriodicFields reduction, so  no GBGStreamingStatistics bridge is needed for it. The structure-function and scaling-constant  paths (via radial_correlation and the scalar diagnostics) share the shape and are deferred.\n(2026-07) Resolution-based viscosity — the run layer consumes a Phenomenology law to build a  run. Simulation now sets nu from the grid by default (no viscosity): it reads the  dissipation the box sustains from the box-normalized dissipation law Scalings.Li2008  (k(Lvarepsilon)^23 approx 09, a box-normalized group — distinct from the classical dissipation anomaly C_varepsilon = varepsilon L_11u^3 on the integral scale, related only on a common length by G = tfrac32 C_varepsilon^-23 — calibrated to  JHTDB), then sets nu so the resolution criterion kappa_maxeta equals the k_max_η  option (default 2) — the highest Reynolds number consistent with that resolution criterion. This is the  construction half of the consume-and-fit loop (§4.7): laws are read to build runs, not only to  validate them.  The run is forced-and-stationary by default (a BandRescaling holds the total energy at kinetic_energy,  default 1.5, i.e. u=1); an explicit viscosity opts back into direct control. It does not  breach the extraction boundary — Li2008 stays a pure scalar law with no GBGPeriodicFields /  GBGStreamingStatistics / dynamics dependency; only the run layer (never Phenomenology) knows both  sides. The accuracy target is kappa_maxeta (spectral content at the highest evolved  wavenumber), not etaDelta x (geometric): the former is dealiasing-aware through  kappa_max = f tfrac N2tfrac2piL (max_wavenumber), which is correct because  accuracy itself depends on the dealiasing.\n(2026-07) The physics-first carving is checked against a formal structural model  (docs/architecture.als). The module carving and the Mori–Zwanzig role wiring were re-examined  against an explicit relational-logic model (built and checked in Alloy) under an adversarial multi-lens  review — a design-time artifact, deliberately not a shipped ledger — to counter a pull toward  software-shaped, object-first structure. The model carries only structure: the dependency graph  (checked against the actual using edges), the state as one product of typed slots on a single module,  the Model ladder as a coarsening poset with an explicit fine→coarse projection, the  reversible/irreversible/source split of the generator wired to the observables that measure it, closure  required exactly where a rung is a proper projection, and the passive tracer kept distinct from an  inertial (slipping) phase. It carries no dynamics — the nonlinear operator, the exact rates, the  memory integral, the symplectic bracket stay with the numerical oracle (§10, decision 12) — so it is not  a FORMALIZATION.md by another route. Four adversarial passes converged: the structural claims are  mutually consistent and every self-check is non-vacuous (perturb its premise and the check fails). The  exercise confirmed the carving is internally consistent and surfaced two things now recorded: the four  MZ roles are level-agnostic — abstractions the coarser Model rungs will populate, ready but not  populated while DNS is the only built rung (not a speculative extension point, invariant 9) — and the  state-realization discipline now folded into invariant 4.\n(2026-07) Mori–Zwanzig raised to foundation; the physics-first renames; this document rewritten.  Following the structural verification (decision 21), Mori–Zwanzig is made the foundation of the  architecture, not merely the central lens — reversing decision 8. The reversal is safe because the  decomposition is exact (a projection is an identity, not an approximation); what stays investigated,  never assumed is the closure that models the memory it leaves, so the foundation and the \"investigate,  don't assume\" charter (invariant 6) are one commitment. It founds the architecture — the carving  and the names — not the numerics, which are Navier–Stokes solved pseudospectrally regardless. The four  roles take their role names: Dynamics → Generator, TimeIntegration → Propagator, and  SimulationState split into SimulationDomain (the frame) and State (the field), completing and  partly revising decision 16. Decision 16's \"realization keeps job-names\" is re-cut on the sharper line  it was reaching for — a level-agnostic Mori–Zwanzig role wears its role name, a concrete mechanism  wears its job — under which the propagator is a role present at every rung, not mere realization, so  Propagator is correct while its schemes (IFLowStorageRK3) keep their descriptive names.  ARCHITECTURE.md was rewritten whole (except this append-only log) to read holistically from the  foundation down.\n(2026-07) Review-round refinements: Physics → FlowConditions folded into Generator, the  forcing-protocol renames, and an open-systems sub-lens. A close read of the rewrite settled three  changes. (a) The parameter bundle Physics — the viscosity, the Schmidt number, and the two forcings,  the physical conditions that tune the generator, misfiled under the Propagator stepper — is renamed  FlowConditions and folded into Generator, the right-hand side it parametrizes; this adds the  honest Generator → Forcing dependency (the RHS includes the forcing source). A six-perspective naming  panel chose FlowConditions over System (the bundle is a proper subset of the §3 System axis — it  lacks the initial condition and the domain — so a type named for the axis while holding less is ambiguous  and imprecise) and over GeneratorParameters (relational, and imprecise once a coarser rung's generator  gains closure coefficients). (b) The AbstractForcing protocol methods are made grammatically uniform,  verb-first: set_forcing_rate!, add_forcing_source!, prepare_forcing!, apply_forcing!. (c) The  open-systems / non-equilibrium reading is recorded as a sub-lens under Mori–Zwanzig (§8), not new  state types: a six-tradition panel (unanimous) confirmed the system/environment split is the  Mori–Zwanzig resolved/modeled cut, but that whatever a model retains of the environment is a finite  Markovian surrogate on the system's own side — a modeled slot on the one State, never a separate  'environment state' — so a stateful forcing's internal state homes there when StochasticOU is  de-archived, and no type is minted now (invariant 9).\n(2026-07) The domain module is SimulationDomain, its concrete type FixedDomain. Decision 22  split the frame into a SimulationDomain module; naming its concrete type took one more cut, because  Julia will not let a module cleanly re-export a type of its own name — using ..SimulationDomain:  SimulationDomain binds the module and shadows the type, so a bare SimulationDomain(...) constructor  call stops resolving (verified empirically). The module keeps the user-facing concern name  SimulationDomain (the discretized box a run lives on); its type takes a name for what it is among the  alternatives. Weighed against SimpleDomain (relational and vague — simple next to what?) and  PeriodicCube (a property the deferred sheared frame also has), the choice is FixedDomain: the  fixed, unsheared, orthogonal cube with time-independent, separable wavenumbers. It names the very axis  the deferred ShearedDomain (the Rogallo moving frame, §7) varies — fixed versus sheared, separable  versus time-dependent — so the two type names read as one family. FlowConditions needs no such care: a  type, not a module, it cannot collide.\n(2026-07) Design-review batch: the recovery split, ForcingSpec → DeferredForcing, a scalar  spectrum IC, and precision-aware Hou–Li damping. Four refinements settled on a reading of the API.  (a) The fused velocity_vorticity! is split into the sibling recoveries velocity_to_physical!  and vorticity_to_physical!: its two halves share no intermediate — curl-then-transform for the  vorticity, copy-then-transform for the velocity, over separate buffers — so the split carries no  compute or memory penalty and reads as one convention with scalar_to_physical! (X_to_physical!  produces the physical X, its source shown in the signature). (b) ForcingSpec is renamed  DeferredForcing, naming the object by its meaning — a forcing specified before the domain exists  and materialized once it does — rather than the spec abbreviation (superseding the name minted in  decision 14). (c) random_scalar! gains a spectrum keyword mirroring  random_solenoidal_velocity!, seeding the scalar from a target radial scalar-variance spectrum (an  Obukhov–Corrsin form from Phenomenology) for spectrum-matched initial conditions and faster  warm-up. (d) HouLi's strength defaults to nothing, meaning auto: resolved at kernel-build  time to -log of the element type's machine epsilon (approx 36 for Float64,  approx 16 for Float32), so the Nyquist mode always damps to that precision's epsilon —  precision as a studied axis (invariant 4); an explicit strength overrides.\n(2026-07) The state reified as ResolvedFields; the per-step scratch relocated to Propagator.  The DNS state — the evolving spectral velocity hatboldsymbolu and optional scalar  hatvarphi — is made first-class as ResolvedFields (module State), completing the  Mori–Zwanzig triad whose State leg had been missing: the fields were scattered across the Simulation  value while the diagnostics read the physical scratch as if it were state (kinetic_energy(sim) read  buffers.velocity). It is a bare, inert record with every operation external — the panel's ruling,  which adopts the category lens's guardrail (arrows primary, no methods on the carrier) and rejects its  dissolution. ResolvedFields holds only the spectral truth (hatboldsymbolu,  hatvarphi); the physical velocity, vorticity, and scalar are a Method-side derived view, never  stored, so the state cannot go stale — and because the physical chart is rung-specific (EDQNM has no  phases to invert), keeping it out of the state keeps the record fit to lift, unchanged, to one slot of  the future product-of-slots State (§7, abstraction-ready, not built). The observables become thin  functions of the natural representation with no State dependency:  kinetic_energy/scalar_variance/derivative_skewness/energy_spectrum are Parseval reductions of  hatboldsymbolu/hatvarphi, dissipation curls into scratch and reduces, and the run layer  prepares what each needs — the mirror of the generator, both now thin externals over bare fields.  Consequently the monolithic FieldBuffers is dissolved: the generator's operators take their  individual scratch fields (naming no bundle), so the scratch is free to live with the integrator  that owns it as IntegratorBuffers (module Propagator), built by allocate_buffers dispatching  on the concrete scheme; ScalarBuffers stays a nested optional sub-bundle. The name ResolvedFields  (over ResolvedState / DNSState / FieldState) follows the FixedDomain axis-lesson taken to the  slot level: the module State carries the level-agnostic role, so the concrete slot is named for its  representation — the resolved fields — with FilteredFields (LES/FDF) and a spectrum record (EDQNM)  its eventual siblings in the product. A six-lens naming panel with an adversarial pass fixed the slate  (IntegratorBuffers, and the scratch-field names product_physical/product_spectral over the  velocity-only lamb). (Amended by decisions 30–31: the physical chart the diagnostics read is now  typed — the DerivedFields cache in State — the recoveries live with the state, the assembly  scratch on the ResolvedGenerator, and IntegratorBuffers holds only the scheme's registers; the  individual-scratch-arguments discipline this entry set applies unchanged to the internals.)\n(2026-07) Phase-shift dealiasing, on GBGPeriodicFields' translation_kernel. Adds  PhaseShift as an opt-in DealiasingStrategy (the default stays the rotational form +  spherical 23). Each quadratic nonlinear term is assembled twice — on the grid and on a grid  displaced half a cell — and averaged, cancelling the aliasing images that wrap in an odd number of  axes; a spherical truncation at  kappa_max = tfracsqrt23Ncdottfrac2piL approx 094 kappa_mathrmNy removes the  surviving two-axis images (a single half-cell shift is exact only in 1-D; Patterson & Orszag 1971,  Rogallo 1981). It keeps a sqrt2 wider band than the 23 rule (approx 28times the modes  in the retained sphere) at 2times the nonlinear-term transforms and a displaced-evaluation scratch  field. This realizes the deferred D6 assembly generalization (§7) for this sibling: DealiasingStrategy  now dispatches the whole hatboldsymboluto dealiased-term computation, not only a post-multiply  kernel. The displacement is built once as two translation_kernels (the shift theorem's phase, a  GBGPeriodicFields translation primitive whose Nyquist even/odd split makes the kernel a real  field); because the cutoff is  sub-Nyquist the Nyquist-attenuation caveat never bites. Verified against a zero-padded oracle — exact to  machine precision across the retained band on a field whose raw product is 44 % aliased.\n(2026-07) Defaults live only at the Simulation front door; observables read the state; buffers are  per-scheme. A round of API tightening after a fresh docs read.\nDefaults only in SimulationRun. The low-level types carry no convenience defaults — FixedDomain, FlowConditions, the initial-condition seeders, and Tracers require their arguments; the Simulation front door supplies the run-layer values. Model/preset types (Pope2000, HouLi, …) keep their canonical parameters; rng may default anywhere. (This removed a laundered smell — an IC seeder that defaulted kinetic_energy = 0.5 while the front door supplied 1.5, once documented \"by design\" rather than fixed.)\nscalar_type → element_type (the keyword names the array element type, not the passive scalar's type); Tracers(domain, positions), so backend and precision flow from the domain.\nstep! advances the State (ResolvedFields), not loose hatboldsymbolu / hatvarphi.\nObservables are functions of the state, not the Simulation. No f(sim) form: an observable taking a Simulation reached across state / buffers / conditions, smearing the single source of truth (decision 26); the run layer supplies the explicit state variables.\nallocate_buffers is per-scheme and exact (invariant 4): each integrator allocates only the registers it uses; over-allocation is a hard error. The friction of the now-explicit FlowConditions is the standing argument for a reified Generator operator (§7). (Realized: decision 31.)\n(2026-07) The RK3 stage ordering: both right-hand sides before either update; the  dealiasing × scheme convergence matrix. The IFLowStorageRK3 stage loop updated and rotated  hatboldsymbolu before assembling the scalar's advection. A mask strategy never reads  hatboldsymbolu there, but PhaseShift's displaced evaluation does (decision 27) — so the  displaced flux paired the next stage's velocity with this stage's scalar, breaking the odd-axis  alias cancellation at O(Delta t) and degrading the scalar from third to first order  (measured self-convergence ratios 2.02 against 7.97 for the velocity; IFRK2's call sites were  consistent, and the term-level padding oracle cannot see a call-site mismatch — it checks the  assembly with matched inputs). The comment that had licensed the old order (\"u and φ̂ are  untouched above\") was written before PhaseShift made the assembly read hatboldsymbolu  — an invalidated premise, not a wrong derivation. Both right-hand sides are now evaluated at the  stage state before either field is updated (bit-identical for the mask strategies), and the suite  carries the dealiasing × scheme convergence matrix: both fields must converge at scheme order  under every strategy. The bug is the motivating instance for decision 31's seam — a stage that  reads a mid-update intermediate is a malformed factorization, which a reified right-hand side makes  unrepresentable.\n(2026-07) The observation layer: DerivedFields — stamped, memoized charts over the state; the  recoveries move to State. The physical recoveries are the chart map of the state — they  read only (hatboldsymbolu hatvarphi) and the frame — so they home in State  (Generator imports them for its displaced evaluations; the new Generator → State edge respects  the include order). DerivedFields memoizes their values: preallocated slots (:velocity,  :vorticity), each stamped with the fields epoch it was computed at; an accessor  (physical_velocity, physical_vorticity) returns the stored field when current and recomputes it  first otherwise — the deposit-or-recompute contract — and step! advances the epoch as it  completes. An out-of-band mutation is declared through resolved_fields_modified!, named for  the event that breaks the chart–state invariant (the naming lesson of SciML's u_modified! →  derivative_discontinuity! rename: name the verb for the violated invariant, not the mutated  slot); the sanctioned mutators call it themselves, and the seeders' docstrings direct hand-run  loops to it. This kills the step! silent-precondition footgun — there is no caller prefill left  to forget. Epoch granularity is deliberately coarse: one counter per dynamical factor of the  tower (fields, particles), no finer — every production memoization precedent surveyed (LAMMPS  computes' timestep stamps, OpenFOAM's registry event numbers, Fluidsim's iteration stamps) uses a  single counter, within-step consistency is the seam's job (decision 31), and an over-fine epoch  system is itself a silent-staleness surface. Charts are opt-in (an undeclared chart errors —  resident GPU memory is a choice), and the cache's shared spectral scratch replaces two  IntegratorBuffers slots — net one resident spectral vector fewer than before the layer.  Naming: DerivedFields was fixed by a six-lens panel (5–1 over DerivedQuantities, upheld by  an adversarial pass) with a license clause the docstring carries — each slot holds a derived  field's values, on the grid lattice or at the tracer positions; particle content never enters —  a recorded rename trigger (the first particle-sourced slot, e.g. a number density, reopens the  name), and a recorded guard: varepsilon, k, E(kappa) never become resident slots  (statistics belong to GBGStreamingStatistics; occasional diagnostics allocate per call). Kills,  not to be re-proposed: Observations (collides with Observables), DerivedViews/StateViews  (a Julia view cannot be stale), Charts (these are non-invertible functionals, not manifold  charts), Diagnostics (the community word for scalar time series), Snapshot (asserts  restart-copy structure), DerivedQuantities (indiscriminate extension).\n(2026-07) The generator reified: ResolvedGenerator and the six-verb protocol; schemes shrink  to stage arithmetic. Closes §7's deferred item on its own terms — the reification now carries  what the loose functions could not: the assembly scratch (the quadratic products, the PhaseShift  buffers, the forcing-rate arrays), the conditions, and the step lifecycle. The protocol:  prepare! (open the step — advance forcing state, arm linear rates), velocity_rhs! /  scalar_rhs! (each field's explicit right-hand side at a stage state: the dealiased nonlinear  term plus the additive forcing source), velocity_factor! / scalar_factor! (each field's exact  linear subflow — the generator is the semilinear split partial_t hat q = Ahat q + N(hat q)  with a diagonal, exact linear operator, the exponential-integrator family's special case), and  finalize! (close the step — the post-step forcing projections). The rhs/factor pairs are the  required core; prepare!/finalize! default to no-ops (a two-tier required/optional protocol).  finalize! on the generator is a recorded deviation from the solver-library precedent (the  SciML/PETSc/SUNDIALS right-hand-side objects are pure evaluators, with post-step action in  callback layers), adopted because the projection is part of the step's contract, not of  observation: the charts, the diagnostics, and the tracer push must see the forced end-of-step  state, and molecular-dynamics practice places exactly this operation inside the step (a  constraint's velocity-projection stage, a thermostat's end-of-step rescale, the trailing factor of  a symmetric splitting). The signature becomes step!(integrator, state, generator, domain,  buffers, derived, dt): both schemes now contain no physics — no viscosity, no forcing calls, no  conditions — and Propagator drops its Forcing dependency. The old public assembly trio  (velocity_nonlinear!, scalar_nonlinear!, integrating_factor!) demotes to internals behind  the protocol, retiring the eight-argument assembly signatures. The name extends the axis  convention — ResolvedFields ↔ ResolvedGenerator, resolved against filtered, with  FilteredGenerator (carrying its closure) the eventual LES sibling — and the run layer's  _resolve_forcing was renamed _materialize_forcing, reserving resolved for that axis against  DeferredForcing's materialization vocabulary. Every migration stage was verified  bit-identical on fixed-seed trajectories (all three dealiasing strategies and the  StochasticOU-forced case) against the pre-migration code.\n(2026-07) Forcing hooks own their scratch; the lifecycle order is contract. The four-hook  interface no longer receives the integrator's buffer bundle (\"for interface uniformity\" — the  smell decision 26's discipline predicted): set_forcing_rate!, apply_forcing!,  prepare_forcing!, and add_forcing_source! take only what they act on, and StochasticOU owns  its noise scratch (one field in physical and one in spectral representation, allocated on first  use like its hatboldsymbolf state) instead of reaching into buffers.product_* by  field name — at the recorded price of two resident fields for a velocity-forcing instance,  accepted to sever the one cross-component reach-in (the extended-system discipline: a thermostat  never touches the integrator's registers; the OU re-add stays gated on a GPU-tuned generator  either way). The hook lifecycle — prepare and rate before the stages, source at every stage,  projection after the completed step — is documented as part of the AbstractForcing contract,  with the rate and the source recorded as two channels of one forcing term (the exactly linear  part in the factor, the remainder at stages). This stage deliberately ran before the generator  reification, against the migration plan's original order: the borrow had to be severed before the  borrowed scratch could move homes. BandRescaling carries the Berendsen-class caveat — a  deterministic clamp has no conserved quantity and no well-defined ensemble except in limiting  cases (Bussi, Donadio & Parrinello 2007) — a spin-up and amplitude-holding tool, with stochastic  rescaling the recorded upgrade path when faithful fluctuations of the held quantity matter.\n(2026-07) The memory audit: a frozen buffer ledger and a liveness lower bound, in the standard  suite. \"Minimal footprint\" becomes a checked property instead of an aspiration  (test/memory_audit.jl, run by Pkg.test()). Two instruments. (a) The ledger: a reflective  walk over every resident field-sized buffer a run owns (state, observation cache, generator,  integrator registers, forcing-owned state and kernels, the tracer buffers), counted per shape  class over the full five-axis combination space — scheme × dealiasing × scalar ×  (velocity forcing × scalar forcing) × tracers. The frozen spec is compositional: twelve  unforced, tracer-free base cells plus a per-forcing delta table and a tracer delta, and an  exhaustive sweep over the whole product (~360 combinations) asserts measured = base + deltas,  cell by cell — a cross-term (a buffer whose existence depends on two axes at once) fails the  sweep and must be recorded. The observation cache's charts axis (resident charts are  opt-in) is audited at the API level with its own delta; for any stepping run it is pinned by  the physics — the rotational generator's declared working set requires both charts — and becomes  a live run axis when per-component chart declarations or further charts land. Adding or removing  a buffer anywhere fails the suite, so a footprint change must be a conscious, recorded decision.  The particle-integrator axis has one point today (the Heun pusher), entering as on/off with the  per-tracer (M 3) arrays audited in their own units; precision, backend, and the  interpolation scheme change bytes, never counts, so they are outside a count ledger. Two scope  boundaries are deliberate: the ledger audits the resident working set — construction-time  seeding transients and the recorded per-call diagnostic allocations (invariant 4's exception,  e.g. the derivative-skewness tensors) are peak-memory events a separate instrument would  cover — and the frame (the domain's plans, multipliers, kernels) is excluded as shared,  combination-independent infrastructure, a constant offset rather than an axis. (That second  boundary was withdrawn by decision 36: constant in the combination space is not the same as  small, and the frame now carries a ledger of its own.) (c) The episode  ledger closes the resident-vs-peak gap: the hot loop is allocation-free (the step guards), so  the stepping-phase peak is the resident ledger; outside it the transient episodes are few,  sequential, and frozen in units of N^3 reals with mechanical @allocated drift guards —  seeding approx 103 (construction only; includes a 32-unit transient from the aliased  Leray projection, raised to GBGPeriodicFields), the energy spectrum approx 16 (the  backend's radial-spectrum staging), and the velocity-derivative skewness approx 196 — the  dominant episode, about half again the default resident set, and on by default in warmup!'s  skewness gate: the binding device peak of a default warm-up at the largest fitting N.  This prices invariant 4's per-call-diagnostic trade (resident traded into episodic peak) and  names its reduction paths: symmetric six-component tensor storage at the backend (the tensors are  stored with all nine components today), or gating the skewness off when memory binds. The  archived StochasticOU's prepare phase also projects its noise aliased — an OU-forced step is  not allocation-free today (approx 3 units per step), recorded on its de-archival gate. The  allocator-level high-water mark (GC and pool timing) remains an empirical measurement on the  benchmark hosts, a fitness number rather than a test invariant. (b) The  liveness audit: each step! body is a straight-line program, so its op list (reads and writes  per primitive field operation) gives every value a live range, and the peak number of  simultaneously live values per shape class is the lower bound on the buffers the implemented  operation order needs — the classical register-allocation argument. The audit asserts achieved ≥  bound and that every nonzero gap carries a named holder; an unclaimed gap fails the test.  Current verdicts: the physical-vector count sits exactly at its bound under the chart contract  (the charts are contract-live across the step; the epoch-at-start chart-reuse that would release  two registers during the stages is the recorded candidate); one spectral-vector gap is held by  the State/Generator ownership boundary (the cache's recovery scratch and the generator's  product registers could alias in the implemented schedule but live on opposite sides of a  deliberately kept seam); the spectral-scalar gaps are held by the shared factor register  (deletable by a fused exponential-multiply convolution — raised to GBGPeriodicFields as a  request, with an in-place transform mode for disposable sources as the second request, which  would halve the archived StochasticOU's noise scratch on de-archival) and by the scalar's  transient registers (copy/nonlinear/factor could share one slot). Bounds are relative to the  typed-field reuse model; the byte-level refinement (a spectral and a physical field have equal  byte size under the rfft padding) is recorded as a GBGPeriodicFields-level question, not chased  here. The op lists are re-derived whenever a step! body or the generator protocol changes — the  ledger failing is the reminder.\n(2026-07) The compute audit: frozen transform and pass ledgers with fusion bounds, in the  standard suite. The compute twin of decision 33 (test/compute_audit.jl): the same  straight-line op lists — extracted to test/audit_ops.jl as the single source of truth both  audits project, and upgraded to pass-exact fidelity to step! (stage 1 seeds the low-storage  accumulator, the last stage skips its rotation) — priced in the two compute currencies of a  pseudospectral step on a GPU. (a) The transform ledger: scalar-equivalent FFT counts (c2r,  r2c) per step, frozen per scheme × dealiasing × scalar cell with the chart recomputes amortized  in (each chart is recovered once per fields epoch, wherever first read — which is also why the  tracer transform delta is zero: the Heun endpoints ride the epoch cache, the observation  layer's compute dividend). Transforms carry the flops — tfrac52 N^3log_2 N^3 per scalar  transform against O(1–20) flops per point for every pointwise kernel, far under the roofline  machine balance — so flops are derived from the count, never an independent frozen axis.  The counts are per-evaluation × evaluations (asserted structurally), the mask evaluation is  (6 3) for the velocity and (1 3) for the scalar, and PhaseShift doubles the velocity  transforms exactly — the dealiasing axis's memory↔compute trade now priced on both sides.  Verdicts: every cell sits at its lower bound (each physical component field consumed costs  one c2r, each spectral product component one r2c, for the algorithms as chosen) except  PhaseShift × scalar, where scalar_rhs! re-shifts and re-recovers the displaced velocity that  velocity_rhs! left fresh at the same stage state — 3 c2r per evaluation, held by the named  shifted-velocity reuse gap (the fix is a same-stage freshness contract on the generator's  displaced scratch across two protocol verbs — a seam question, recorded, not chased). (b) The  pass ledger: full-field memory passes per shape class for everything between transforms —  pointwise kernels are bandwidth-bound, so what fusion buys is passes, and the fusion bound  is the liveness argument transplanted to traffic: per between-barriers segment, a perfectly  fused kernel reads each live-in once, writes each live-out once, reads each field-scale  configuration array once, and a pure intermediate costs nothing. Verdicts: the physical-side  and configuration classes sit at the fused ideal in every cell; every gap lives in the two  spectral classes and is held by PF kernel granularity (the numpy model's op-per-kernel  boundaries) plus the factor materialization the open GBGPeriodicFields  fused-exponential request would delete. Forcing enters as frozen per-hook deltas (only the  stochastic forcing transforms — its noise pipeline; the OU-velocity column is cross-checked  against its op list). Both ledgers are static projections; their currency is anchored by the  shared op lists (decision 33's re-derive obligation) and regenerated for review by the  AUDIT_PRINT aid. Kernel-launch counts and achieved bandwidth on the GPU hosts remain  benchmark-side empirics, outside the suite.\n(2026-07) A recorded cost is not a minimal one: every audited cell is held against a physical  floor, and the residual distance must be claimed. Decisions 33 and 34 built ledgers that  record what a run costs and fail when the number drifts. That certifies stability, not  minimality: a frozen cell measures what is, never what should be, and a cost with no  lower bound held against it is unexamined rather than optimal. The correction is to give every  cell a floor — the cost the quantity cannot go below on physical grounds — and to require  that achieved − floor > 0 carry a named holder, split into FIX-then-retire (a local win,  removed when taken) and PF-RAISE (a recorded backend request). An unclaimed gap fails the  suite. This inverts enforcement from did the number change to is there residual distance to  the floor, and is it accounted for, and it subsumes a third axis the two ledgers could not see:  form — not how well a fixed expression is scheduled, but which of the mathematically  equivalent expressions is evaluated at all (Parseval for a quadratic reduction, the Dirichlet  form for a gradient energy, the solenoidal identity for enstrophy, a spectrum that already  integrates to the energy). Cubic and nonlinear invariants are floors, not gaps: the Lamb vector,  the scalar flux, and det S have no quadratic shortcut.\nTwo structural consequences beyond the ledgers. The op model must extend past step! to  every surface a run touches — the initial-condition seeders, the observables, the run-loop  accounting, the tracer push, the forcing hooks — since a cost outside the stepped region is  invisible, not absent. And an eighth axis joins the seven: host–device synchronization.  On GPU-primary code every reduction that returns a Julia scalar is a blocking device-to-host  transfer that drains the pipeline, and no footprint or traffic count can see a barrier; the  floor is one such readback per step under an adaptive CFL (only the speed bound gates the next  launch) and none under a fixed step. Decision 34 had placed synchronization outside the suite as  a benchmark empiric — correct for achieved bandwidth, wrong for the count, which is a static  property of the schedule and belongs with the other ledgers.\nThe instrument is test/optimality_audit.jl, projecting the same op model the two ledgers do.  It carries the holder register — every named holder, its cause, and its disposition — and  enforces the discipline in both directions: an unclaimed gap fails, and so does a stale  holder, one whose gap has been closed everywhere, so retiring it is part of taking the win. It  also splits the pass gap into its two tiers, which have different owners: achieved − bound is  unused fusion (kernel granularity), while bound − floor is the traffic that exists only  because the per-mode symbols are materialized as N^3 arrays. The split is diagnostic — the  spectral-vector class is entirely the first tier (its bound already sits at its floor), and the  real-spectral-scalar class entirely the second (already perfectly fused, yet every unit of it is  lvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2 swept once per integrating-factor build), so a lazy  per-mode symbol apply would delete that class outright and do nothing for the other.\nOn the synchronization axis the default configuration issues three blocking readbacks per  step — the CFL speed bound, and BandRescaling's band and whole-field observables — against a  floor of one. Only the timestep's is irreducible; a forcing's scalar is consumed by the very  next kernel and visits the host only because it is written as a Julia number.\nFirst fruits, each verified and each retiring its holder rather than recording it: the tracer  push no longer retains a start-of-step velocity field (§4.6) — an N^3times3 resident  buffer and per-step copy serving an Mtimes3 interpolation, which the ledgers had faithfully  recorded as a tracer delta and never challenged, in plain conflict with invariant 4's footprint  over compute; the run layer measures varepsilon through the Dirichlet form rather than  building a vorticity to square away; and IFRK2 builds each integrating factor once per step  instead of twice. Where a residual is deliberately not taken it is named and its premise  tested, not asserted — the coincident integrating factors at mathrmSc = 1 are left  unexploited because the guard would make the hot loop's schedule depend on a runtime value, and  because the sound condition is equal diffusivities and both linear rates unarmed, never  mathrmSc = 1 alone.\n(2026-07) The frame is ledgered too, and the two ledgers must jointly account for every  field-scale array. Decision 33's ledger walked what a run evolves — the state, the  observation cache, the generator's assembly scratch, the integrator registers, the tracers. It  never walked the FixedDomain, the read-only frame the run lives on, so that storage was  outside every instrument: the audit claimed to bound residency while measuring only part of it.  The frame is not small. It holds three field-scale arrays — lvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2  as a real array, the same lvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2 again as the weighted_inner  weight, and the dealiasing kernel — 322.5 MiB at 256^3 in double precision, some 8 % on top  of the default working set, and 2.51 GiB at 512^3.\nWhat made the omission urgent rather than untidy is where the incoming lazy per-mode symbol work  lands. The phase-shift translation kernels hang off the generator and were therefore inside the  old ledger, while the dealias mask and both copies of lvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2 sit  in the frame and were outside it — so of the materialized symbols a 256^3 PhaseShift run  carries, the instrument could see 44 %. It would have confirmed the smaller half of the win and  been structurally blind to the larger.\nThe two ledgers are kept apart because they answer different questions — the working set is  what the schedule needs live, the frame is what the discretization costs to have precomputed —  and are joined by a completeness invariant: their sum must account for every field-scale  array reachable from a live Simulation, cell by cell. That invariant, not the frame table, is  what keeps the hole shut, since it fails on new residency wherever it hangs. One exclusion is  named rather than left absent: a backend FFT plan's work area is owned by the vendor library,  reachable from no Julia field, and observable only by a device query, so it stays a benchmark-side  empiric.\nThe frame's floor is zero field-scale arrays — every entry is a closed-form function of the  O(N) per-axis multipliers — and here the floor is demonstrated rather than argued: the  audit reconstructs lvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2 as the outer sum of three axis  vectors, checks the Dirichlet weight against it exactly, and checks that each strategy's kernel  is constant on the level sets of lvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2 — sharp, smooth, and  spherical alike. A future frame entry carrying genuine N^3 information will fail those  reconstructions and move the floor.\nTwo findings the ledger surfaced the first time it ran, both claimed by the existing symbol  holder and retiring with it: lvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2 is resident twice in two  element types, because weighted_inner takes a spectral-scalar-typed weight while the envelope  and integrating-factor broadcasts want the real array; and both spectral-scalar frame entries are  real-valued symbols held in complex containers, at twice their information content. The audit  op model's fifth shape class was renamed in the same step — a class holding  lvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2 and the dealias mask cannot be called rate for one of  its occupants, and the frame table would have reported a nonzero rate cell for a domain that  carries no forcing at all.\n(2026-07) An assumption may never be an input to the bound it is measured against. The  liveness projection of decision 33 extends some values' live range across the whole step, and it  did so for two different reasons under one name. The evolving state  (hatboldsymbolu, hatvarphi, a stochastic forcing's internal state) is live because  the dynamics says so — every stage reads and writes it, and no schedule exists in which it is  not live — so extending it is a statement of fact and belongs in a lower bound. The observation  cache's charts are live because a design choice says so: the epoch-at-end contract requires  them to survive the step, and a different design does not. Extending those and then reporting  the result as the bound made the physical-vector cell a tautology — the ledger carries exactly  those charts, so achieved == bound held by construction, and the suite positively asserted the  equality.\nMeasured on the same operation lists: the honest liveness floor is three physical vectors —  the two substage recoveries plus the product register — against a ledger of five. The audit  reported a gap of zero where there are two physical vectors, 768 MiB at 256^3 in double  precision, the largest single residency item the optimality campaign has surfaced. The holder  that should have claimed it, CHART CONTRACT, had been written down in the register's own  comment from the start and never bound to a cell, because the assumption was consumed by the  bound instead.\nThis is decision 35's failure mode occurring inside the instrument decision 35 depends on.  Decision 35 corrected a recorded cost is not a minimal one; the residue it left is sharper — a  bound built out of the very thing it should test is worse than a loose bound, because a loose  bound at least fails honestly. The correction splits the set in two, admits only the dynamics half  to the projection, and makes the chart contract a holder with a FIX disposition, so the gap is  claimed and retires when the boundary-refresh design closes it.\nTwo details keep the fix honest. The traffic model of decision 34 keeps the union, because a  value that outlives a fusion segment must reach memory whatever the reason it survives — so the  compute ledger is untouched. And a guard test now asserts the assumption is load-bearing:  adding the chart extension back must raise the count, and raise it exactly onto the ledger, so  re-merging the two sets fails the suite rather than silently re-certifying the tautology. The  same test pins that the split is confined to its class — excluding the charts changes neither  spectral class, confirming the state's extension was never the defect.\n\n","title":"11. Decision log"},{"category":"section","location":"architecture/#References","page":"Architecture","text":"Cited author–year above; full entries here (DOIs are omitted rather than filled from memory, per the no-guessed-citation rule). The extraction-bound Phenomenology leaf (§4.8) is exempt: each reference law carries its own self-contained citation in a Reference value, so this list need not duplicate the ~two dozen it cites.\n\nKolmogorov, A. N. (1941). The local structure of turbulence in incompressible viscous fluid for very large Reynolds numbers. Dokl. Akad. Nauk SSSR 30, 301–305.\nBetchov, R. (1956). An inequality concerning the production of vorticity in isotropic turbulence. J. Fluid Mech. 1(5), 497–504. (The isotropy relations behind the strain-skewness constant, §4.3.)\nPatterson, G. S. & Orszag, S. A. (1971). Spectral calculations of isotropic turbulence: efficient removal of aliasing interactions. Phys. Fluids 14, 2538–2541. (Phase-shift dealiasing; the PhaseShift strategy, §7, decision 27.)\nOrszag, S. A. (1971). On the elimination of aliasing in finite-difference schemes by filtering high-wavenumber components. J. Atmos. Sci. 28, 1074. (The 23 truncation rule.)\nOrszag, S. A. & Patterson, G. S. (1972). Numerical simulation of three-dimensional homogeneous isotropic turbulence. Phys. Rev. Lett. 28, 76–79. (The pseudospectral method.)\nRogallo, R. S. (1981). Numerical experiments in homogeneous turbulence. NASA TM-81315. (The sheared-frame scheme, §7; the spherical-truncation dealiasing of the PhaseShift strategy, decision 27.)\nHou, T. Y. & Li, R. (2007). Computing nearly singular solutions using pseudo-spectral methods. J. Comput. Phys. 226, 379–397. (The smooth spectral filter; the HouLi dealiasing strategy.)\nCichowlas, C., Bonaïti, P., Debbasch, F. & Brachet, M. (2005). Effective dissipation and turbulence in spectrally truncated Euler flows. Phys. Rev. Lett. 95, 264502. (Thermalization of the Galerkin-truncated conservative system; the nu = 0 oracle, §7.)\nChollet, J.-P. & Lesieur, M. (1981). Parameterization of small scales of three-dimensional isotropic turbulence utilizing spectral closures. J. Atmos. Sci. 38, 2747–2757. (The spectral eddy viscosity nu_e(kappakappa_c); the deferred spectral large-eddy rung, §7.)\nEswaran, V. & Pope, S. B. (1988). An examination of forcing in direct numerical simulations of turbulence. Computers & Fluids 16, 257–278. (The stochastic Ornstein–Uhlenbeck forcing.)\nLundgren, T. S. (2003). Linearly forced isotropic turbulence. CTR Annual Research Briefs, 461–473. (The linear negative-damping forcing.)\nBussi, G., Donadio, D. & Parrinello, M. (2007). Canonical sampling through velocity rescaling. J. Chem. Phys. 126, 014101. (Stochastic velocity rescaling; its deterministic limit is the Berendsen thermostat — no conserved quantity, no well-defined ensemble except in limiting cases — the caveat BandRescaling carries.)\nWilliamson, J. H. (1980). Low-storage Runge–Kutta schemes. J. Comput. Phys. 35, 48–56. (The 2N low-storage family; the package's third-order coefficients are Williamson's Eq. (11), his recommended case 7 — the set later adopted for spectral DNS and popularly named \"RKW3\".)\nPope, S. B. (2000). Turbulent Flows. Cambridge University Press. (Notation; the filtered-density-function Model and mixing models, §7–§8.)\nMori, H. (1965). Transport, collective motion, and Brownian motion. Prog. Theor. Phys. 33, 423–455; Zwanzig, R. (1973). Nonlinear generalized Langevin equations. J. Stat. Phys. 9, 215–220. (The projection formalism, the foundation of §3 and §8.)\nMorrison, P. J. (1986). A paradigm for joined Hamiltonian and dissipative systems. Physica D 18, 410–419. (The metriplectic bracket formulation; the reversible/irreversible split, §4.2, §8.)\nÖttinger, H. C. (2005). Beyond Equilibrium Thermodynamics. Wiley. (GENERIC; the reversible/irreversible split, §8.)","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#Spectra","page":"Spectra","text":"","title":"Spectra"},{"category":"section","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#The-composed-spectrum","page":"Spectra","text":"A model energy spectrum is an inertial core times a large-scale and a dissipation-range roll-off.","title":"The composed spectrum"},{"category":"section","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#Roll-off-factors","page":"Spectra","text":"The swappable large-scale and dissipation-range shapes, and Pope's forms.\n\nThe factor evaluators, extended by each roll-off form.","title":"Roll-off factors"},{"category":"section","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#Velocity-models","page":"Spectra","text":"The named velocity-spectrum presets of the composed ModelSpectrum.","title":"Velocity models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#Scalar-spectra","page":"Spectra","text":"The passive-scalar spectrum models.","title":"Scalar spectra"},{"category":"section","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#References","page":"Spectra","text":"The published provenance of each preset on this page, read from its provenance(law) — the extraction-bound leaf carries its own citations, so these are not duplicated in the ARCHITECTURE.md reference list.\n\nimport GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology as Phen\nimport GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra as Spec\nimport Markdown\nlaws = [Spec.Pope2000(), Spec.VonKarman1948(), Spec.Pao1965(), Spec.ObukhovCorrsin1949()]\ncites = unique([Phen.provenance(l).citation for l in laws])\nMarkdown.parse(join([\"- \" * c for c in cites], \"\\n\"))","title":"References"},{"category":"module","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra","page":"Spectra","text":"Phenomenology.Spectra\n\nModel spectra — parametrizations of the reference velocity spectrum E(kappa) and the scalar spectrum E_varphi(kappa), evaluated by spectrum. A velocity model is composed: an inertial-range core C_Kvarepsilon^23kappa^-53 times a large-scale roll-off and a dissipation-range roll-off, each swappable.\n\nModelSpectrum — the composed velocity spectrum: a Kolmogorov constant, a LargeScaleFactor, and a DissipationFactor.\nLargeScaleFactor / DissipationFactor — the swappable roll-off pieces, evaluated by large_scale_factor / dissipation_factor: PopeLargeScale / PopeDissipation are Pope's forms, VonKarmanLarge the classic kappa^4 energy range, PaoDissipation Pao's cutoff, ExponentialDissipation the closure-predicted exponential cutoff, SaffmanDissipation a Gaussian cutoff, and UnitLargeScale / UnitDissipation are the trivial (no-roll-off) factors.\nPope2000 / VonKarman1948 / Pao1965 — named velocity-spectrum presets of ModelSpectrum.\nScalarSpectrum — the passive-scalar spectra; ObukhovCorrsin1949 is the inertial-convective range.\n\nThe velocity-spectrum presets Pope2000 / VonKarman1948 / Pao1965 are factory functions that assemble and return a composed ModelSpectrum, whereas the scalar preset ObukhovCorrsin1949 — like the structure-function and scaling laws — is a concrete type with a keyword constructor; both are invoked identically as Name(; …).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.EnergySpectrum","page":"Spectra","text":"EnergySpectrum <: TurbulenceLaw\n\nSupertype of the velocity energy-spectrum models — parametrizations of E(kappa) evaluated by spectrum. The concrete composed form is ModelSpectrum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.EnergySpectrum"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.ModelSpectrum","page":"Spectra","text":"ModelSpectrum(C_K, large_scale, dissipation, ref)\n\nThe composed model energy spectrum\n\nE(kappa) = C_K varepsilon^23 kappa^-53 f_L(kappa L_11) f_eta(kappaeta)\n\nan inertial-range core with Kolmogorov constant C_K times a large-scale roll-off large_scale (a LargeScaleFactor, giving f_L) and a dissipation-range roll-off dissipation (a DissipationFactor, giving f_eta); ref is its Reference. Build one directly to mix roll-off forms, or use a preset such as Pope2000. Evaluated by spectrum(model, κ, fp), which reads varepsilon and the integral scale L_11 from fp and forms eta with kolmogorov_length.\n\nThis is the standard generic form for a model spectrum (Sagaut & Cambon 2018; Pope 2000; Meyers & Meneveau 2008); the large-scale roll-off sets the low-wavenumber slope E(kappato 0)proptokappa^sigma — sigma = 2 (the Saffman-type kappa^2 low-wavenumber spectrum, the PopeLargeScale default) or sigma = 4 (the Batchelor-type kappa^4, VonKarmanLarge).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.ModelSpectrum"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.LargeScaleFactor","page":"Spectra","text":"LargeScaleFactor\n\nSupertype of the dimensionless large-scale roll-off factors f_L(kappa L) of a composed ModelSpectrum — the shape that bends the inertial kappa^-53 law over into the energy-containing range, with f_L to 1 as kappa L to infty. Evaluated by large_scale_factor; PopeLargeScale is Pope's form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.LargeScaleFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.DissipationFactor","page":"Spectra","text":"DissipationFactor\n\nSupertype of the dimensionless dissipation-range roll-off factors f_eta(kappaeta) of a composed ModelSpectrum — the shape that cuts the spectrum off near the Kolmogorov scale, with f_eta to 1 as kappaeta to 0. Evaluated by dissipation_factor; PopeDissipation is Pope's form.\n\nnote: Bottleneck models deferred\nThese factors are inertial-normalized cutoffs. Models of the near-dissipation bottleneck — a bump before the cutoff (Qian 1984; Ishihara et al. 2005; Kraichnan 1959, all of the form A(kappaeta)^gammaexp(-betakappaeta) with gamma  0) — do not satisfy f_etato 1 and need a richer spectrum structure; they are deliberately deferred.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.DissipationFactor"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.PopeLargeScale","page":"Spectra","text":"PopeLargeScale(c_L, p₀)\n\nPope's large-scale roll-off f_L(kappa L) = left(frackappa Lsqrt(kappa L)^2 + c_Lright)^53 + p_0, which tends to 1 in the inertial range and rolls the spectrum off below the integral scale. c_L is set by the requirement that the spectrum integrate to the kinetic energy; p₀ fixes the low-wavenumber slope (Pope 2000).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.PopeLargeScale"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.PopeDissipation","page":"Spectra","text":"PopeDissipation(c_η, β)\n\nPope's dissipation-range roll-off f_eta(kappaeta) = expbig(-beta(kappaeta)^4 + c_eta^4^14 - c_etabig), which tends to 1 in the inertial range and cuts the spectrum off near the Kolmogorov scale. c_η is set by the requirement that the spectrum yield the dissipation rate; β fixes the sharpness of the cutoff (Pope 2000).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.PopeDissipation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.VonKarmanLarge","page":"Spectra","text":"VonKarmanLarge(c_L)\n\nThe von Kármán large-scale roll-off f_L(kappa L) = left(frac(kappa L)^2(kappa L)^2 + c_Lright)^176, which tends to 1 in the inertial range and produces the classic kappa^4 energy-containing range at low wavenumber (E sim kappa^-53f_L sim kappa^4); c_L fixes the peak scale (von Kármán 1948).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.VonKarmanLarge"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.UnitLargeScale","page":"Spectra","text":"UnitLargeScale()\n\nThe trivial large-scale factor f_L equiv 1 — no energy-containing roll-off, for a spectrum defined only over the inertial and dissipation ranges (the Pao1965 form).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.UnitLargeScale"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.UnitDissipation","page":"Spectra","text":"UnitDissipation()\n\nThe trivial dissipation-range factor f_eta equiv 1 — no viscous cutoff, for a spectrum defined only over the energy-containing and inertial ranges (the classic VonKarman1948 form).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.UnitDissipation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.PaoDissipation","page":"Spectra","text":"PaoDissipation(α)\n\nPao's dissipation-range roll-off f_eta(kappaeta) = expbig(-tfrac32 alpha (kappaeta)^43big), from the hypothesis that the spectral energy flux stays constant through the dissipation range; α is the Kolmogorov constant C_K (Pao 1965).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.PaoDissipation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.ExponentialDissipation","page":"Spectra","text":"ExponentialDissipation(β)\n\nThe pure-exponential dissipation-range roll-off f_eta(kappaeta) = exp(-beta kappaeta) — the n = 1 case of the general exp(-beta(kappaeta)^n) family, and the form the triadic spectral closures (EDQNM, DIA, LRA) consistently predict (Kaneda 1993; Sagaut & Cambon 2018). It is the far-dissipation form (kappaeta gg 1), where DNS resolving that range fit beta approx 67 (Khurshid, Donzis & Sreenivasan 2018; consistent with Chen et al. 1993, beta approx 71); the factor tends to 1 in the inertial range (kappaeta to 0). The near-dissipation range is instead a stretched exponential (exponent approx 23; Ishihara et al. 2005), a different form this factor does not model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.ExponentialDissipation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.SaffmanDissipation","page":"Spectra","text":"SaffmanDissipation()\n\nA Gaussian dissipation-range roll-off f_eta(kappaeta) = exp(-2(kappaeta)^2) — a simple, parameter-free cutoff that tends to 1 in the inertial range. The Gaussian form follows Saffman (1963, appendix; about half the earlier Townsend 1951 spectrum), whose coefficient is skewness-dependent (about 2.6 to 3.3) and is simplified downward to 2 here for a parameter-free, deliberately soft cutoff (retaining a little more near-cutoff energy than the physical range would).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.SaffmanDissipation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.large_scale_factor","page":"Spectra","text":"large_scale_factor(f, κL) -> Float64\n\nEvaluate the dimensionless large-scale roll-off f (a LargeScaleFactor) at the argument kappa L (κL). A new large-scale form is a subtype of LargeScaleFactor that adds a method here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.large_scale_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.dissipation_factor","page":"Spectra","text":"dissipation_factor(f, κη) -> Float64\n\nEvaluate the dimensionless dissipation-range roll-off f (a DissipationFactor) at the argument kappaeta (κη). A new dissipation-range form is a subtype of DissipationFactor that adds a method here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.dissipation_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.Pope2000","page":"Spectra","text":"Pope2000(; C_K = 1.5, c_L = 6.78, p₀ = 2.0, c_η = 0.40, β = 5.2)\n\nThe Pope (2000) model spectrum — a preset ModelSpectrum with PopeLargeScale and PopeDissipation roll-offs:\n\nE(kappa) = C_K varepsilon^23 kappa^-53 f_L(kappa L_11) f_eta(kappaeta)\n\nC_K is the Kolmogorov constant; the defaults c_L = 6.78, c_η = 0.40 are the high-Reynolds-number values Pope obtains from the energy and dissipation normalizations, with the shape exponents p₀ = 2 and β = 5.2. Override any coefficient to re-fit. Evaluate with spectrum(Pope2000(), κ, fp).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.Pope2000"},{"category":"function","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.VonKarman1948","page":"Spectra","text":"VonKarman1948(; C_K = 1.5, c_L = 1.0)\n\nThe von Kármán (1948) energy spectrum — a preset ModelSpectrum with the VonKarmanLarge roll-off and no viscous cutoff (UnitDissipation):\n\nE(kappa) = C_K varepsilon^23 kappa^-53\n             left(frac(kappa L_11)^2(kappa L_11)^2 + c_Lright)^176\n\nthe classic interpolation between the kappa^4 energy-containing range and the kappa^-53 inertial range, with no dissipation-range cutoff (use Pope2000 when the cutoff matters). C_K is the Kolmogorov constant; c_L fixes the peak scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.VonKarman1948"},{"category":"function","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.Pao1965","page":"Spectra","text":"Pao1965(; C_K = 1.5)\n\nThe Pao (1965) energy spectrum — a preset ModelSpectrum with no energy-containing roll-off (UnitLargeScale) and the PaoDissipation cutoff:\n\nE(kappa) = C_K varepsilon^23 kappa^-53 \n             expbig(-tfrac32 C_K (kappaeta)^43big)\n\ncovering the inertial and dissipation ranges from a constant-spectral-flux hypothesis — the Kolmogorov constant C_K appears in both the amplitude and the cutoff, so the cutoff carries no free constant (unlike Pope2000). It has no energy-containing range; use Pope2000 or VonKarman1948 when the large scales matter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.Pao1965"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.ScalarSpectrum","page":"Spectra","text":"ScalarSpectrum <: TurbulenceLaw\n\nSupertype of the passive-scalar spectrum models — parametrizations of E_varphi(kappa), the density in wavenumber of the scalar variance langlevarphi^2rangle, evaluated by spectrum. ObukhovCorrsin1949 is the inertial-convective range.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.ScalarSpectrum"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Spectra/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.ObukhovCorrsin1949","page":"Spectra","text":"ObukhovCorrsin1949(; C_OC = 0.67)\n\nThe Obukhov–Corrsin inertial-convective scalar spectrum\n\nE_varphi(kappa) = C_OC varepsilon_varphi varepsilon^-13 kappa^-53\n\nthe scalar analogue of the Kolmogorov -53 law: for a Schmidt number of order one the scalar's inertial-convective range coincides with the velocity inertial range. C_OC is the Obukhov–Corrsin constant (approx 067 for the three-dimensional spectrum), quoted — unlike Yaglom's classical 43 — for the full-variance scalar dissipation 2varkappalanglelvertnablavarphirvert^2rangle, so it pairs directly with this package's full-variance varepsilon_varphi (scalar_dissipation) with no factor-of-two conversion. The name is a dual eponym — Obukhov (1949) and Corrsin (independently, 1951); the 1949 handle is Obukhov's year. spectrum reads the scalar dissipation varepsilon_varphi (fp.scalar_dissipation, which must be set) and the kinetic-energy dissipation varepsilon from fp.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Spectra.ObukhovCorrsin1949"},{"category":"section","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#SimulationDomain","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"","title":"SimulationDomain"},{"category":"section","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#The-discretized-box","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"The reusable, read-only frame every buffer is allocated from — the FixedDomain, a fixed, unsheared periodic cube (a sheared frame is deferred).","title":"The discretized box"},{"category":"section","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#Dealiasing-strategies","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"The aliasing-control strategy passed to FixedDomain — the supertype, then the sharp 23-rule default, the smooth Hou–Li filter, and the exact half-cell phase-shift with spherical truncation.\n\nThe dealiasing cutoff it sets — the highest wavenumber the domain evolves, and the resolution criterion kappa_maxeta a run is built to meet.","title":"Dealiasing strategies"},{"category":"section","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#Scratch-field-allocators","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"Allocate one zeroed velocity, scalar, or second-order tensor field on the domain's backend and element type.","title":"Scratch-field allocators"},{"category":"section","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#Dealias-filtering","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"Apply the domain's dealias low-pass kernel to a spectral field in place.","title":"Dealias filtering"},{"category":"module","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"SimulationDomain\n\nThe discretized periodic box a run lives on — the reusable, read-only frame:\n\nFixedDomain — the fixed, unsheared cubic box (grid, transform plans, spectral multipliers, squared-wavenumber field, dealias kernel).\nDealiasingStrategy — the aliasing-control strategy, TwoThirds, HouLi, or PhaseShift, and the cutoff it sets, max_wavenumber.\nspectral_vector, scalar_spectral, … — the field-sized scratch allocators.\ndealias! — apply the dealias kernel to a field in spectral representation, in place.\n\nPrecision and backend enter here and flow everywhere from here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain"},{"category":"type","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.FixedDomain","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"FixedDomain(grid_points_per_axis, side_length;\n            dealiasing, element_type, array_type)\n\nThe discretized periodic box a run lives on — the reusable, read-only frame: a cube of side length side_length sampled with grid_points_per_axis points along each axis, holding everything the pseudospectral method needs on that box.\n\nArguments\n\nFixedDomain carries no defaults — every argument is explicit; the Simulation front door supplies the run-layer defaults (side_length = 2π, dealiasing = TwoThirds(), element_type = Float64, array_type = Array).\n\ngrid_points_per_axis — grid points N along each axis (the box is N^3).\nside_length — the physical side length L (the box is 0L)^3).\ndealiasing — the dealiasing strategy: TwoThirds (the sharp 23 rule) and HouLi (the smooth Hou–Li filter) apply dealias! as a coefficientwise multiply, while PhaseShift averages a half-cell-displaced evaluation (exact, keeping a fuller band at 2times the transforms). It controls the aliasing of the quadratic nonlinearity and sets the advective-CFL ceiling through recommended_cfl.\nelement_type — the real element type (Float32 is GPU-friendly).\narray_type — the storage backend: Array (CPU) or CuArray / ROCArray (GPU).\n\nFields\n\ngrid, layout — the Grid3D and its RealFourierLayout memory layout.\nvelocity_plans, scalar_plans, tensor_plans — the real-to-complex FieldTransformPlans, one per field rank: the velocity (a vector field), the scalar (a scalar field), and the rank-2 strain tensor the derivative-skewness diagnostic needs.\nmultipliers — the FieldSpectralMultipliers, the spectral first-derivative operators.\nsquared_wavenumber, squared_wavenumber_kernel — lvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2 (in spectral representation -nabla^2 acts as multiplication by it), kept both as a real array and as a scalar-field kernel for weighted spectral reductions.\ndealias, dealiasing — the dealias kernel and the DealiasingStrategy that built it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.FixedDomain"},{"category":"type","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.DealiasingStrategy","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"DealiasingStrategy\n\nHow the aliasing of the quadratic nonlinearity is controlled. A FixedDomain is built with one; it fixes the domain.dealias kernel and, through recommended_cfl, the advective-CFL ceiling. Concrete strategies: TwoThirds (the sharp 23 rule, the default), HouLi (the smooth Hou–Li filter), and PhaseShift (the half-cell shift-average with spherical 2sqrt23 truncation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.DealiasingStrategy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.TwoThirds","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"TwoThirds()\n\nThe sharp isotropic 23-rule truncation: keep the modes kappa le kappa_max = tfrac23kappa_mathrmNy and zero the rest, where kappa_mathrmNy = tfrac N2cdottfrac2piL is the Nyquist wavenumber and kappa_max the dealiased maximum — two-thirds of the way out to Nyquist. Exact dealiasing of the quadratic nonlinearity, and the lean, GPU-friendly default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.TwoThirds"},{"category":"type","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.HouLi","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"HouLi(; order = 36, strength = nothing)\n\nThe smooth Hou–Li spectral filter, transfer function expbig(- textttstrength (kappakappa_mathrmNy)^textttorderbig), with kappa_mathrmNy = tfrac N2cdottfrac2piL the Nyquist wavenumber. The order sets the sharpness of the spectral roll-off (higher is closer to a sharp cutoff); the strength sets the damping at the Nyquist mode, which is multiplied by mathrme^-textttstrength. By default (strength = nothing) strength is chosen for the domain's element type as -log of its machine epsilon — approx 36 for Float64, approx 16 for Float32 — so the Nyquist mode always damps to that precision's machine epsilon; pass an explicit strength to override. It keeps more modes than the 23 rule but only damps aliasing rather than removing it exactly — a stabilization, hence an opt-in, not the default. Applied isotropically in kappa. Because it retains energy out to the Nyquist wavenumber it takes a proportionally smaller CFL step than the 23 rule — handled automatically by recommended_cfl. The smooth spectral filter is due to Hou & Li (2007).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.HouLi"},{"category":"type","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.PhaseShift","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"PhaseShift()\n\nPhase-shift dealiasing: assemble each quadratic nonlinear term on the grid and on a grid displaced by half a cell, average the two, and truncate spherically at kappa_max = tfracsqrt23Ncdottfrac2piL = tfrac2sqrt23kappa_mathrmNy (with kappa_mathrmNy = tfrac N2cdottfrac2piL the Nyquist wavenumber). The half-cell average cancels the aliasing images that wrap in an odd number of axes; the spherical cutoff removes the surviving two-axis images. It dealiases the quadratic exactly while keeping the resolved band out to tfrac2sqrt23kappa_mathrmNy approx 094 kappa_mathrmNy — a factor sqrt2 further than the TwoThirds rule's tfrac23kappa_mathrmNy (approx 28times the modes in the retained sphere).\n\nUse it when that top third of wavenumbers is worth the price: a second nonlinear-term transform per stage (2times the transforms) and the displaced-evaluation scratch (a spectral field per phase-shifted term). Opt-in, not the default. Because it retains energy out to tfrac2sqrt23kappa_mathrmNy it takes a proportionally smaller advective-CFL step than the 23 rule — handled automatically by recommended_cfl.\n\nnote: The Nyquist mistranslation does not bite\nA half-cell displacement cannot translate the Nyquist mode of a real field — it attenuates it by cos(pi h_aDelta x_a), which vanishes at half a cell. This never bites here: the spherical cutoff at 2sqrt23 kappa_mathrmNy  kappa_mathrmNy leaves the field with no Nyquist content to mistranslate.\n\nPhase-shift dealiasing follows Patterson & Orszag (1971); the combination with spherical truncation is Rogallo (1981).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.PhaseShift"},{"category":"function","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.max_wavenumber","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"max_wavenumber(domain)\n\nThe highest wavenumber magnitude that domain actually evolves — the dealiasing cutoff\n\nkappa_max = f frac N2 frac2piL\n\nwith N the grid points per side, L the box side, and f the retained-Nyquist fraction of the DealiasingStrategy (23 for TwoThirds, 1 for HouLi, 2sqrt23 for PhaseShift). It is the cutoff the dealiasing sets — not the grid Nyquist tfrac N2tfrac2piL itself; the modes above it are zeroed each step. It fixes both the advective-CFL step (recommended_cfl) and the resolution criterion kappa_maxeta that a run is set up to meet.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.max_wavenumber"},{"category":"function","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.spectral_vector","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"spectral_vector(domain)\n\nAllocate a single zeroed vector field in spectral representation on domain's backend and element type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.spectral_vector"},{"category":"function","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.physical_vector","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"physical_vector(domain)\n\nAllocate a single zeroed vector field in physical representation on domain's backend and element type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.physical_vector"},{"category":"function","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.scalar_spectral","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"scalar_spectral(domain)\n\nAllocate a single zeroed scalar field in spectral representation on domain's backend and element type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.scalar_spectral"},{"category":"function","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.scalar_physical","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"scalar_physical(domain)\n\nAllocate a single zeroed scalar field in physical representation on domain's backend and element type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.scalar_physical"},{"category":"function","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.tensor_spectral","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"tensor_spectral(domain)\n\nAllocate a single zeroed second-order tensor field in spectral representation on domain's backend and element type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.tensor_spectral"},{"category":"function","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.tensor_physical","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"tensor_physical(domain)\n\nAllocate a single zeroed second-order tensor field in physical representation on domain's backend and element type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.tensor_physical"},{"category":"function","location":"api/SimulationDomain/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.dealias!","page":"SimulationDomain","text":"dealias!(field, domain)\n\nApply the run's dealias low-pass domain.dealias to field in place — a coefficientwise multiply that controls the aliasing of the quadratic nonlinearity. The kernel is whatever the run's DealiasingStrategy built: the sharp 23 cutoff (TwoThirds, exact dealiasing), the smooth HouLi filter (which only damps the aliasing), or the spherical 2sqrt23 kappa_mathrmNy truncation (PhaseShift). The field may be a vector or scalar field in spectral representation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain.dealias!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Generator/#Generator","page":"Generator","text":"","title":"Generator"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Generator/#Flow-conditions","page":"Generator","text":"The physical conditions the generator is built from — viscosity, Schmidt number, and the velocity and scalar forcings.","title":"Flow conditions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Generator/#The-reified-generator","page":"Generator","text":"The right-hand side as one object — the conditions bundled with the assembly scratch its evaluations write through. A time-marching scheme consumes it only through the protocol below.","title":"The reified generator"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Generator/#The-generator-protocol","page":"Generator","text":"One step of the dynamics, as the scheme sees it: open with prepare!, evaluate the right-hand sides and the exact linear factors at each stage, close with finalize!.","title":"The generator protocol"},{"category":"module","location":"api/Generator/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator","page":"Generator","text":"Generator\n\nThe flow's equations of motion — the incompressible Navier–Stokes right-hand side in rotational form (the dealiased, Leray-projected nonlinear term), the passive-scalar advection, and the exact viscous integrating factor.\n\nIts contents (each formula lives in the operation's own docstring):\n\nFlowConditions — the physical conditions the right-hand side is built from (the viscosity, the Schmidt number, the forcings).\nResolvedGenerator — the reified generator: the conditions bundled with the assembly scratch its evaluations write through, consumed by a scheme through the protocol below.\nprepare! — open a step: advance the forcings' internal state and arm the linear rates.\nvelocity_rhs!, scalar_rhs! — assemble the explicit right-hand sides (the dealiased, Leray-projected momentum term and the scalar advection, each plus its additive forcing source) at a stage state.\nvelocity_factor!, scalar_factor! — build each field's exact linear subflow (the viscous/diffusive integrating factor, with any linear forcing rate folded in).\nfinalize! — close a step: apply the post-step forcing projections to the advanced state.\n\nThe physical-representation recoveries the assemblies consume (velocity_to_physical! and its siblings) live with the state, in State.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Generator/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator.FlowConditions","page":"Generator","text":"FlowConditions(; viscosity, schmidt, velocity_forcing, scalar_forcing)\n\nThe physical conditions the generator is built from — the kinematic viscosity nu (viscosity; the Reynolds number is mathrmRe = 1nu), the Schmidt number mathrmSc (schmidt; the scalar diffusivity is varkappa = numathrmSc), and the velocity and scalar forcing strategies (velocity_forcing, scalar_forcing, both AbstractForcing). Consumed by the time step (step!).\n\nEvery argument is explicit — FlowConditions carries no defaults; the Simulation front door supplies the run-layer values (schmidt = 1.0, a stationary BandRescaling velocity forcing, and NoForcing() for the scalar).\n\nFields\n\nviscosity, schmidt — the kinematic viscosity nu and the Schmidt number mathrmSc (both Float64).\nvelocity_forcing, scalar_forcing — the velocity and scalar forcing strategies, each an AbstractForcing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator.FlowConditions"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Generator/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator.ResolvedGenerator","page":"Generator","text":"ResolvedGenerator(domain, conditions; scalar = false)\n\nThe reified right-hand side of the resolved dynamics — the flow's generator as one object: the FlowConditions parameter record together with the assembly scratch its evaluations write through (the quadratic-product fields, the displaced-evaluation buffers when the domain dealiases by PhaseShift, and the per-mode forcing-rate arrays). A time-marching scheme consumes it through the generator protocol — prepare! once per step, velocity_rhs! / scalar_rhs! at each stage state, velocity_factor! / scalar_factor! for the exact linear subflows, finalize! after the completed step — and itself does only stage arithmetic, so a scheme can never assemble the right-hand side from a mismatched intermediate state.\n\nBuild it on the FixedDomain domain from the conditions; pass scalar = true when the run carries a passive scalar (it allocates the scalar's forcing-rate array, required for a scalar-forced run). The Simulation front door builds one for you.\n\nFields\n\nconditions — the FlowConditions parameter record (nu, mathrmSc, the forcings).\nproduct_physical, product_spectral — the quadratic products in physical and spectral representation (the Lamb vector boldsymbolutimesboldsymbolomega, and the scalar flux boldsymboluvarphi).\nphase_shift — the displaced-evaluation scratch and translation kernels (PhaseShift dealiasing only), else nothing.\nvelocity_rate, scalar_rate — the per-mode linear forcing-rate arrays A(boldsymbolkappa) (prepare! fills them). Each is nothing unless that field's forcing declares uses_linear_rate, so a field driven by a post-step projection, a stochastic source, or nothing at all carries no rate storage; scalar_rate is also nothing for a scalar-free generator.\nvelocity_rate_active, scalar_rate_active — whether prepare! armed each rate this step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator.ResolvedGenerator"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Generator/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator.prepare!","page":"Generator","text":"prepare!(generator, state, domain, dt)\n\nOpen a step of the generator: advance each forcing's internal state once (its per-step prepare_forcing! hook) and arm the per-mode linear forcing rates (its set_forcing_rate! hook, written into the generator's rate arrays) from the start-of-step state (a ResolvedFields) over the timestep dt, on the FixedDomain domain. A scheme calls this once before its Runge–Kutta stages; the stage evaluations and the integrating factors then consume what it prepared. Returns generator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator.prepare!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Generator/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator.velocity_rhs!","page":"Generator","text":"velocity_rhs!(N̂, generator, û, u, ω, domain)\n\nAssemble the explicit right-hand side of the momentum equation into N̂ — the dealiased, Leray-projected rotational nonlinear term plus any additive forcing source — from the stage velocity û in spectral representation and its physical velocity u and vorticity ω, using the ResolvedGenerator generator's conditions and scratch.\n\nIn physical space the rotational form writes the convective term as the Lamb vector, through the identity\n\n(boldsymbolucdotnabla)boldsymbolu = nablabig(tfrac12lvertboldsymbolurvert^2big) - boldsymbolutimesboldsymbolomega\n\nso that, with the Leray–Helmholtz projection mathbbP onto divergence-free fields absorbing the pressure and the nabla(tfrac12lvertboldsymbolurvert^2) gradient, the momentum equation becomes\n\npartial_tboldsymbolu = mathbbP (boldsymbolutimesboldsymbolomega) + nunabla^2boldsymbolu + boldsymbolf\n\nThe term computed is the spectral image of mathbbP (boldsymbolutimesboldsymbolomega), dealiased by the domain's DealiasingStrategy, plus the per-stage additive source of the generator's velocity forcing (its add_forcing_source! hook). A mask strategy (TwoThirds, HouLi) forms the term once from u, ω and multiplies by the low-pass domain.dealias, leaving û unused; PhaseShift forms it twice — from u, ω, and from the fields recovered on a grid displaced by half a cell (using û and the generator's displaced-evaluation scratch) — averages the two to cancel the aliasing, then truncates spherically with domain.dealias.\n\nTwo linear pieces are deliberately not in N̂: the viscous term and the exactly-linear part of a forcing are advanced exactly by the integrating factor (velocity_factor!). The Lamb vector and its transform are held in the generator's own product scratch, so the operation allocates nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator.velocity_rhs!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Generator/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator.scalar_rhs!","page":"Generator","text":"scalar_rhs!(N̂, generator, û, φ̂, u, φ, domain)\n\nAssemble the explicit right-hand side of the passive-scalar equation into N̂ — the dealiased advection term plus any additive forcing source — from the stage velocity û and scalar φ̂ in spectral representation and their physical u, φ, using the ResolvedGenerator generator's conditions and scratch.\n\nIn conservative form the advection is -nablacdot(boldsymboluvarphi) (equal to -boldsymbolucdotnablavarphi since nablacdotboldsymbolu = 0); the conservative form conserves the scalar mean exactly — the divergence has no boldsymbolkappa=boldsymbol 0 component, and the dealias kernel domain.dealias (the sharp 23 rule by default) leaves that mode untouched.\n\nIt is computed pseudospectrally as - mathrmdealiasbig(mathrmiboldsymbolkappacdotwidehatboldsymboluvarphibig), plus the per-stage additive source of the generator's scalar forcing (its add_forcing_source! hook). The dealiasing strategy controls the aliasing of the quadratic product: a mask strategy (TwoThirds, HouLi) forms the flux once from u, φ, leaving û and φ̂ unused; PhaseShift instead averages it with the flux recovered on a grid displaced half a cell — using û, φ̂, and the generator's displaced-evaluation scratch — before the spherical truncation. The diffusion varkappanabla^2varphi and the exactly-linear part of a forcing are not in N̂: they are advanced exactly by scalar_factor!.\n\nThe scalar flux boldsymboluvarphi reuses the generator's momentum product scratch, so the operation allocates nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator.scalar_rhs!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Generator/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator.velocity_factor!","page":"Generator","text":"velocity_factor!(ℰ, generator, domain, τ)\n\nWrite the velocity's exact linear subflow over a substep tau (τ) into ℰ (a scalar field in spectral representation). The linear part of the momentum equation — the diffusion partial_tboldsymbolu = nunabla^2boldsymbolu, plus any linear forcing rate A(boldsymbolkappa) that prepare! armed — is solved exactly in spectral representation by the per-mode multiplier\n\nmathcalE(boldsymbolkappa) = expbig((A(boldsymbolkappa) - nulvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2) taubig)\n\nwith A = 0 when no linear forcing is active, so multiplying a field by ℰ advances its viscous (and linear-forcing) part exactly — machine-exact, with no stability restriction; only a scheme's explicit stage combination carries truncation error. The ResolvedGenerator generator supplies nu and the armed rate; the domain (a FixedDomain) supplies the squared-wavenumber field lvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2 — in spectral representation -nabla^2 acts as multiplication by it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator.velocity_factor!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Generator/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator.scalar_factor!","page":"Generator","text":"scalar_factor!(ℰ, generator, domain, τ)\n\nWrite the passive scalar's exact linear subflow over a substep tau into ℰ — the same per-mode multiplier as velocity_factor! with the scalar diffusivity varkappa = numathrmSc in place of nu and the scalar forcing's armed linear rate in place of the velocity's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator.scalar_factor!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Generator/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator.finalize!","page":"Generator","text":"finalize!(generator, state, domain)\n\nClose a step of the generator: apply each forcing's post-step projection (its apply_forcing! hook — e.g. the energy rescale of a BandRescaling) to the advanced state, velocity first, then the scalar when the run carries one. A scheme calls this once after its final stage, so everything that follows the step — the diagnostics, the derived charts, the tracer push — sees the forced end-of-step fields. Returns state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator.finalize!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-overview","page":"Overview","text":"The package is an umbrella module (GBGIsotropicTurbulence) assembled from submodules. Its re-exported submodules, in dependency order, are SimulationDomain → State → Forcing → Generator → Observables → Propagator → Particles → SimulationRun; the extraction-bound Phenomenology leaf leads the include order but is not re-exported (reach it opt-in). The umbrella re-exports the public surface of the listed submodules, so using GBGIsotropicTurbulence brings everything into scope.\n\nGBGIsotropicTurbulence: the top-level module and public re-export surface.\nSimulationDomain: the discretized periodic box a run lives on — the FixedDomain (with its choice of DealiasingStrategy), the scratch-field allocators, and the resolved-wavenumber ceiling max_wavenumber.\nState: the evolving state (ResolvedFields — the velocity and scalar in spectral representation, the single source of truth), the physical-representation recoveries, the DerivedFields observation cache (stamped physical velocity/vorticity charts with recompute-on-staleness accessors), and the random initial conditions that seed it — a divergence-free velocity and a mean-zero scalar.\nForcing: the interchangeable energy-injection strategies under AbstractForcing, chosen independently for the velocity and the scalar.\nGenerator: the flow's equations of motion — the rotational-form Navier–Stokes right-hand side (dealiased, Leray-projected nonlinear term), the passive-scalar advection, the exact viscous integrating factor, and the reified ResolvedGenerator — the FlowConditions (nu, the Schmidt number, the forcings) bundled with the assembly scratch behind the protocol a scheme consumes. The physical-representation recoveries it consumes live with the State.\nObservables: the measured functions of state — kinetic energy, dissipation, the velocity-derivative skewness, the scalar variance and dissipation, the maximum velocity component that sets the CFL step, and the measured energy spectrum and integral scale.\nPropagator: the integrating-factor time-stepping schemes under AbstractIntegrator — IFRK2 (Heun) and the low-storage IFLowStorageRK3.\nParticles: Lagrangian tracer tracking — Tracers, second-order advect! (Heun-in-time), and sample! for the Lagrangian statistics.\nSimulationRun: the ergonomic run layer — Simulation and the functions that advance it (advance!, run!, warmup!), report its measured flow state (flow_parameters), and compare its measured energy spectrum to a Phenomenology model (validate_spectrum).\n\nThe reference turbulence laws (model spectra, structure-function laws, scaling constants) live in a separate, opt-in Phenomenology group — not re-exported by the umbrella, and designed to be extractable into a standalone package.\n\nPages = [\n  \"api/GBGIsotropicTurbulence.md\",\n  \"api/SimulationDomain.md\",\n  \"api/State.md\",\n  \"api/Forcing.md\",\n  \"api/Generator.md\",\n  \"api/Observables.md\",\n  \"api/Propagator.md\",\n  \"api/Particles.md\",\n  \"api/SimulationRun.md\",\n]\nDepth = 2","title":"API overview"},{"category":"section","location":"api/GBGIsotropicTurbulence/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence","page":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence","text":"The umbrella module: it sets the include order and re-exports the public surface of the submodules.","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence"},{"category":"section","location":"api/GBGIsotropicTurbulence/#Index","page":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence","text":"Modules = [GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationDomain, GBGIsotropicTurbulence.State, GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Generator, GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Observables, GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing, GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Propagator, GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles, GBGIsotropicTurbulence.SimulationRun]","title":"Index"},{"category":"module","location":"api/GBGIsotropicTurbulence/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.GBGIsotropicTurbulence","page":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence","text":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence\n\nTools for simulating and analyzing homogeneous isotropic turbulence on three-dimensional periodic domains: a velocity field and a single passive scalar, each optionally forced, advanced by a choice of integrating-factor time-stepping scheme, with Lagrangian particle tracking and field sampling for the forthcoming Eulerian + Lagrangian statistics.\n\nBuilt on GBGPeriodicFields.\n\nThe GBG part in the name comes from the city of Göteborg (Gothenburg, Sweden), where the primary development took place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.GBGIsotropicTurbulence"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Forcing/#Forcing","page":"Forcing","text":"","title":"Forcing"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Forcing/#Forcing-strategies","page":"Forcing","text":"The forcing supertype and its concrete strategies: the unforced baseline, deterministic band rescaling, and the two linear-rate forcings. One implementation serves either the velocity or the scalar.","title":"Forcing strategies"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Forcing/#Deferred-forcing","page":"Forcing","text":"The operator-free deferred form a strategy's constructor returns, materialized into a concrete forcing once the domain exists.","title":"Deferred forcing"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Forcing/#Forcing-methods","page":"Forcing","text":"The four step methods a strategy may implement (each a no-op by default), listed in the order step! fires them, followed by the trait that declares a strategy supplies a linear rate — part of implementing set_forcing_rate!, since the generator allocates a rate array only where one is declared.","title":"Forcing methods"},{"category":"module","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing","page":"Forcing","text":"Forcing\n\nThe interchangeable energy-injection strategies, concrete AbstractForcing subtypes. A strategy acts on the step through up to four methods, each defaulting to doing nothing:\n\nset_forcing_rate! — a linear forcing rate folded into the integrating factor; uses_linear_rate declares that a forcing supplies one.\napply_forcing! — a post-step projection or rescale.\nprepare_forcing! — a per-step internal-state advance.\nadd_forcing_source! — an additive per-stage source.\n\nThe strategies are NoForcing, BandRescaling, Linear, and ConstantPower; one implementation serves both the velocity (a vector field) and the scalar (a scalar field), through tensor-order-generic field operators. The velocity and scalar are forced independently: FlowConditions takes a separate velocity_forcing and scalar_forcing (each an AbstractForcing), so a run can force the velocity while the scalar decays freely, force both, or leave either unforced.\n\nAn operator-free keyword constructor (BandRescaling(; …), Linear(; …), ConstantPower(; …)) returns a DeferredForcing that a Simulation materializes once the domain exists.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.AbstractForcing","page":"Forcing","text":"AbstractForcing\n\nSupertype of the energy-injection strategies. A strategy may implement any of set_forcing_rate! (a linear forcing rate folded into the integrating factor), apply_forcing! (a post-step projection), prepare_forcing! (a per-step state advance), and add_forcing_source! (an additive per-stage source); all default to doing nothing, and a strategy owns whatever scratch its hooks need.\n\nThe hooks form one per-step lifecycle with a fixed order: prepare_forcing! and set_forcing_rate! run once before the Runge–Kutta stages (the state a later hook consumes must be prepared here), add_forcing_source! runs at every stage, and apply_forcing! runs after the completed step. The linear rate and the additive source are two channels of one forcing term — the part linear in the field is folded exactly into the integrating factor, the remainder is added at the stages — so a single strategy may legitimately use both.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.AbstractForcing"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.NoForcing","page":"Forcing","text":"NoForcing()\n\nThe unforced case: all four methods keep their defaults (do nothing) — a freely decaying field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.NoForcing"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.BandRescaling","page":"Forcing","text":"BandRescaling(domain; target, over = :band, lowest_shell = 1, highest_shell = 2)\n\nDeterministic rescaling forcing (post-step) that holds a field's amplitude fixed. Each step the in-band Fourier modes (integer shells textttlowest_shell le kappa le texttthighest_shell of 2piL) are rescaled by a single factor to hold the target.\n\nThe target is the observable the forcing holds, chosen to agree with the diagnostics: the kinetic energy tfrac12langlelvertboldsymbolurvert^2rangle (kinetic_energy) for the velocity, the scalar variance langlevarphi^2rangle (scalar_variance) for the scalar — the field's L^2 observable, carrying the tfrac12 only for the vector.\n\nThe over keyword selects where the target applies:\n\n:band (default) — hold the band's own value; the whole-field value then floats, oscillating slowly about its mean as the band feeds the cascade.\n:total — rescale the band to absorb the whole-field deficit so the whole-field value equals target (for the velocity this pins the r.m.s. velocity u and hence mathrmRe_lambda). If the out-of-band value alone exceeds target, the band is zeroed (the closest reachable state).\n\nRescaling preserves the divergence-free constraint either way.\n\nnote: A deterministic clamp, not a faithful stationary sampler\nHolding an amplitude fixed by deterministic rescaling is the Berendsen-thermostat limit of stochastic velocity rescaling: it has no conserved quantity, is not associated with a well-defined ensemble except in limiting cases, and suppresses the physical fluctuations of the quantity it holds (Bussi, Donadio & Parrinello 2007). Use it to spin up or to pin an amplitude; when the held quantity is itself measured, prefer a fixed-injection forcing (ConstantPower), which leaves the energy free to fluctuate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.BandRescaling"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.Linear","page":"Forcing","text":"Linear(domain; rate, lowest_shell = 1, highest_shell = 2)\n\nLinear (negative-damping) forcing f = A textttfield in a low-wavenumber band (a band-limited variant of Lundgren 2003, whose linear forcing acts at every wavenumber), with the fixed rate A = textttrate on shells textttlowest_shell le kappa le texttthighest_shell. The rate is folded into the integrating factor (exact for the linear term), so the forced band modes grow at net rate A - Dlvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2 (D the field's diffusivity). A positive rate amplifies the field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.Linear"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.ConstantPower","page":"Forcing","text":"ConstantPower(domain; target, lowest_shell = 1, highest_shell = 2)\n\nConstant-injection-rate forcing: a banded linear forcing f = A textttfield on integer shells textttlowest_shell le kappa le texttthighest_shell of 2piL (default 1–2), whose rate A is set each step so the field's injection rate holds the target. In stationarity the injection balances dissipation, so target is the dissipation rate, chosen to agree with the diagnostics: dissipation varepsilon for the velocity, scalar_dissipation varepsilon_varphi for the scalar. The rate is folded into the integrating factor; like Linear, it preserves a solenoidal velocity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.ConstantPower"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.DeferredForcing","page":"Forcing","text":"DeferredForcing\n\nA deferred forcing. The operator-free keyword constructors — Linear(; rate, …), ConstantPower(; target, …), and BandRescaling(; target, over, …) — return a DeferredForcing capturing the same keywords as their domain-taking forms (the band lowest_shell / highest_shell, and BandRescaling's over); it is materialized into the concrete AbstractForcing once the FixedDomain exists (done for you by Simulation). A forcing argument therefore accepts either a ready-built AbstractForcing (from the domain-taking constructor) or a DeferredForcing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.DeferredForcing"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.prepare_forcing!","page":"Forcing","text":"prepare_forcing!(forcing, field, domain, dt) -> nothing\n\nUpdate a forcing's internal state once per step, before the Runge–Kutta stages, from the start-of-step field (in spectral representation) and the timestep dt, on the FixedDomain domain. A stateful forcing owns whatever scratch this needs. The default does nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.prepare_forcing!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.set_forcing_rate!","page":"Forcing","text":"set_forcing_rate!(forcing, rate, field, domain) -> Bool\n\nFor a forcing linear in the field, fill the real per-mode rate array rate with A(boldsymbolkappa) (the forcing f = A textttfield) and return true; the integrator folds it into the diffusive integrating factor mathcal E(boldsymbolkappa) (see velocity_factor!), so the forced modes advance at the net linear rate A(boldsymbolkappa) - Dlvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2 (D the field's diffusivity). Return false (the default) when the forcing contributes no linear rate. The field is the start-of-step field in spectral representation; domain is the FixedDomain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.set_forcing_rate!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.add_forcing_source!","page":"Forcing","text":"add_forcing_source!(forcing, nonlinear, domain) -> nonlinear\n\nAdd an additive forcing source to the dealiased nonlinear-term buffer nonlinear at each Runge–Kutta stage (the source is held fixed over the step, so it integrates at the scheme's order), on the FixedDomain domain. The default does nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.add_forcing_source!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.apply_forcing!","page":"Forcing","text":"apply_forcing!(forcing, field, domain) -> field\n\nApply a post-step forcing to field (a velocity or scalar varphi field in spectral representation) in place, on the FixedDomain domain. The default does nothing; see BandRescaling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.apply_forcing!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Forcing/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.uses_linear_rate","page":"Forcing","text":"uses_linear_rate(forcing) -> Bool\n\nWhether forcing drives its field through a linear per-mode rate — that is, whether it implements set_forcing_rate!. false by default; a forcing that does implement the hook must declare it true.\n\nThe generator allocates a rate array A(boldsymbolkappa) per field only for a forcing that declares this, so an unforced field, a post-step projection, or a stochastic source carries no rate storage at all. Declaring it is therefore part of implementing set_forcing_rate!, not an optimization: a forcing that fills a rate without declaring one is never given an array to fill, and the constructor rejects it rather than let the forcing go silently unapplied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Forcing.uses_linear_rate"},{"category":"section","location":"api/phenomenology/Scalings/#Scalings","page":"Scalings","text":"","title":"Scalings"},{"category":"section","location":"api/phenomenology/Scalings/#Laws","page":"Scalings","text":"The dimensionless scaling constants and their Reynolds-number trends, evaluated by scaling.","title":"Laws"},{"category":"section","location":"api/phenomenology/Scalings/#Predicting-dissipation","page":"Scalings","text":"Invert a box-normalized dissipation law to the dissipation a stationary run sustains — the seam the run layer uses to set the viscosity from the grid and target energy.","title":"Predicting dissipation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/phenomenology/Scalings/#References","page":"Scalings","text":"The published provenance of each law on this page, read from its provenance(law) — the extraction-bound leaf carries its own citations, so these are not duplicated in the ARCHITECTURE.md reference list.\n\nimport GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology as Phen\nimport GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Scalings as Scal\nimport Markdown\nlaws = [Scal.Sreenivasan1998(), Scal.Sreenivasan1995(), Scal.Li2008()]\ncites = unique([Phen.provenance(l).citation for l in laws])\nMarkdown.parse(join([\"- \" * c for c in cites], \"\\n\"))","title":"References"},{"category":"module","location":"api/phenomenology/Scalings/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Scalings","page":"Scalings","text":"Phenomenology.Scalings\n\nDimensionless turbulence constants and their Reynolds-number trends, evaluated by scaling.\n\nSreenivasan1998 — the normalized dissipation constant C_varepsilon = varepsilon Lu^3, a high-Reynolds-number plateau with an optional finite-Reynolds-number correction.\nSreenivasan1995 — the (three-dimensional) Kolmogorov constant C_K of the inertial-range spectrum.\nLi2008 — the box-normalized dissipation group k(Lvarepsilon)^23, which (inverted, via stationary_dissipation) predicts the dissipation a stationary run sustains from its energy and box side length.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Scalings"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Scalings/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Scalings.ScalingLaw","page":"Scalings","text":"ScalingLaw <: TurbulenceLaw\n\nSupertype of the laws for a dimensionless turbulence constant — a pure number characterizing developed turbulence (the normalized dissipation C_varepsilon, the Kolmogorov constant C_K, the derivative skewness, …), possibly Reynolds-number-dependent. A law predicts the constant via scaling(law, fp); the measured value comes from a run, and the comparison is prediction against measurement.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Scalings.ScalingLaw"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Scalings/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Scalings.Sreenivasan1998","page":"Scalings","text":"Sreenivasan1998(; asymptote = 0.5, correction = 0.0)\n\nThe normalized dissipation constant C_varepsilon = varepsilon Lu^3 as a high-Reynolds-number plateau with an optional finite-Reynolds-number correction,\n\nC_varepsilon(mathrmRe_lambda) = C_varepsiloninftyleft(1 + fracamathrmRe_lambdaright)\n\nwith asymptote the plateau C_varepsiloninfty and correction the coefficient a of the 1mathrmRe_lambda correction. Sreenivasan (1998) established that C_varepsilon approaches a constant of order 05 (the default; the precise value depends on the integral-scale definition) above mathrmRe_lambda sim 50. The correction defaults to 0 (the pure plateau); set it to a positive value to model the low-Reynolds-number rise. scaling(law, fp) reads mathrmRe_lambda from fp via taylor_reynolds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Scalings.Sreenivasan1998"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Scalings/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Scalings.Sreenivasan1995","page":"Scalings","text":"Sreenivasan1995(; C_K = 1.5)\n\nThe three-dimensional Kolmogorov constant C_K of the inertial-range energy spectrum E(kappa) = C_K varepsilon^23kappa^-53 — the amplitude a Spectra model uses. Reviewing the experimental and numerical record, Sreenivasan (1995) found it close to universal, a reliable estimate being C_K = 15 pm 01 (the default; Sagaut & Cambon 2018), with no significant Reynolds-number trend; scaling(law, fp) returns it, independent of fp.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Scalings.Sreenivasan1995"},{"category":"type","location":"api/phenomenology/Scalings/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Scalings.Li2008","page":"Scalings","text":"Li2008(; constant = 0.9)\n\nThe box-normalized dissipation group\n\nG = frack(Lvarepsilon)^23 approx 09\n\nrelating the turbulent kinetic energy k, the periodic-box side length L, and the dissipation rate varepsilon — nearly Reynolds-number-independent from the Johns Hopkins Turbulence Database (JHTDB) forced-isotropic dataset (mathrmRe_lambda approx 433) and consistent with higher-Reynolds-number periodic-box DNS. It is a distinct group from the classical dissipation anomaly C_varepsilon = varepsilon L_11u^3 (Taylor 1935; Sreenivasan 1998; Kaneda et al. 2003 for the periodic box), whose high-Reynolds-number plateau C_varepsilon approx 05 is normalized on the integral scale L_11, not the box side L. Formed on a common length the two are exactly related by G = tfrac32 C_varepsilon^-23 (using u^2 = 2k3); but the quoted G approx 09 (box side) and C_varepsilon approx 05 (integral scale) sit at different lengths (LL_11 approx 5), so they must not be substituted into that identity directly.\n\nThe constant is the group value G (default 0.9); scaling(law, fp) returns it; inverted, stationary_dissipation(law, energy, side_length) predicts the dissipation a stationary run of energy k in a box L sustains, varepsilon = (kG)^32L — the seam the run layer uses to set the viscosity from the grid and target energy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Scalings.Li2008"},{"category":"function","location":"api/phenomenology/Scalings/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Scalings.stationary_dissipation","page":"Scalings","text":"stationary_dissipation(law, energy, side_length)\n\nThe dissipation rate varepsilon a stationary run sustains, predicted by a box-normalized dissipation law from the turbulent kinetic energy energy (k) and the periodic-box side length side_length (L) — the inverse of the law's scaling group (each law owns its own inverse; Li2008 gives the (kG)^32L form). This is the seam by which a run's viscosity can be set from its grid and target energy, rather than the reverse.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Phenomenology.Scalings.stationary_dissipation"},{"category":"section","location":"#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.jl","page":"Home","text":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.jl is a small, focused pseudospectral direct-numerical-simulation (DNS) package for homogeneous isotropic turbulence on a three-dimensional periodic domain: an incompressible velocity field boldsymbolu together with a single passive scalar varphi, each optionally forced, advanced by a choice of integrating-factor time-stepping scheme, with Lagrangian particle tracking and field sampling.\n\nIt is built on GBGPeriodicFields, which supplies every field operation — transforms, spectral differential operators, convolution, interpolation, and reductions. The GPU is the primary target, so each time step is in-place and allocation-free over preallocated buffers (occasional diagnostics allocate per call).","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"This is a private, unregistered package. With repository access:\n\nusing Pkg\n\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/GrigorySarnitsky/GBGIsotropicTurbulence.jl\")\n\nThen:\n\nusing GBGIsotropicTurbulence","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"Build a case at the front door, spin it up to stationarity, and advance it:\n\nusing GBGIsotropicTurbulence\n\nsim = Simulation(grid_points_per_axis = 64)   # a 64³ forced-stationary run;\n                                              # ν is chosen from the resolution criterion\nwarmup!(sim)                                  # spin up to statistical stationarity\nrun!(sim; turnovers = 5)                      # advance five eddy-turnover times\n\nkinetic_energy(sim.state.velocity)            # read a measured observable off the state\nflow_parameters(sim)                          # the run's measured flow state","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-it-solves","page":"Home","text":"With boldsymbolu the velocity, boldsymbolomega = nablatimesboldsymbolu the vorticity, nu the kinematic viscosity, mathbbP the Leray–Helmholtz projection onto divergence-free fields, and boldsymbolf a forcing, the momentum equation is advanced in rotational form (its advective term carried by the Lamb vector boldsymbolutimesboldsymbolomega),\n\npartial_t boldsymbolu = mathbbP (boldsymbolutimesboldsymbolomega) + nunabla^2boldsymbolu + boldsymbolf\nqquad nablacdotboldsymbolu = 0\n\nand the passive scalar varphi by the advection–diffusion equation\n\npartial_t varphi = -nablacdot(boldsymbolu varphi) + fracnumathrmScnabla^2varphi + f_varphi\n\nwith mathrmSc the Schmidt number and f_varphi a forcing on the scalar. In both equations the diffusive (stiff) term is integrated exactly through an integrating factor, the nonlinear term explicitly by a Runge–Kutta stage combination, and the quadratic product is dealiased by the default 23 rule (other strategies are available).\n\nnote: Work in progress\nThe package is being built in vertical slices. The coupled velocity + passive-scalar solver is in place, advanced by the integrating-factor schemes (IFLowStorageRK3 — the default — and IFRK2), with the forcing menu NoForcing, BandRescaling, Linear, and ConstantPower, and Lagrangian particle tracking (Tracers). The reference turbulence laws (Phenomenology) provide usable model spectra, structure-function laws, and scaling constants; the Eulerian and Lagrangian statistics are being added.","title":"What it solves"},{"category":"section","location":"#Components","page":"Home","text":"SimulationDomain: the discretized periodic box a run lives on — the FixedDomain, its choice of DealiasingStrategy, and the scratch-field allocators.\nState: the evolving state (ResolvedFields, the single source of truth) a step advances, its physical recoveries, the DerivedFields observation cache (stamped charts of the physical velocity and vorticity, recomputed exactly when stale), and the random initial conditions — a divergence-free velocity and a mean-zero scalar.\nForcing: the interchangeable energy-injection strategies under AbstractForcing, chosen independently for the velocity and the scalar.\nGenerator: the flow's equations of motion — the rotational-form Navier–Stokes right-hand side, the passive-scalar advection, and the exact viscous integrating factor — reified as the ResolvedGenerator a scheme consumes through the generator protocol.\nObservables: the measured functions of state (kinetic_energy, dissipation, derivative_skewness, scalar_variance, scalar_dissipation, the Courant–Friedrichs–Lewy (CFL) helper max_speed, and the energy_spectrum / integral_scale).\nPropagator: the interchangeable integrating-factor schemes under AbstractIntegrator.\nParticles: Lagrangian tracer tracking — second-order advection and field sampling along trajectories, with a selectable interpolation scheme.\nSimulationRun: the ergonomic run layer — assembles a Simulation and runs it with advance! / run! / warmup! (the CFL-adaptive loop with automatic warm-up to stationarity); it reports the measured flow state (flow_parameters) and compares the measured energy spectrum to a Phenomenology model (validate_spectrum).\nPhenomenology: the reference turbulence laws — model spectra, structure-function laws, and scaling constants — to validate a run against. A separate, opt-in group (not re-exported by the umbrella), designed to be extractable into a standalone package.","title":"Components"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Particles/#Particles","page":"Particles","text":"","title":"Particles"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Particles/#The-tracer-set","page":"Particles","text":"Tracer positions plus a selectable interpolation scheme, preallocated for allocation-free advection, with the accessors that read a tracer set's state — its positions, most-recent trajectory velocities, and count.","title":"The tracer set"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Particles/#The-tracking-loop","page":"Particles","text":"The steps of Lagrangian tracking: put the start-of-step velocity on record along the trajectories, advance the tracers with second-order Heun-in-time advection once the fields have moved, then read a physical field along their trajectories — all feeding the Lagrangian statistics. The push is two verbs because the run holds a single physical velocity field, so the start-of-step values are taken off it before the state advances.","title":"The tracking loop"},{"category":"section","location":"api/Particles/#Sample-buffers","page":"Particles","text":"Allocate the point-value out for sample! — a VectorPointValues for the velocity, a ScalarPointValues for the scalar.","title":"Sample buffers"},{"category":"module","location":"api/Particles/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles","page":"Particles","text":"Particles\n\nLagrangian tracer tracking:\n\nTracers — the tracer set (positions and a selectable interpolation scheme).\nrecord_trajectory_velocity! — put the start-of-step Lagrangian velocity on record.\nadvect! — the second-order tracer push (Heun-in-time on the start- and end-of-step velocity).\nsample! — read a field along the trajectories; velocity_buffer / scalar_buffer allocate its out buffers.\ntracer_positions, tracer_velocities, number_of_tracers — the trajectory readouts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles"},{"category":"type","location":"api/Particles/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles.Tracers","page":"Particles","text":"Tracers(domain, number_of_tracers; scheme, rng = default_rng())\nTracers(domain, positions; scheme)\n\nA set of Lagrangian tracers, built on domain's backend and element type. The first form seeds number_of_tracers tracers uniformly at random in the periodic box of domain; the second wraps a given (M, 3) array of M tracer positions (the three columns are the x y z components), converting it to domain's backend and element type. The scheme is the GBGPeriodicFields velocity-interpolation scheme used to evaluate the velocity (and any sampled field) at the tracer positions. CatmullRom is the C^1 choice (the Simulation front door's default tracer_scheme); LalescuSpline(n, m) and LagrangeSpline(n) offer higher smoothness or interpolation order — at a wider stencil, useful for derivative-sensitive readouts such as acceleration statistics — with n (and, for LalescuSpline, m) the stencil and smoothness parameters. The rng (first form only) is the random stream seeding the uniformly-random tracer positions; it defaults to Random.default_rng().\n\nRead the tracer state with tracer_positions, tracer_velocities, and number_of_tracers; the remaining fields are preallocated predictor and point-value scratch, so the tracer push allocates no per-step memory.\n\nFields\n\npositions — the (M, 3) tracer positions (read with tracer_positions).\npredictor — (M, 3) scratch for the Heun predictor.\nposition_points, predictor_points — PointSets wrapping positions / predictor for interpolation.\nvelocity1, velocity2 — the interpolated velocities at the start-of-step and predictor positions; velocity1 is the retained trajectory velocity (read with tracer_velocities).\nscheme — the velocity-interpolation scheme.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles.Tracers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Particles/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles.tracer_positions","page":"Particles","text":"tracer_positions(tracers)\n\nThe (M, 3) array of current tracer positions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles.tracer_positions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Particles/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles.tracer_velocities","page":"Particles","text":"tracer_velocities(tracers)\n\nThe (M, 3) array of the most recent trajectory velocities boldsymbolu(t_n boldsymbolX) (set by advect!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles.tracer_velocities"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Particles/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles.number_of_tracers","page":"Particles","text":"number_of_tracers(tracers)\n\nThe number of tracers M.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles.number_of_tracers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Particles/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles.record_trajectory_velocity!","page":"Particles","text":"record_trajectory_velocity!(tracers, u) -> tracers\n\nRecord the Lagrangian velocity boldsymbolu(t_n boldsymbolX) — the flow velocity seen by each tracer where it currently sits — by interpolating the physical velocity field u boldsymbolu onto the tracer positions with the set's interpolation scheme.\n\nCall it on the start-of-step velocity, before the field solver advances the state; advect! then completes the push from the recorded values and the end-of-step field. The push is split across these two verbs because a run holds only one physical velocity field: the observation cache recomputes that chart in place once the state has advanced, so the start-of-step values must be taken off it while it still holds boldsymbolu(t_n). Recording them costs M times 3 values for M tracers, where keeping the field itself would cost N^3 times 3.\n\nWhat is recorded is also the trajectory velocity read back by tracer_velocities, and the quantity the Lagrangian statistics accumulate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles.record_trajectory_velocity!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Particles/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles.advect!","page":"Particles","text":"advect!(tracers, velocity_end, domain, dt) -> tracers\n\nAdvance the tracer positions by one step of size dt, in place, by Heun-in-time on the velocity at the two ends of the step — the start-of-step values that record_trajectory_velocity! put on record, and the end-of-step physical velocity field velocity_end = boldsymbolu(t_n+1):\n\nbeginaligned\nboldsymbolv_1 = boldsymbolu(t_n boldsymbolX) \nboldsymbolX^* = boldsymbolX + Delta t boldsymbolv_1 \nboldsymbolv_2 = boldsymbolu(t_n+1 boldsymbolX^*) \nboldsymbolX leftarrow boldsymbolX + tfracDelta t2(boldsymbolv_1 + boldsymbolv_2)\nendaligned\n\nwith positions wrapped into 0 L), where the box side L is read from domain (a FixedDomain). This is second-order accurate in time — advecting on the frozen start-of-step velocity alone would be first order.\n\nnote: Note\nboldsymbolv_1 is read from the tracer set, not recomputed here, so record_trajectory_velocity! must already have run against boldsymbolu(t_n) for this step. Calling advect! twice without re-recording pushes the second step off a stale boldsymbolv_1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles.advect!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Particles/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles.sample!","page":"Particles","text":"sample!(out, field, tracers) -> out\n\nSample the physical field at the current tracer positions into the point-value buffer out (a VectorPointValues for the velocity, a ScalarPointValues for the scalar varphi), using the tracers' interpolation scheme. Used to gather a field along the trajectories for the forthcoming Lagrangian statistics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles.sample!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Particles/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles.velocity_buffer","page":"Particles","text":"velocity_buffer(tracers)\n\nAllocate a VectorPointValues of shape (M, 3) matching the tracers, for use as the out of sample! when sampling the velocity along the trajectories.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles.velocity_buffer"},{"category":"function","location":"api/Particles/#GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles.scalar_buffer","page":"Particles","text":"scalar_buffer(tracers)\n\nAllocate a ScalarPointValues of length M matching the tracers, for use as the out of sample! when sampling the scalar varphi along the trajectories.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GBGIsotropicTurbulence.Particles.scalar_buffer"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Notation","page":"Notation","text":"The symbols used throughout the docstrings and the design documents, with the code object each one denotes. All mathematics is rendered LaTeX; a code identifier always appears in backticks and never inside a math span. This page is the single reference — a docstring introduces a symbol on first use and need not repeat the convention.\n\nThe physical conventions are standard turbulence usage — see Pope (2000), Turbulent Flows; the field and spectral conventions follow GBGPeriodicFields, a direct dependency.","title":"Notation"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Conventions","page":"Notation","text":"Concept Symbol Convention\nImaginary unit mathrm i Upright, always — never bare italic i, never imath. Writing it upright frees i for use as an index.\nIndices i j a dots Italic, taken in order. A repeated index within a term is summed over the three components 123 (Einstein convention).\nSpatial average langle cdot rangle The box average frac1lvertOmegarvertint_Omega(cdot)mathrm dboldsymbolx over the periodic domain Omega — the homogeneous ensemble average.\nSpectral representation hat f The field f in spectral (Fourier) representation, to_spectral(f). A field is one physical object; physical and spectral are two representations of it.\nWavenumber clash boldsymbolkappa / kappa / k Kept distinct: bold boldsymbolkappa is the wavenumber vector, kappa = lvertboldsymbolkapparvert its magnitude (the spectrum coordinate), and italic scalar k is the turbulent kinetic energy. The scalar diffusivity varkappa (Parameters) is a fourth, deliberately distinct glyph.","title":"Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Physical-variables","page":"Notation","text":"Symbol Meaning Code\nboldsymbolu the incompressible velocity field, nablacdotboldsymbolu = 0 the velocity state\nboldsymbolomega the vorticity nablatimesboldsymbolu vorticity_to_physical!\nvarphi the passive scalar (never theta) the scalar state\np the pressure (absorbed by the Leray–Helmholtz projection, never stored) —\nboldsymbolf a body forcing on the momentum equation AbstractForcing\nf_varphi a body forcing on the passive-scalar equation AbstractForcing (via scalar_forcing)\nboldsymbolx, t position in the box Omega; time —","title":"Physical variables"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Parameters","page":"Notation","text":"Symbol Meaning Code\nnu kinematic viscosity (sets the Reynolds number) conditions.viscosity\nmathrmSc Schmidt number conditions.schmidt\nvarkappa scalar diffusivity numathrmSc ν / schmidt\nmathrmRe, mathrmRe_lambda Reynolds number; Taylor-microscale Reynolds number 1 / ν (mathrmRe_lambda measured)\n\n(The scalar diffusivity varkappa is a deliberately distinct glyph from the wavenumber magnitude kappa and the turbulent kinetic energy k.)","title":"Parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Spectral-representation-and-operators","page":"Notation","text":"Symbol Meaning Code\nboldsymbolkappa wavenumber vector, components kappa_a FieldSpectralMultipliers\nkappa wavenumber magnitude lvertboldsymbolkapparvert (spectrum coordinate) —\nlvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2 squared wavenumber; in spectral space -nabla^2 acts as multiplication by it domain.squared_wavenumber\nmathcal F, mathcal F^-1 forward / inverse real transform to_spectral, to_physical\nnablatimes, nablacdot curl, divergence curl, divergence\nmathbb P the Leray–Helmholtz projection onto divergence-free fields helmholtz_projection\nmathcal E(boldsymbolkappa) the diffusive integrating factor expbig((A(boldsymbolkappa)-Dlvertboldsymbolkapparvert^2)Delta tbig) (D the diffusivity — nu for the velocity, varkappa for the scalar; A(boldsymbolkappa) the linear forcing rate) velocity_factor!, scalar_factor!\nstar periodic convolution (the dealias/band/envelope kernels) convolve","title":"Spectral representation and operators"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Diagnostics","page":"Notation","text":"Symbol Meaning Code\nk turbulent kinetic energy tfrac12langlelvertboldsymbolurvert^2rangle kinetic_energy\nvarepsilon mean dissipation rate nulanglelvertboldsymbolomegarvert^2rangle dissipation\nE(kappa) the energy spectrum, in two readings related by the shell width Deltakappa = 2piL: as measured, the energy in each wavenumber-magnitude shell (k = sum_kappa E(kappa)); as a Phenomenology model, the spectral density per unit wavenumber (k = int_0^infty E(kappa)mathrm dkappa) energy_spectrum (measured; the GBGPeriodicFields radial spectrum, halved) / spectrum (model density)\nlanglevarphi^2rangle scalar variance scalar_variance\nvarepsilon_varphi scalar dissipation rate 2varkappalanglelvertnablavarphirvert^2rangle scalar_dissipation","title":"Diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Derived-scales","page":"Notation","text":"Measured from the resolved state, or derived from it through a FlowParameters bundle and its helpers.\n\nSymbol Meaning Code\nu root-mean-square velocity (one component), u = sqrt2k3 flow_parameters\nL_11 longitudinal (parallel) integral length scale, tfrac3pi4int_0^infty (Ekappa)mathrm dkappa  int_0^infty Emathrm dkappa (the lateral is L_22 = L_112) integral_scale\neta Kolmogorov (dissipation) microscale, (nu^3varepsilon)^14 kolmogorov_length\ntau_eta Kolmogorov time, (nuvarepsilon)^12 kolmogorov_time\nlambda Taylor microscale, sqrt15 nu u^2varepsilon taylor_microscale","title":"Derived scales"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Numerics","page":"Notation","text":"Symbol Meaning Code\nN grid points per side grid_points_per_axis\nL box side (Omega = 0L)^3) side_length\nDelta t timestep dt\nC advective-CFL ceiling, Delta t = C Delta xmax_ilvert u_irvert recommended_cfl\nkappa_max dealiased maximum wavenumber, tfrac23cdottfrac N2cdottfrac2piL for the default 23 rule (tfracsqrt23Ncdottfrac2piL for PhaseShift; set by the dealiasing strategy) DealiasingStrategy\nA_i B_i d_i the low-storage Runge–Kutta stage coefficients and node increments IFLowStorageRK3\nT the real element type (Float32 / Float64) element_type","title":"Numerics"},{"category":"section","location":"notation/#Forcing","page":"Notation","text":"Symbol Meaning Code\nA(boldsymbolkappa) per-mode linear forcing rate, folded into mathcal E set_forcing_rate!\nvarepsilon_texttarget target energy-injection rate ConstantPower(target=…)","title":"Forcing"}]
